(265, 225, 1609, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-04-11', 'ng elect. Good time to schedule. Had one done quite similar on air compressors. BPA. CHemical manufacturer. Paper whitener for newsprint. Byproduct zinc oxide sold too. Water caustic soda ash. Pumps fairly small. Big motors on exhust fan, river water pump, two large chillers. NG for fluidized bed dryer. (after press).  ', 43, NULL, NULL),
(266, 225, 336, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-11', 'Calling again per request from Kevin Wooster. Out for vacation next week starting tomorrow. Will send you letter offering an assessment (to your email). I am assuming you are eligible (over 100,000k a year in energy cost.)', 42, NULL, NULL),
(267, 225, 1154, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-04-11', 'Extremely interested in energy evaluation. Whole engineering staff looking at all next week. Electrical looking at blowers. Spend well over what other Woodgrain facilities spend. Grew and hired over 50 peoples. Blowers air big HP, quite. Two pellet machines since last audit. 400HP pellet mill. A lot of projects going on. But can prioritize for you. The sooner the better. Prineville facility such a large footprint. Big opportunities. ', 42, NULL, NULL),
(268, 332, 1591, 1, 1, 10412, '2013-04-12', 'Asked for clarification regarding availability of excess bark to run possible biomass dryer, as well as clarification regarding availability of sawdust from sawmill to run through larger dryer.', NULL, 731, NULL),
(269, 225, 1154, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-11', 'Hi Ben,\n\nI was glad to speak to you today and would be glad to bring a team visit your facility and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return to get get things going are the:\nPre-qualifying data and\nUtility Release (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they are helpful and can improve the job we are able to do for you. Please call me if you have any questions.\n\nYou said you would like to get us in as soon as possible. Would any of the following dates work?\nFriday, May 3\nFriday, May 10\nWednesday, May 15\n(Please include the \"OSU IAC Manage List\" <osuiacmanage@engr.orst.edu> on your reply as I will be out next week)\n\nThanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n', 42, NULL, NULL),
(270, 225, 1567, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-11', 'Rich,\n\nWould one of the following days work for you as a date to re-schedule a visit?\nWed, April 24\nWed, May 1\nWed, May 8\n- Thanks Joe', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(271, 225, 1599, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-15', 'Hi Jon,\n\nI was glad to talk to you last week. I\'ll be glad to field a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return to get thing rolling are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (this includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nOnce we get these two items we can start the process of analyzing your current energy use.\n\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they are helpful and can improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nAs we agreed on the phone, we are targeting a visit for the last week in June. I look forward to hearing from you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.', 38, NULL, NULL),
(272, 225, 1608, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-15', 'Hi Tony,\n\nI was glad to talk to you last week. I\'ll be pleased to field a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return to get things rolling are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nOnce we get these two items we can start the process of preparing and analyzing your current energy use.\n\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they can improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nAs I mentioned on the phone, I may be able to fit in a visit to your site this Spring, but if so, I am not sure of the potential date(s) yet. Of course the goal will be to select a date that works for you and allows us to line up and a solid team of analysts. If we can\'t find a spring date we certainly can schedule a visit for this summer. We should be able to sort that out when I get back into the office next week. \n\nI look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(273, 225, 1609, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-15', 'Hi Steve,\n\nI was glad to talk to you last week. I\'d be pleased to field a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return to get things rolling are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nOnce we get these two items we can start the process of preparing and analyzing your current energy use.\n\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they can improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nAs I mentioned on the phone, I may be able to fit in a visit to your site this Spring, but if so, I am not sure of the potential date(s) yet. Of course the goal will be to select a date that works for you and allows us to line up and a solid team of analysts. If we can\'t find a spring date we certainly can schedule a visit for this summer. We should be able to sort that out when I get back into the office next week. \n\nI look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n', 43, NULL, NULL),
(274, 225, 1605, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-15', 'Hi Gene,\n\nThanks for your interest!  I\'d be pleased to field a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment. \n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return to get things rolling are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nOnce we get these two items we can start the process of preparing and analyzing your current energy use.\n\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they can improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nAs we agreed on the phone, we are targeting a visit to your facility in the last week of June. We can select the day of the week that works for you (I understand it would be best to avoid Wednesdays) and that allows us to line up and a solid team of analysts. \n\nSorry for the delay in getting this to you. I have been in \"triage mode\" for the last couple of overly full weeks.\n\nI look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.', 39, NULL, NULL),
(275, 225, 1615, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-03-25', 'Email From Scott\nHi Joe,\nI was contacted by Doug Swier at Cowlitz PUD about you Energy Assessment Program. I would be interested in having you come to our facility for an energy audit. Please contact me at your convenience.\nThank you,\nScott Spencer\n ', 47, NULL, NULL),
(276, 225, 1616, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-03-13', 'Email from Laurie\nHi Andreas\nI have filled out the paperwork to begin our industrial assessment.\nWe are available any time after March 25th.\nThank you\nLaurie Pierce\n ', 48, NULL, NULL),
(277, 332, 1617, 2, 1, 10340, '2013-04-18', 'Spoke with representative from STAK properties regarding bio mass dryer solution. Got specific numbers and pricing.', NULL, 731, NULL),
(278, 315, 1564, 1, 1, 10252, '2013-04-19', 'emailed Chris to get a status update on assessment paperwork and see if he has had a chance to review my process description.', NULL, 716, NULL),
(279, 325, 1618, 1, 1, 10419, '2013-04-22', '', NULL, 733, NULL),
(280, 325, 1618, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-04-12', 'Called and established April 29 as assessment day.  he is expecting a preaudit phone call on the week of the 22nd.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(281, 225, 1619, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-18', 'Em from them: Am reaching out to you regarding the Industrial Assessment program at OSU.\nThe Coca-Cola Company facility located in Portland is interested in learning more about this program and the possibility of partnering in a project to support its Sustainability / PLANET initiatives. \n \nThank you for any preliminary information you can provide, as well as how best to proceed from here.  I look forward to your response!\n \nRegards,\n \nAnn Macdonald\nCoca-Cola Refreshments\nRegion Environmental Manager â€“ NW\nC:  510-364-4832\namacdonald@coca-cola.com\n ', 49, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `ContactLog` (`ContactLogId`, `EmployeeId`, `PersonId`, `ContactTypeId`, `GrantId`, `TaskId`, `Date`, `Notes`, `LeadId`, `ProjectId`, `LocationId`) VALUES
(282, 225, 1619, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-22', 'Hi Ann,\n\nThanks for your interest in the Industrial Assessment Center. I\'d be glad to talk to you about partnering in a project to support the Coca-Cola company\'s Sustainability / PLANET initiatives. Are you interested in having us visit one or a number of your sites for an assessment, or do you have some other efforts in mind? \n\nIf  it is assessments, U.S.DOE funding allows us to offer no-cost full scope energy and efficiency assessments to eligible industry. The key eligibility criteria is that the assessed site has energy costs over $100,000 a year and preferably over $200,000 a year.  The center has provided over 600 of these to Northwest industries since 1986 and identified over $129 Million in cost savings and productivity opportunities. \n \nThe assessment process entails a one day visit to the facility after collecting preliminary information. The team of analysts will tour the site, brainstorm on potential opportunities and devote the remainder of the day to collecting data that supports an analysis. Within a nominal 60 days, a report is delivered with specific recommendations to reduce energy and waste streams costs and increase productivity and profitability. The OSU EEC/IAC has a full compliment of state of the art equipment to bring to bear on these assessments including combustion analyzers, thermal imaging cameras, a non-contact ultrasonic flow meter, specialized refrigeration system analysis tools, power quality analyzers, and a broad selection of data loggers. If you have any systems that might benefit from the evaluation these allow, the center can help. \n\nYou can find out more about us at:  http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu/\n  \nIs there a good time for me to call you to fill you in more and answer any questions you might have?\n\nMuch appreciated \n\n- Joe Junker\n\n(Sorry for the delay in reply - I was on a much needed break last week!)\n', 49, NULL, NULL),
(283, 225, 1567, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-04-23', 'Either May 1 or 8 will work. Will let me know tomorrow.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(284, 225, 1607, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-04-23', '. Would it work for you to have us visit in the second half of May? If so, let me know if one of the following potential dates can work for you (I think earlier is better for us):\n\nMonday, May 20\nWednesday, May 22\nFriday, May 24\n\nMonday, May 27\nWednesday, May 29\nFriday, May 31\n', 43, NULL, NULL),
(285, 331, 1620, 2, 1, 10302, '2013-04-20', 'Didn\'t answer. ', NULL, 716, NULL),
(286, 331, 1621, 2, 1, 10302, '2013-04-26', 'Called for an RTO estimate and talked to Scott who gave me his email address. I then emailed him with the information he wanted for the RTO.', NULL, 716, NULL),
(287, 310, 1618, 2, 1, 10423, '2013-04-25', 'Pre-assessment phone walk-through call with Joe J.', NULL, 733, NULL),
(288, 310, 1618, 1, 1, 10423, '2013-04-29', 'Emailed James about Plant 2 Audit date and asked about picking up data loggers next Tuesday morning.', NULL, 733, NULL),
(289, 225, 1606, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-01', '(Sent pre-audit) Thanks Renee, I am still working on my summer schedule. We will continue to work towards a July or August visit. - Joe', 41, NULL, NULL),
(290, 225, 1154, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-01', 'Thanks Benjamin,\n\nWe will plan to schedule you in for a day in the week of June 24th then. Are there any days of the week we should avoid and days that are particularly better than others?\n\nI\'ve attached a letter that outlines our assessment process - I think it will provide a good general outline to help you best prepare. It also outlines some information and what we need to collect to prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return so we can get started preparing are the:\nPre-qualifying data and\nUtility Release (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they are helpful and can improve the job we are able to do for you. Please call me if you have any questions.\n\nMuch appreciated - Joe Junker\n ', 42, NULL, NULL),
(291, 316, 1594, 2, 1, 10381, '2013-04-19', 'Talked to randy about their excess flared gas use and implementation costs. Conclusion was that Hurley Engineering was coming to look at the boilers to see if a VFD could be installed as well as the cost. He also informed by for a number of reasons why they have to flare some of their gas and can not use it more for their boilers. ', NULL, 729, NULL),
(292, 316, 1594, 2, 1, 10381, '2013-04-25', 'Talked to randy for follow up from last phone call. I asked him about using their flared gas because they have so much in excess and he said that some of it could maybe be used but due to the complications of the digester and how much is produced an analysis would be too far in depth. Also discussed where exactly the cost savings could be from and he confirmed that digester gas savings by tuning the boiler directly correlates to cost savings on natural gas. As far as implementation costs go he was going to email me the results of that. ', NULL, 729, NULL),
(293, 316, 1594, 1, 1, 10381, '2013-04-28', 'Randy emailed me the implementation costs for the current boilers as well that for installing new more efficient boilers. He later emailed me for insulation data. information sumarrized in OMC\'\ns SIte DAta and A.R\'s', NULL, 729, NULL),
(294, 310, 1558, 1, 1, 10228, '2013-05-02', 'Sent report out to client review.  YAY!', NULL, 715, NULL),
(295, 225, 1622, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-02', 'Bill called after getting email from Josh Ruddick at EWEB. They make granola and cereal. Multiple ovens for corn flakes and granola. Located on Prairie road just North of Beltline Road. Targeting Summer or Fall visit.  24-7 Operation. 2-300 Employees.', 52, NULL, NULL),
(296, 225, 1622, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-02', 'Sent Preaudit doc. \n\nHi Bill,\n\nI was glad to get your call today. I\'d be pleased to field a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to best prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return so we can get things started are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes eligibility and logistic information)\nUtility Release (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they are helpful and can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nPlease call me if you have any questions. I am currently anticipating that we will schedule a visit to your site this summer or next fall. I look forward to hearing from you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n\n(You might also want to visit our web-site to find out more about our program: http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu/)\n', 52, NULL, NULL),
(297, 225, 436, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-02', 'Calling per a Clark PUD (Larry and Sam) suggestion - set up an assessment. Its been since 96 since we were there.', 50, NULL, NULL),
(298, 225, 1619, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-02', 'Hi Ann,\n\nI was glad to catch you by phone today. I\'d be pleased to field teams to visit your facilities in Portland, OR; Wilsonville, OR; and Bellevue, WA to perform assessments. It sounds like they would all be eligible.\n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to prepare. The critical things to return to get things rolling are the following for each plant:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nOnce we get these items we can start the process of preparing and analyzing your current energy use.\n\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nAs I mentioned, we can make it to the Portland and Wilsonville sites throughout the year. We can serve the Bellevue plants during breaks (typically summer) and would usually be working to identify a couple of other facilities to serve on the same trip (to leverage travel cost). I am currently talking to a facility in Mt Vernon, WA  and your Bellevue plant could possibly be included in that trip this summer. \n\nI look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n\n(You might also want to visit our web-site to find out more about our program: http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu/)\n\n', 49, NULL, NULL),
(299, 311, 1623, 2, 1, 9932, '2013-05-03', 'Contacted Rick regarding an assessment. Recently had an energy audit performed by an independent contractor. It sounded like they had a very specific project in mind. Gave me the lead on Pacific Foods contact Doug Kutella', NULL, 709, NULL),
(300, 225, 1625, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-06', 'LVM - following up on suggestion from Andreas Winardi', 53, NULL, NULL),
(301, 225, 1625, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-06', 'Hi John,\n\nI just left you voice mail. I was following up on a suggestion by Andreas Winardi at Puget Sound Energy regarding the possibility of setting up a no-cost full scope energy and efficiency assessment of your facility  We have provided over 600 of these to Northwest industries since 1986 and identified over $129 Million in cost savings and productivity opportunities. \n \nThe assessment process entails a one day facility visit after collecting preliminary information. The team of analysts tours the site, brainstorms on potential opportunities and devotes the remainder of the day to collecting data that supports an analysis. Within a typical 60 days, a report is delivered with specific recommendations to reduce energy and waste streams costs and increase productivity and profitability. We have full compliment of state of the art equipment to bring to bear on these assessments including combustion analyzers, thermal imaging cameras, a non-contact ultrasonic flow meter, specialized refrigeration system analysis tools, power quality analyzers, and a broad selection of data loggers. If you have any systems that might benefit from the evaluation these allow, the center can help. \n \nWe will be visiting two other plants in you area during the week of June 24th, so it would be an opportune time to serve you. You can give me a call at the number below, or I can follow up to find out if you have an interest. \n\nThanks - Joe Junker', 53, NULL, NULL),
(302, 225, 436, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-06', 'Trying to catch you again. Apologies not sooner in the day. ', 50, NULL, NULL),
(303, 310, 1618, 1, 1, 10423, '2013-05-06', 'Sent Trip Report and requested more data for analysts.', NULL, 733, NULL),
(304, 310, 1618, 1, 1, 10436, '2013-05-06', 'Emailed about time of audit this Friday and asked about lunches.', NULL, 734, NULL),
(305, 310, 1618, 1, 1, 10423, '2013-05-07', 'Emailed James the PDFs of the old reports we\'ve done for OFD before, OR0004 and OR0070.', NULL, 733, NULL),
(306, 225, 436, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-07', 'Busy in the summer. Fall would be a good time for an audit. Send him the audit letter. We can work towards a fall audit. ', 50, NULL, NULL),
(307, 225, 1625, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-07', 'From: John Pokorney - Would very much like to schedule an audit when you are here in the area. Lets close the loop. 253 863 0373  x108 - Look forward to talking.\n\n>> I called back and LVM : \"will email audit letter/offer but lets close the loop\".', 53, NULL, NULL),
(308, 225, 436, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-08', 'Hi Jack,\n\nI was glad to catch you by phone yesterday. I look forward to fielding a team to visit your facility and perform an assessment. It sounds like you will be too busy this summer so lets target a fall date for our visit. \n\nI\'ve attached the letter I mentioned when we spoke. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to best prepare for an assessment. A lot should be familiar from our previous work with you. The critical things to return so we can get things started are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes eligibility and logistic information)\nUtility Release (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While not required, they a can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you. Please call me if you have any questions.  I look forward to hearing from you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.', 50, NULL, NULL),
(309, 225, 1625, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-08', 'Hi John,\n\nI was glad to get your message that you would like to have us include your facility when we are in the area for the the week of June 24th. I left you voice mail in return. I will be glad to start getting things set up. We should still try to speak but in the mean time I\'ve attached the letter I mentioned. It outlines our assessment process and what we need to collect to best prepare for an assessment. The critical things to return so we can get things started are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (that includes eligibility and logistic information)\nUtility Release (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While they are not required, they are helpful and can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you.\n\nBy the way, we are trying to load the assessments on the front end of the week. Are there any particularly worse and better days of the week to plan to visit you?\n\nI look forward to speaking with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n', 53, NULL, NULL),
(310, 310, 1558, 1, 1, 10228, '2013-05-10', 'Emailed Kurt about the report and review again.  If haven\'t heard back by 5/15 report will be sent to printing/mailing.', NULL, 715, NULL),
(311, 225, 1567, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-13', 'Hi Rich,\n\nWe\'d like to lock in Wednesday, June 26th to visit your site for the audit. The other facility we are visiting would like us to come on Thursday the 27th so that would work well for us. Let me know if that will not work. \n\nThanks - Joe\n', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(312, 310, 1558, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-13', 'Received email from Kurt about client review, going to flesh out heat pump opportunity and send report back.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(313, 225, 1625, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-13', 'Just trying to close the loop. What day works. 3 plants. Hoping for Mon, Tue, Wed. One says no to Wed. All the same for you?', 53, NULL, NULL),
(314, 225, 1599, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-13', 'Ed,\n\nI sent this to Jon Febus and he let me know we should consider you as our primary contact in setting this up.  One key question: we are working with 3 plants and hoping to set site visits for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday. One plant says Monday and Tuesday can work for them (not Wed). Are all the same for you?\n\nWe are also looking for some of the pre-audit preparatory information as outlined below in the email I sent to Jon last week. Just in case you do not have it handy, I am attaching the original letter we sent (with the noted forms included).\n\nLooking forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n- Joe Junker\n\n\n\n\nOn Wed, May 8, 2013 at 1:09 PM, Joseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com> wrote:\nHi Jon,\n\nI thought I would touch base again. We are looking forward to visiting your facility in the week of June 24th. We have been watching for the following from you so we can start getting ready:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form (this includes pre-qualifying data) and\nUtility Release Permission (so we can collect a one year month-to-month profile of your energy use and utility expenses)\n(Hopefully I have not missed your sending these already.)\n\nI also wanted to get a sense from you regarding the best day of the week to plan on visiting your site. We are hoping to schedule all in the first part of the week. Are all days equal for you or are some days better than others?\n\nThanks - Joe Junker\n', 38, NULL, NULL),
(315, 225, 1608, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-13', 'Thanks for all the preparatory information Tony. No need for apologies. I will get the team started on pre-analysis. \n\nMeanwhile we should work on the specific date for a visit that will work for you best. I am currently thinking it might work to schedule you in July. That would allow us to stop to assess your plant before we continue north to an assessment in the Seattle area. However if that can not work, you are close enough to Corvallis that we could visit on a one day round trip and fit you in almost anywhere in our summer schedule. \n\n- Joe', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(316, 325, 1628, 2, 1, 10205, '2013-05-14', 'Discussed motor recommendations and contact with center.', NULL, 714, NULL),
(317, 315, 1629, 2, 1, 10421, '2013-05-14', 'requested VSD retrofit price quote', NULL, 716, NULL),
(318, 225, 1598, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-14', 'Hi Joe\nTuesday or Wednesday would work best.  Thanks\n \nEd Draper\nSonoco - Sumner, WA\nOffice:  253-863-6366x334\nMobile:  253-320-5662\ned.draper@sonoco.com\n \n \nFrom: Joseph F. Junker [mailto:joseph.f.junker@gmail.com] \nSent: Tuesday, May 14, 2013 2:26 PM\nTo: Ed Draper\n\n\nSubject: Re: Subject: Assessment offer: Sonoco Sumner, WA\n \nThanks Ed, Let me know what you find out. - Joe\n==========\n\nOn Tue, May 14, 2013 at 8:18 AM, Ed Draper <ed.draper@sonoco.com> wrote:\nGood Morning Joe â€“\n\nI got your message this morning.  I will check with the other people that will help with this survey at our morning production meeting.  I will let you know today, what day works the best.  Thanks\n \n\n \nFrom: Joseph F. Junker [mailto:joseph.f.junker@gmail.com] \nSent: Monday, May 13, 2013 4:14 PM\nTo: Ed Draper\n\n\nEd,\n \nI sent this to Jon Febus and he let me know we should consider you as our primary contact in setting this up.  One key question: we are working with 3 plants and hoping to set site visits for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday. One plant says Monday and Tuesday can work for them (not Wed). Are all the same for you?\n \nWe are also looking for some of the pre-audit preparatory information as outlined below in the email I sent to Jon last week. Just in case you do not have it handy, I am attaching the original letter we sent (with the noted forms included).\n \nLooking forward to working with you. Thanks!\n \n- Joe Junker\n ', 38, NULL, NULL),
(319, 225, 1625, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-15', 'Any day mon, tue , wed will work. Told him I would email him soon with the best day for us then. They only producd macdonalds buns. BP: They have a vsd exhaust on their ovens instead of a barometric damper. I neglected to ask what parameter they control it on. ', 53, NULL, NULL),
(320, 225, 1607, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-20', 'OK to bring Climacheck along says Gloria.', 43, NULL, NULL),
(321, 320, 1618, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-05-20', 'Called James to ask questions about Forward Osmosis AR and plan a time to pick up the data loggers.  James will be on campus on 5/22 and will meet up with Michael in order to pass off the data loggers.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(322, 225, 1630, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-21', 'John Thornton did an energy model. He will send that sheet. Kendall is all about energy management and saving money. Big freezer. Two main meters plant IQF and freezer. Lighting studies. Lots of Cascade Energy studies. Late July early August at their busiest. Typically start hard in early July blueberries til mid September. after blueberries, some cranberries. Have extremes on both ends of technology - IQF old school. New freezer fully instrumented. Audit in July or after October 1.', 46, NULL, NULL),
(323, 225, 1615, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-22', 'Following up belatedly - was checking for permission from USDOE. Need to wait until next fiscal year: i.e. after October 1. Let me know if you are still interested. Again apologies for letting this slip off of my plate. \n- Joe', 47, NULL, NULL),
(324, 315, 1590, 1, 1, 10421, '2013-05-22', 'received VFD retrofit price quote from David', NULL, 716, NULL),
(325, 225, 1616, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-22', '(called first and followed up with an email)\n\nHi Laurie,\n\nI am following up on voice mail that I just left you. I owe you an apology for this late follow-up to you regarding scheduling an assessment of your site.  I had been trying to get permission from U.S.DOE to serve one more non-industrial site. They fund our program for industrial assessments and recently gave us leeway to serve two non industrial sites a year. I already had two wastewater plants in our schedule this year so I hoped they would allow one more. The answer was \"no\". I was contacting you to see if you might be interested in having us visit in our next fiscal year (after October 1).  If so, pleased do let me know,  I would be pleased to work with you.\n\nAgain, my apologies for the extended delay in my follow-up. \n\nThanks - Joe Junker \n', 48, NULL, NULL),
(326, 324, 1481, 1, 1, 10308, '2013-05-28', 'Asked Shawn for a heat exchanger quote.', NULL, 716, NULL),
(327, 225, 1608, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-05-30', 'OK to move the audit date to Monday June 24th.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(328, 315, 1564, 1, 1, 10252, '2013-06-03', 'sent an email to Chris to provide him with a status update of OR 604', NULL, 716, NULL),
(329, 315, 1590, 2, 1, 10298, '2013-06-05', 'called David to follow up on our earlier conversation and requested a price quote, which he said he could get to me on Friday', NULL, 716, NULL),
(330, 318, 1607, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-06-05', 'Email correspondence with Gloria about AR questions answers are: Question answers below. \n \nThe recommendation to replace the air diaphragm pump doesn\'t address the issues of repairing the other types.  This pump has proven to be more than efficient and reliable then the other sump pumps. \n \nSteve mentioned that you didn\'t mentioned re-using the exhaust from the air compressors â€“ is there a way or use we can get from that exhaust? \n \nYou mention reduce ZnO waste what about the zinc floor sweepings (warehouse) That is what I think you are referring to the zinc not the zinc oxide (ZnO) dryer room.\n \nInsulate the fluidizing tank would read better by calling it the fluidizing dryer   \n \nThanks\nGloria\n \nFrom: Mike Knapp [mailto:mi.w.knapp@gmail.com] \nSent: Tuesday, June 04, 2013 4:06 PM\nTo: Owen, Gloria\nSubject: OSU EEC Trip Report and Follow Up Questions\n \nHello Gloria, \n \nAttached is the trip report for the OSU EEC assessment that was conducted May 24th. \n \nMy team does have a couple questions concerning some of the recommendations we are looking into. \n \n1.      In order to better understand nitrogen use at the facility, how much nitrogen would you say is used for your annual production? For example if you produce 1,000 kg of material do you need 100 SCFs of Nitrogen?\n\nUntil last year we made the nitrogen using a generation unit.  We donâ€™t have any real records of usage before last year.  This would also be a difficult number to come up with as tank filling and usage would differ every week.  Nitrogen blankets all three storage tanks and depending on inventory levels â€“ usage would very too much?  Nitrogen is also loaded into trucks as they are loaded.  Some drivers leave the nitrogen on longer than others. Maybe isolating nitrogen usage on tanks when not in use or when we are down would be a better way to save money?   There is no way to know how much nitrogen is going to any area?       \n\n \n2.      When you are not using river water for process purposes does the river water pump remain on but at a lower operating load? If so, is this required or can the pump be shut-off when it is not needed for process purposes?\n\nYes it has a VFD that ramps the motor up when usage is needed and slows it when it is idling.  If we are not planning on running (12 hours) the river water pump is shut off.  This is hard on the equipment (starting and stopping) If we are between batches and planning on starting another batch we donâ€™t shut the pump off but it does idle down. \n\n \n3.      Clarification on the disposal of Hazardous Waste:\n\nIs the cost $600/barrel (5 gallons)? $6/pound? and $300 per trip?\n\nThe cost is one $300 pick-up fee and $300 per container fee (minimum) The pick-up fee is for ALL containers â€“ not just the 5 gallon drum.  So if we have three bins and 6 drums it would be one $300 pick-up then the per container minimum charge would be added. \n\n \n4.      Is 2200 the proper mesh size for a cleaning filter for the zinc and zinc oxide powders?\n\nNo requirements it needs to be small enough to allow the zinc and zinc oxide to flow and hold the debris that would clog the strainers on the pump. \n\n \n5.      Is the warehouse cooled with AC during the summer? \n\nNo we open the doors and allow the breeze to cool the warehouse.  The offices and lunch room have air conditioning.  Also the lab. \n\n \n6.      How are the radiative heater controlled? With a temperature or timing setpoint? or manually turned off or on?\n\nThe warehouse heaters have a thermostat (there are two) one in the front of the warehouse and one by the lab both systems run independent of the other.  When it is really cold out I don\'t think the heaters ever reach a temperature to shut off? \n ', 43, NULL, NULL),
