(350, 325, 1625, 2, 1, 10548, '2013-06-20', 'Phonecall with John clarifying some details about the facility such as refrigeration time, bills and boiler specs.', 53, 740, NULL),
(351, 310, 1618, 1, 1, 10427, '2013-06-25', 'Hi James,\n\nWe are in the process of wrapping up the report for Plant 1 and are getting close to finished with Plant 2\'s report.  I hope to have Plant 1\'s report PDF to you within the week.  I have listed a few more facility related questions to finish up the smaller details on these reports below.\nWhat are the annual operating hours for Plant 1?  For Plant 2?\nHow many employees work at Plant 1?  How many work at Plant 2?\nWhat are the facility areas for Plants 1 and 2?  The facility area for Plant 2 can include Plant 3 and the Administration Building as well.\nWhat are the annual sales for Plant 1 and Plant 2?  If you have those numbers separate that would be best.\nWhat are the annual production volumes for Plant 1 and Plant 2?\nSince we have split the energy assessments into two reports for Plants 1 and 2 I also need this data (which you mostly provided on the pre-assessment form) to be split between the plants.  If you have any comments or concerns about these questions please let me know.\n\nThank you,', NULL, 733, NULL),
(352, 0, 1569, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-06-25', 'Hi Joe,\n \nI visited Consolidate Pine (moldings) in Prineville yesterday.  Annual electrical costs are about $200K.  Very little done in the last 10+ years, I saw a lot of opportunity (lighting, some VFD, process heating, space heating, etc.).   The company is struggling but willing to make investments in good paybacks.  Would you be interested in this and what time frame?\n \nThanks,\nMark\n ', 9, NULL, NULL),
(353, 225, 1644, 1, 1, NULL, '2013-06-25', 'Hello Darren,\n\nAndreas Windardi at Puget Sound Energy suggested that I contact you regarding a no-cost energy and efficiency assessment of Janicki Industries. We are setting up a trip to your area to visit another of his industrial clients in August and he thought it could be a good chance to serve your facility as well. \n\nOur program, the Oregon State University Energy Efficiency Center and Industrial Assessment Center (OSU EEC/IAC) offers no-cost full scope energy and efficiency assessments to industry. We have provided over 600 of these to Northwest industries since 1986 and identified over $129 Million in cost savings and productivity opportunities. \n \nThe assessment process entails a one day visit to your facility after collecting preliminary information. The analyst team tours your site, brainstorms on potential opportunities and devotes the remainder of the day to collecting data that supports an analysis. Within a typical 60 days, a report is delivered with specific recommendations to reduce energy and waste streams costs and increase productivity and profitability. \n \nThe OSU EEC has a full compliment of state of the art equipment to bring to bear on these assessments including combustion analyzers, thermal imaging cameras, a non-contact ultrasonic flow meter, specialized refrigeration system analysis tools, power quality analyzers, and a broad selection of data loggers. If you have any systems that might benefit from the evaluation these allow, the center can help. \n \nIf this sound interesting to you let me know. I would be glad to fill you in more.  You can also find out more about the OSU EEC at: http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu\n\nThanks\n\n- Joe Junker\n', 58, NULL, NULL),
(354, 318, 1607, 2, 1, 10528, '2013-06-25', 'Talked with Gloria and Steve about their infrared natural gas heaters. The heaters are Roberts Gordon, Inc. Vantage II and model CTH2-60. The firing rate is 60,000 Btu/hr and they have 6 of them. Operating time is about 6 months annually.', 43, 735, NULL),
(355, 225, 1644, 2, 1, NULL, '2013-06-26', 'Mfg composites aerospace, green energy sector, marine energy technology. Big focus, measurement tool to sort out where energy goes. Big energy users, cncs milling, pneumatic controls, weld and machine shop  dust control, bag-house.  Composites need to be cured in ovens, propane burners 175 to 300 deg, a lot of HVAC many air exchanges, have to maintain temperature, painting bays. Biggest obstacle is modeling energy use where.  150 sq ft facility. Developing database. Two sites close to each other. One each day. ', 58, NULL, NULL),
