(1808, 378, 2320, 2, 0, 14018, '2018-06-28', 'Called Charlie Parks after initializing contact via email the same day. Discussed energy savings opportunities at Eugene Wastewater. Was unable to acquire a quote due to vendor needing more information to come up with a reasonable figure.', NULL, 890, 609),
(1809, 378, 2303, 1, 0, 14018, '2018-06-29', 'Hi Peter:\n\nKristin passed your information to me to review. We can mix all four channels using less than 40 HP. The attached video is testing the velocity and flow redirection (baffle). The test basin is about the size of the channels shown on your drawings. The test does show that the  mixing plates can easily be 20 feet apart. We are an anoxic mixer it will not add appreciable DO, you will need to add diffusers if you require the channels to be aerobic.  Most of the channel mixing requirements want the channel to be anoxic.\n\n \n\nThe air line from the compressor to the controllers need to be SCH 40 SS there are options depending on the runs, which are more cost effective then SCH 40 SS. The airlines from the controller to the plates can be CPVC however SS lasts a lot longer. Rough cost would be 150K for the mixers and compressor plus the piping and installation.\n\n \n\nI have another option that I will send via a second e-mail and that would be using blower air to make the large mixing bubble.\n\nCheers\n\nKris Drewry\n\n(425) 785-3225', NULL, 890, 609),
(1810, 225, 2295, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-07-19', 'Jody must handle and will forward paperwork,\n\nTimeframe? Dont know - projects a big issue. Nasty solids in plant. Dewatering digester one and taking out of service. All equally good (bad;).  Target some time in August. \n\nWill get back to me regarding Paperwork from Jody. ', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1811, 381, 2077, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-20', 'Sent an email, called and left a message', NULL, NULL, 842),
(1812, 360, 2283, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-23', 'Morning Aaron, \n\nWe just had a couple more questions. I will need answers to these questions by Wednesday 7/25 to keep the report on schedule. If we don\'t hear back, we will just use the assumptions that are mentioned below. \n\nInstall VFD on Main Blowers \nWe recieved the datalogger back for the 200 HP blower, and we were wondering if the blower was operating normally during the data collection period (6/28 - 7/5)? \nIs the 200 HP blower a backup blower for the 350 HP blower? \nDoes the 200 HP blower typically run at 100% load, 7008 hours/yr? \nNotched V-Belts\nCould you indicate which motors that have a notched v-belt on the motor list? If that\'s too much, we will just recommend savings for the 200 HP, 350 HP and the Melamine blower.\nThanks!', NULL, NULL, 532),
(1813, 360, 2283, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-23', 'Aaron, \n\nI apologize for the number of questions, but I forgot to attach another question to the previous email.\n\nUtility Data\nOn the bill that you provided us, it only shows the transportation cost from NW Natural. Are you purchasing natural gas in commodity from someone else? If so, could you provide us a sample bill? If not, what would you estimate your natural gas cost to be (per therm or per MMBtu)? We\'re currently estimating $4.00/MMBtu. If this is a reasonable assumption, we will continue to use this for our analyses. \n\nThanks for all the help Aaron,\nMonica Heng', NULL, NULL, 532),
(1814, 360, 2283, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-06', 'Hi Aaron, \n\nI have attached a trip report which briefly summarizes our assessment and the opportunities that we have identified for recommendations. \n\nWe have performed initial estimates and we have some additional questions for you and your team. Please reply to these questions at your earliest convinience. \n\n\nGeneral Background Information\nMotor Inventory (if possible)\nCompressed Air Leaks \n75 HP compressor nameplate data \nWhat are the current controls of the air compressors? Are the 100 HP and 75 HP compressors both VFD controlled? \nCould we assume that the air compressor is operating at 70% load capacity? If not, would the capacity be higher or lower? \nInstall VFD on Cooling Tower Fan\nFan motor name plate from SF2 cooling tower\nDoes the cooling tower fan operate at full capacity during the summer? \nFor our initial estimate, we assumed that the average load on the fan could drop to approximately 80% year round. Does this sound like a reasonable assumption? \nCould we assume thate the average fan load could drop down to as low as 60% during winter months?\nReduce Urea Waste\nPrice per lb of urea and yearly consumption of urea\nMaximum weight capacity of the conveyor that feeds urea into kettles 3 and 7\nReduce RTO Gas Consumption\nCan you provide any more information on the composition of the total VOC emissions from sf3?  \nInstall VFD on Blowers\nWhat is the most efficient pressure in the methanol tanks?\nWhat is the current pressure of the methanol tanks?\nWhat is the behavior of the pressure release valve? (Setpoint, how often it opens, for how long is it open)\nPreheat Boiler Makeup Water\nHow often are the boilers running?\nWhat is the usage divide between the natural gas boiler and the boiler running off SF3 exhaust?\nBoiler nameplate data?\nNatural gas usage for boilers?\nStack temperature for boilers?\nCondensate not collected? Location of this condensate in facility?\nCurrently analysts are thinking of either installing an economizer on your boiler stack, or collecting condensate for return. Is there any other option for waste heat in your facility that you can think of?\nUtility Analysis\nCommodity charges for natural gas?\n\nThanks,', NULL, NULL, 532),
(1815, 367, 2321, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-07-20', 'Spoke with Todd and he is interested in having us out. Asked me to call back on Tuesday after he meets with management.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1816, 367, 2321, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-20', 'Sent Todd followup email with old report attached and explained some of our process', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1817, 367, 2321, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-07-24', 'LVM', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1818, 381, 2077, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-30', 'Called and left a message', NULL, NULL, 842),
(1819, 360, 2309, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-31', 'Hi Bryan, \n\nWe had a question about how the air compressors were operating during the data collection period. Was there scheduled down time on Thursday July 12? It looks like there was maybe 40 minutes of down time on Tuesday June 30, but for the rest of the data collection period it looks like normal production. Is this correct? \n\n\nThanks,', NULL, NULL, 532),
(1820, 225, 2323, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-01', 'Shooting for visit after October 1. Sending pre-audit letter.\n\nGhost loads when facility on holiday. Two meters - one on 1990 original building, one on 2002 Expansion. Ghost loads on the original meter.', NULL, NULL, 953),
(1821, 368, 2256, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-02', '[Follow Up] Called and left a voicemail and sent an email.', NULL, NULL, 921),
(1822, 368, 0, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-02', '[Follow up] David does not work there anymore. Michael will be handling the follow up from here. Ive spoken with him and sent him an email with the list of recommendations. He will dig up the report and get back to me soon.', NULL, NULL, 364),
(1825, 373, 2018, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-07', 'Email sent, talked to Dave on the phone, says he will call me back.', NULL, NULL, 861);
INSERT INTO `ContactLog` (`ContactLogId`, `EmployeeId`, `PersonId`, `ContactTypeId`, `GrantId`, `TaskId`, `Date`, `Notes`, `LeadId`, `ProjectId`, `LocationId`) VALUES
(1826, 373, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-07', 'Implementation - Emailed Steve Nelson and left a voicemail.', NULL, NULL, 882),
(1827, 360, 2311, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-01', 'Hello Andrew Jones,\n\n\nMy name is Monica Heng, an Operations Manager at the Energy Efficiency Center at OSU and I will be leading the assessment on August 15th, 2018.\n\n\nWe would like to schedule a 30 min. Conference call with you to familiarize ourselves with Lamb Weston. During this phone call, Dr. Brian Fronk, the Principal Auditor of the assessment, and myself will introduce the program to you, detail the logistics of the day, and respond to any questions you may have. On our end, some of the information that we would like to obtain from the conversation is the following:\n\n\n\nLamb Weston’s process flow and operations\n\nSignificant energy uses\n\nIssues or concerns most important to Lamb Weston\n\nRough estimates of annual sales, employees, facility surface area, main product and quantity produced, and operation hours per year.\n\nTotal motor HP, Max motor HP, steam capacity/pressure, air compressor HP/pressure (can be addressed day of assessment)\n\nA main contact for the day of the assessment\n\n\nFeel free to invite anyone you believe may be helpful to participate in the call as well.\n\n\nOur preference for the phone call is Friday 1:00 PM, although if this time does not fit your schedule we can make adjustments.\n\n\nWe look forward to working with Lamb Weston.\n\n\nRegards,', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1828, 376, 2318, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-02', 'I asked a variety of questions about the facility. The questions were answered promptly afterward. ', NULL, NULL, 939),
(1829, 381, 2089, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-10', 'Sent an email, called and left a message', NULL, NULL, 842),
(1830, 225, 2304, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-09', '\nLaver, Ross\nThu, Aug 9, 1:26 PM (5 days ago)\nto me\n\nJoe,\n\n \n\nHow would we go about setting this up? I am available next week and I would like to see if it would be of benefit to our plant. In the past few years we have worked closely with the Oregon Energy Trust on a number of projects and we can discuss that more.\n\n \n\nRegards,\n\n \n\nRoss Laver', NULL, NULL, 941),
(1831, 225, 2304, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-10', '\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\nFri, Aug 10, 4:51 PM (4 days ago)\nto EEC, Joseph, Ross\n\nHi Ross, \n\nThere is not much to setting it up:\nI expect you meet U.S.DOE criteria (they pay for these assessments): the most important being annual energy costs over $100K a year (gas, electric, propane, etc).\nWe would just agree on a visit date, collect preparatory information and schedule a preparatory phone conversation between you and the assessment team leads.\nWould you have time for a call next Tuesday 8/14 at some time in the window of 2PM to 4PM so I can answer any questions you have?   (If not, is there a better time?  I have a lot of open space on my calendar for a good part of Wednesday and Thursday - 8/15 & 8/16.)', NULL, NULL, 941),
(1832, 225, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-14', 'Hi Ross,\n\nI just tried calling to see if I might get lucky and catch you. I\'d be glad to fill you in on the details of our program. I can can call at a time you suggest, or feel fee to call me at 541/737-5034.\n\n\n- Joe Junke', NULL, NULL, 941),
(1833, 225, 2326, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-10', 'Hello Joe,\n\n \n\nMy name is Rachel Sullivan. I’m the manager of a family business in Brewster, Washington. We grow, pack and ship fresh apples. We are interested in learning more about what your team does, and in having an energy assessment audit conducted for our business. I would love to discuss this with you if you are interested. I have copied Derek Divis on this e-mail. Derek is our food safety and compliance manager. You can reach either one of us at 509-689-0224, or you can let us know via e-mail when the best time to call you would be.\n\n \n\nThank You,\n\n \n\nRachel Crane Sullivan\n\nCrane & Crane, Inc.\n\nC&S Orchards II L.P.\n\n509-689-0224\n\n509-733-1334\n\nNotice: The information contained in this e-mail (including any attachments)  message may be privileged, confidential and protected from disclosure. If you are not the intended recipient, any dissemination, distribution or copying of the contents of this message is strictly prohibited. If you think you received this message in error, please delete the message and e-mail the sender at Rachelc@cranefamilyorchards.com.', NULL, NULL, 955),
(1834, 225, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-14', 'Called back - agreed to send pre-audit letter. \n\nGrow, pack an ship apples, NH4 refrigeration and a packaging line. At full capacity. Start packing sept 4 til end of May. Spring break might work.\n\nBrewster Washington, close to Wenatchee. \n\nAnnual energy - $16K+ a month. Utility: Douglas County PUD. \n\nCapacity to store 40 K bins of apples - Full in October end, slowly dwindles to near empty in spring break. \n\nIrrigation - Columbia River - 8 pumps 4-5 booster. up to 200 HP.  (100 foot lift?)\n\nPackaging - compressed air and elect motors\npropane water heating. \n\n', NULL, NULL, 955),
(1835, 373, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-15', 'Replied to my email. Implementation followup done.', NULL, NULL, 882),
(1836, 373, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-15', 'Implementation - Steve emailed me back, interview done.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1837, 225, 2231, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Richard - hoping to get your sense of wher Tacoma WW is. I am getting to a point where I need to schedule them or somone else to finish my summer, but then will not be able to work with them until our next break (winter, spring, or summer)\n\nHe called back - Jody the one to talk to but no number. We tried to call Norm together and LVM.', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1838, 225, 2304, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', '\nJoseph F. Junker\n11:34 AM (0 minutes ago)\nto Ross\n\nHi Ross, \n\nJust tried calling again. I left voice mail but I am not sure it is your box. The outgoing message did not clarify if it was your line.  \n\nAgain: I\'d be glad to fill you in on the details of our program. I can can call at a time you suggest, or feel fee to call me at 541/737-5034.  ', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1839, 225, 2304, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Laver, Ross\n12:34 PM (1 hour ago)\nto me\n\nJoe, \n\nI will call you back later today or tomorrow. You have the correct phone number. It forwards to my cell and when I am on the plant floor I do not carry it.\n\nSorry for the delay in my response.\n\nThanks,\n\nRoss\n\n__________________\n(I replied)\nSounds good Ross, thanks for the confirmation. I will look forward to your call. If you call tomorrow (Friday) try using my cell number: 541/619-6634 as I will not be at my desk for a good part of the day.', NULL, NULL, 941),
(1840, 225, 2322, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', '\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\n11:40 AM (2 hours ago)\nto Kelly.Alexander, Colleen, Anika.Sarkar, EEC, Joseph\n\nHi Kelly, \n\nI am not sure if you are back from vacation yet..... \n\nBut I am looking forward to hearing from you regarding the best day to visit in the week of September 10th so I can lock in a team. Can you get back to me on that at your earliest convenience?\n\nThanks! - Joe Junker\n', NULL, NULL, 947),
(1841, 225, 2322, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Right now i have Tuesday the 11th locked down as the best day. Let me know if this works for your team.', NULL, NULL, 947),
(1842, 225, 2322, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Great, Thanks Kelly.\n\nI think Tuesday September 11th is a good day for the team to visit you.  (Management team - can correct me if I am wrong?)\n\nMuch appreciated - Joe', NULL, NULL, 947),
(1843, 225, 2308, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Hi Grant,\n\nI thought I would circle back to see what you found out when you talked to your management team about having us come in for a no-cost assessment of your wastewater process and perhaps some other subset of your plant.\n\nWhen we talked last you thought you might want us to come in late August. At the moment we may still have one more opening in that time frame. (I have talked to a couple places about visiting then but none have locked in the date yet.) In any case I would be pleased to put you on our calendar in the next 6 months or so at a time that works for you and our team. \n\nLet me know - I will be glad to hear from you.\n\n- Joe Junker', NULL, NULL, 944),
(1844, 225, 2295, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'LVM: Trying to schedule this assessment, sounds like I need to talk to Jody, but I do not have her number. Could you share it with me?', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1845, 225, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'LVM: Trying to schedule this assessment, sounds like I need to talk to Jody, but I do not have her number. Could you share it with me?', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1846, 225, 2292, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Eric, Norman, I just left you voice mail. (Jody, I do not have your number so could not call you)\n\nI would like see if we can sort out making our pre-data collection as low effort for you a possible so we can set a data for us to visit your facility. It would be ideal for us to visit before the end of August. Once the OSU school term starts in mid September, we will be hard pressed to travel the distance to your site until winter or spring break. \n\nCan you please contact me at your earliest convenience? \nIf you are able to call today - Thursday - I should be available on my office phone: 541/ 737-5034 . \nTomorrow my cell number: 541 619-6634 will be the best way to reach me as I will be away from my desk for a good deal of the day.\nThanks!\n\n- Joe Junker\n', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1847, 225, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Hi Steve,\n\nI just left you voice mail as well. I would be glad to work with you to set up assessments of your remaining sites in Portland and the Dalles. Is there a good time to call?\n\n- Joe Junker', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1848, 225, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Hi Steve,\n\nI just left you voice mail as well. I would be glad to work with you to set up assessments of your remaining sites in Portland and the Dalles. Is there a good time to call?\n\n- Joe Junker', NULL, NULL, 931),
(1849, 225, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-27', '\nYork, Freeman {FLNA} <Freeman.York@pepsico.com>\nFri, Jul 27, 11:37 AM\nto OSU\n\nGood morning!\n\n \n\nI was given your brochure and contact information from Ray Edwards with Northwest Natural, and was hoping to set up a time to talk about performing an energy assessment at my production facility in Vancouver, Washington? Please let me know, thanks!\n\n ', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1850, 225, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-30', '\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\nJul 30, 2018, 1:24 PM\nto Freeman.York, EEC\n\nHello Freeman,\n\nI would be glad to talk to you about our program. I have a pretty open schedule on this coming Wednesday (Aug 1). How about a conversation at 11 AM?   Let me know if that works.\n\nIf not I currently have an open calendar on Wednesday between 10 AM and 5 PM (other than a 1-2 PM Meeting.) Otherwise we could target another day if that would be better. \n\nIn the mean time here is a little information about our sevice : \n___________________\nThe Oregon State University Energy Efficiency Center (OSU EEC) offers no-cost full scope energy and efficiency assessments to industry. The center has provided over 700 of these to Northwest industries since 1986 and identified over $129 Million in cost savings and productivity opportunities. Based on follow up with assessed facilities, an average implemented savings of approximately $40,000 has been achieved.\n\nThe assessment process entails a one day visit to your facility after collecting preliminary information. The team of analysts will tour your site, brainstorm on potential opportunities and devote the remainder of the day to collecting data that supports an analysis. Within a typical 60 days, a report is delivered with specific recommendations to reduce energy and waste streams costs and increase productivity and profitability. \n\nThe OSU EEC has a full compliment of state of the art equipment to bring to bear on these assessments including combustion analyzers, thermal imaging cameras, a non-contact ultrasonic flow meter, specialized refrigeration system analysis tools, power quality analyzers, and a broad selection of data loggers. If you have any systems that might benefit from the evaluation these allow, the center can help. \n\nIf this sound interesting to you, you can find out more about the OSU EEC at: http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu/\n___________________ \n\nI look forward to talking with you!\n\n- Joe Junker', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1851, 225, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-02', 'Talked to Freeman and followed up with an assessment offer. ', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1852, 225, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'Hi Freeman,\n\nWe just had a late August site visit cancellation so I wanted to reach out to you to see if you would like to work to have us assess you in the next couple of weeks?\n\nLet me know what you think.\n\n- Joe Junker. ', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1853, 225, 2292, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-16', 'I could not make today. Sorry & tomorrow will be difficult for me.  Can we schedule for the week of the 27th?\n\n___________\n\n\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\nThu, Aug 16, 4:14 PM (17 hours ago)\nto Jody, Richard, Eric, NCook, Bob, MWalker2, ESI\n\nHi Jody,\n\nDo you mean schedule our visit for the week of the 27th? Yes that would work well for us. Are any days in that week particularly good or bad ? I suppose later in the week would allow more time to get preparatory data reviewed before we arrive. \n\nIn the mean time would you have time for a conversation early next week? If so are there any good times to target to call? (And a good number to call?)\n\nThanks\n\n- Joe\n\n______________\n\nHi Joe- i\'m working on some priority projects that are taking precedent over putting any time into this until later this month so what I was suggesting was that we have the call that you wanted or requested today or tomorrow for a couple weeks down the road.\n______________\n\nOK - so it sounds like it will not work for us to visit this Summer then? If so, we can schedule that call for the week of the 27th and work towards scheduling a visit for either Winter Break or Spring Break this year. \n\n_________\n\nJoe - let me check with Norm.  We are all busy with some big initiatives but he may be able to work with you or find someone on staff to help you.\n\n\nNorm - I\'ve looked this over & it doesn\'t seem too burdensome to provide the highlighted information Joe is requesting.  Is this something you can handle?  Joe can be up here the last week of August.  I don\'t have any time on my schedule to accommodate working on this with Joe.  Can you?  Joe is wanted to come up before September & it sounds like the last week of August works for him.  \n\nKeep me posted so I can report up that they will be on-site.  Mike and Judy like to support colleges & we may be able to realize some electrical savings as a result.\n​\nThanks,\nJody\n\n\n\n', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1854, 225, 2295, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-17', 'Thanks Jody, \n\nNorm, as noted we can simplify the pre-visit data collection. I would still be glad to schedule a site visit for the week of August 27. Let me know if I should plan to work with you to set that up. ', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1855, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-07-05', 'Hello Jim,\n\nI would be glad to work with you to set up an assessment. As Michele noted, it has been quite awhile since our last visit. (I will follow up with a copy of that report). \n\nAs background for you, the Oregon State University Energy Efficiency Center (OSU EEC) a U.S.DOE Industrial Assessment Center, offers no-cost full scope energy and efficiency assessments to industry. The center has provided over 700 of these to Northwest industries since 1986 and identified over $160 Million in cost savings and productivity opportunities. Based on follow up with assessed facilities, and average implemented savings of approximately $40,000 has been achieved.\n\nThe assessment process entails a one day visit to your facility after collecting preliminary information. The team of analysts will tour your site, brainstorm on potential opportunities and devote the remainder of the day to collecting data that supports an analysis. Within a typical 60 days, a report is delivered with specific recommendations to reduce energy and waste streams costs and increase productivity and profitability. \n\nThe OSU EEC has a full compliment of state of the art equipment to bring to bear on these assessments including combustion analyzers, thermal imaging cameras, a non-contact ultrasonic flow meter, specialized refrigeration system analysis tools, power quality analyzers, and a broad selection of data loggers. If you have any systems that might benefit from the evaluation these allow, the center can help. \n\nIf this sound interesting to you, you can find out more about the OSU EEC at: http://eec.engr.oregonstate.edu/ or contact the me at 541/737-5034, or  Joe.Junker@oregonstate.edu. \n\nI hope to get a chance to work with you \n\n- Joe Junker\n', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1856, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-08', 'Hello Jim, I thought I would follow up again. I would be glad to work with you to set up an assessment. To repeat some background for you, the Oregon State Univ', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1857, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-17', 'I apologize for the delays in getting back to you, please let me know when you would be available next week for a phone conversation so we can get the ball rolling. \n\nSincerely,\n\nJim Cockrell', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1858, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-17', 'Thanks Jim, \n\nNo worries of course. A call to get things going sounds perfect. I have time for a call next Tuesday (8/21) from noon to 2 pm, or after 3 pm OR any time next Wednesday (8/22) afternoon.  If neither day works for you have have time later in the week too.\n', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1859, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-17', 'Tuesday 8/21 @1pm would be great – please call my office phone. Thank you Sent: Friday, August 17, 2018 1:22 PM To: Cockrell, James <James.Cockrell@owenscorning', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1860, 225, 2295, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-20', 'Hi Norm,\n\nI just left you voice mail. I was looking for closure on whether you would like to have us visit this summer, or put things forward to an upcoming winter, spring or summer break. If this summer is not a good option for you then I would like to offer our last U.S.DOE contracted summer assessment to another facility..\n\nThanks - Joe', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1861, 378, 1590, 1, 0, 14154, '2018-08-20', 'Hi Peter:\n \nDuane forwarded me your email. I have been involved with helping C&D make improvements off an on for 10 years when the first project was implemented. That project was buying the Sequencer to control all the Sawmill air compressors plus adding a Lead/lag control to the Planer air compressor for future growth.\n \nTo answer your questions.\n \n1)  The Rogers MAX 3 Sequencer controls all 3 Sawmill air compressors. Our service tech Duane Henderson worked with C & D electricians to get the system functioning proper in the past year. The Sequencer will direct the Starting, Loading, Unloading, and Parking all 3 air compressors.  This included adjusted each air compressor\'s pilot valve to prevent or minimize modulation.\n \nNot sure what balance was set to provide for a reasonably energy efficient operation versus a very energy efficient operation with no air compressor modulating in the Sequencer\'s Load/Unload pressure band. The latter has caused rapid cycling of the air compressors and led to repeated premature component failures. We have recommended to Steve Henry that they redo the wet-side header piping and add 3000 gallon of wet-side receiver capacity. See attached files.\n \n2)  The Planer air compressors are set up to operate Load/Unload. All the necessary control devices are in placed and in operations. Duane reports that the alternator may not been working properly. Like the Sawmill system, due to no wet-side receiver capacity, each air compressor may be modulating as a balance between energy efficiency and compressor reliability.\n \n3)  No.  The waste heat from these air compressors is not usable by a desiccant dryer.   Heat of compression desiccant dryers need a source of compressed air that is oil-free and in excess of 375Â°F.  Also heatless desiccant dryers cannot economically be modified into any form of heated purge dryer.   Best try to see that all their desiccant dryers have energy efficient purge controls and that they are working properly.', NULL, 897, 502),
(1862, 374, 2282, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-15', 'Cory,\n\nThank you for your help with our assessment. I will send a follow-up assessment email after our team debrief in a week. In the mean time we have initial questions sorted by system as follows.  \n\nAdditionally, on Thursday please bring the two forms I gave you regarding information release and report copies completed.\n\nDryer\nDryer temperature (both in and out)\nDryer CFM\nWater Heater\nWater temperature (both in and out)\nAir temperature (both in and out)\nWater and air flowrates\nMost recent combustion readings\nForklifts\nCharger and battery nameplate data\nCan each charger only charge one battery at a time?\nLighting\nWattage for LED fixtures (single bulb) in mechanical room, refrigeration room, second floor hallways, and water heater rooms\nTypical occupant hours for  mechanical room, refrigeration room, second floor hallways, and water heater rooms\nWattage for two bulb fixture sin storage warehouses and typical occupancy hours of storage warehouse\nWattage for metal halid fixtures throughout production rooms (dryer, evap etc)\nWattage for T5, T8, and T12 fixtures\nRefrigeration\nLogs for Ammonia compressors\nCurrent ammonia system approach temperature\nMinimum condensing temperature (estimate is appropriate)\nMinimum allowable pressure drop across the expansion valve in the ammonia cycle\nOther\nContract/condensate information for returning condensate to Coyote River\nCount of motors that are not on notched v-belts, along with annual capacities? We know that the condenser fans do not use notched belts, but we were unable to confirm any other motors.', NULL, NULL, 952),
(1863, 374, 0, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-20', 'With PGE Coyote Springs. Discussed condensate. 541 481 8324', NULL, NULL, 952),
(1864, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-21', 'Joe I have a plant issue that I need to address, can we please reschedule this call for later this week or early next week.\n\nSorry for the late notice,\n\nJim Cockrell\n\n_______\n\nAbsolutely! do you have any particular times at work good for you? If not I can send you some possible times when I get to my desk.\n\n____________\n\nJames,\n\nI have open windows for a call\n\nTomorrow, (Wednesday 8/22), at\n11:30 AM to 12:00 PM\n1:00 PM to 2:00 PM\n3:30 PM to 5:00 PM\nAnd Thursday 8/23 at\n12:00 PM to 5:00 PM\n- Joe', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1866, 378, 0, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-23', 'Pre-Audit phone call. Went over process at facility, areas of interest, future projects the facility is focusing on, and logistics for the assessment.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1867, 378, 2295, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-23', 'Pre-Audit phone call. Went over process at facility, areas of interest, future projects the facility is focusing on, and logistics for the assessment.', NULL, 900, NULL),
(1868, 373, 2018, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-23', 'Email sent, no response. Will make another call.', NULL, NULL, 861),
(1869, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-23', 'Hi Jim,\n\nI was glad to talk to you today and look forward to fielding a team to visit Owens Corning, Linnton Asphalt and perform an assessment.\n\nI\'ve attached our preaudit letter (I mentioned it when we spoke). It describes our assessment process and outlines a number of things we work to collect to best prepare for an assessment. Most important to us are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form:  This requests some logistic details along with eligibility information U.S.DOE asks us to have on file for assessments we perform.\n\nUtility History:  You have several options including the Utility Release Permission form to help us collect a year or more profile of your of month-to-month energy use and utility expenses. Typically most pertinent are Electric and Gas utilities. \nThe letter also includes a list of additional helpful preparatory information. While not required, these items can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you. \n\nOur next step will be to schedule an assessment for a time that works for both you and the students.  (As we agreed this will likely be this winter or next spring.) Before we visit we would also like to schedule a brief pre-visit phone call to discuss logistics and learn more about your process, key energy using equipment, anything you would like us to make sure to consider while there.  \n\nPlease call me if you have any questions.  I look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n\n(You might want to visit our web-site to find out more about our program: http://eec.oregonstate.edu/)\n', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1870, 374, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-24', 'Randy,\n\nThank you for your help so far with Columbia River Technologies and Production steam condensate buyback. I have three hopefully quick questions our client wants us to look into:\nWho is responsible for implementing the pumping and piping system for condensate return? If the facility is responsible, what sort of piping distance and pumping requirements would we need to implement? \nWhat are the benchmark standards for quality of condensate (ppm or conductivity) required for the demineralizer and cooling tower?\nIf you can help me answer either of these questions, I would be very thankful.\n\nThank you for your time and all your help so far!', NULL, NULL, 952),
(1871, 381, 2089, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-27', 'Called, left a message, and sent an email\nResponded with an email saying he did not recall being a part of the audit and could not answer our questions ', NULL, NULL, 842),
(1872, 360, 2339, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-08-29', 'Called Orlando about resizing pumps and implementation costs. He suggested using a VFD and replacing two of the pumps with one pump. He said this would be the  most efficient. Our conversation switched from phone to email because he said he needed time to resize the pump and calculate the implementation cost.', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1873, 360, 2340, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-08-29', 'Good afternoon Jacques, \n\nMy name is Monica Heng and I work for the Oregon State University Energy Efficiency Center. I\'m recommending to a client, C&D Lumber, that they upgrade their pumping system for their log sprinklers. I need assistance with sizing pumps, in addition with obtaining a general quote. Could you help me with my request? If not, could you direct me towards someone who can help me with my request? \n\nThanks,', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1874, 378, 2123, 1, 0, 14222, '2018-08-29', 'Peter,\n\nHere is a study done several years ago, as well as an Energy Audit.\n\nPeter, RJG and Matt,\n\nI downloaded the Protected Water and Yard Water Pumps (forgot to get the Well Pump again), and have updated the data.  I think weÃ¢â‚¬â„¢re saving over 300,000 kWh/y on the Protected Water Pumps currently, and should be able to get another 50,000-100,000 kWh/y if we can help them lower the pressure and eliminate some excess uses.  Peter, IÃ¢â‚¬â„¢m not sure how much youÃ¢â‚¬â„¢re doing with these two systems, but youÃ¢â‚¬â„¢ll have some data if you want to use it.', NULL, 900, NULL),
(1875, 378, 2295, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-04', 'Norman,\n\nHi, this is Peter Sukamto from OSU\'s Energy Efficiency Center. Attached is my site report which details what we did during our assessment and opportunities for recommendations that we will be exploring over the next couple of months. Also attached is a document with questions from our team that if answered will help tremendously with our work and calculations here in Corvallis. Please let me know if you have any questions or concerns.', NULL, 900, NULL),
(1876, 373, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-04', 'Emailed out questions', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1877, 225, 2322, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-05', '\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\nAttachments\n1:52 PM (0 minutes ago)\nto Kelly.Alexander, EEC\n\nHi Kelly, \n\nJust touching base regarding our scheduled visit to your Troutdale site next Tuesday September 11.  Following are some reminders and questions.\nWe will arrive at 8 AM with a team of 5-6 analysts and myself. \nCan your confirm we have the correct address at 5700 NE Sundial RD, Troutdale, OR?\nWe hope you will have a space we can take over to use as a base of operations for the day.\nWe anticipate needing support from your electrical personnel to set data loggers and take live reading on selected equipment. The typical time-frame for this is from 12:30 PM to 2:30 PM. \nI do not recall if you were planning to provide lunch for the team or not. Could you let me know on that?\nI am pasting our standard assessment day & report follow-up schedule below and also attaching it.\nThanks - Looking forward to working with you. \n\n- Joe Junker', NULL, NULL, 947),
(1878, 225, 2322, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-06', 'Called to confirm they are planning to host us. YES. Will provide lunch, order sandwiches that day, electrician at lunch and early afternoon. Standard safety gear fine. ', NULL, NULL, 947),
(1879, 373, 2328, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-11', 'Emailed the second round of questions to Freeman', NULL, NULL, 208),
(1880, 225, 1959, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-12', 'Phil Keith: LVM, \nJohn Bradly:no answer, \nAndy Briesmeister: number for someone else according to voicemail, \nBryan Galloway:number for someone else according to voicemail\n\nFollowed up with IMP email to all four\n', NULL, NULL, 859),
(1881, 225, 2343, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-13', 'Greetings Mr. Junker,\n\n \n\nMy name is Matthew Pekar. I am Manager of the Energy and GHG program for United Technologies Corporation- (UTC). United Technologies Corporation is an international conglomeration of four primary business units: Carrier air conditioning, Otis elevators, Pratt and Whitney jet engines and United Technologies Aerospace. UTC has an ongoing GHG reduction goal of 3% year over year absolute. In pursuit of this goal the corporation mandates that energy audits be conducted at our facilities worldwide every three to five years. Earlier this week I was contacted by one of our sites in Oregon about having an energy audit conducted before the end of the year. One of my job responsibilities is to conduct energy audits at some of our smaller sites. Unfortunately I will not be available to get to this Oregon location. The site I am referring to is a 25,000 sqft facility. It is primarily offices with some machining and test equipment. I would like to speak with you regarding the opportunity for you and your team to conduct an energy audit of the facility. I have had success in the past working with other Industrial Assessment Centers. I was wondering if this opportunity was of interest to you.\n\n \n\nPlease contact me for further discussion. Note, I am located in the Eastern Time Zone. The best time to reach me is between 7am and 2pm your time. I will be in meetings all day Friday. Monday through Thursday next week are open for a phone meeting.\n\n \n\nI look forward to your response.\n\n \n\nBest regards,\n\n \n\nMatthew J. Pekar, PE., CEM.\n\nSenior Manager EH&S\n\nEnergy and Greenhouse Gases\n\n \n\nUnited Technologies Corporation\n\n9 Farm Springs Road\n\nM/S 9FS101\n\nFarmington, CT. 06032\n\n \n\n(O) 860-728-6583\n\n(E) Matthew.Pekar@UTC.com\n\n ', NULL, NULL, 956),
(1882, 225, 2343, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-18', 'Hello Matthew, \n\nI am following up on email I just sent. Thank you for reaching out.  I would be glad to work with you to set up an assessment of your Oregon site. Given your interest in having us visit this year: depending on their location we could either visit them during this fall term or in the beginning of Winter break. In any case I would be glad to talk to you about it. My schedule has openings:\nTomorrow (Wed 9/19) between 1 pm and 2 pm and from 3 pm to 5 pm. \nThursday (9/20) from 11 am to 5 pm.\nLet me know if there is a good time in those windows for you to talk. \n\n- Joe Junker\n\n(I assume you are aware of IAC eligibility criteria through your past collaborations. While most can be waived one criteria that U.S.DOE that pays for these assessments holds to is that the site spends over $100,000 on energy cost. )\n\n- Joe Junker\n', NULL, NULL, 956),
(1883, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-13', 'Sorry for the delay’s – let me know what other data you may need (I’m still looking for a file that has the burner information on it – but not much has changed from the past survey)\n\n \n\nJim Cockrell', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1884, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-18', 'Thank you James,\n\nI will get my baseline team working on this. Last we spoke I think we agreed to target a site visit for this coming Winter term. Does that still work for you?\n\n- Joe ', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1885, 381, 1820, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-18', 'Sent email and called, no longer works at Kroger Swan Island', NULL, NULL, 842),
(1886, 368, 2209, 1, 0, 13860, '2018-09-20', 'Sent an email and called. Got in touch with Mike Mader. We scheduled a call for Friday 9/21/18 at 8 am.\nCalled and left voicemail Friday at 8 am.', NULL, 864, 914),
(1887, 368, 2256, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-20', 'Called and left an email for Kelly Staines.', NULL, -1, 921),
(1888, 368, 2199, 1, 0, 13859, '2018-09-20', 'Scheduled a call for Monday 09/24/18 at 2 p.m. with Kent. He\'s calling me.', NULL, 863, 907),
(1889, 368, 2324, 2, 0, 13958, '2018-09-20', 'Talked to Michael. He will try and get back by Friday 9/21/18. Will call Monday 9/24/18 if he does not get back to me.', NULL, 872, 364),
(1890, 225, 1415, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-21', 'Hi Mr. Junker,\n\n \n\nMy name is Sachin Nimbalkar, the Technical Account Manager (TAM) for the DOE’s Better Plants Program. I work at DOE’s Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and am located in Oak Ridge, Tennessee. I provide technical support to DOE’s Better Plants Program Partners (such as Owens Corning, Volvo, ArcelorMittal, GM, Navistar, etc.) through energy road map development, baselining analysis, In-Plant Trainings, field visits to investigate feasible measures to reduce process energy requirements.\n\n \n\nThe main purpose of my email is to introduce you to Mr. Tim Alexander and Mr. Brian Faivre of Deschutes Brewery. Deschutes Brewery is located in Bend, Oregon. The facility produces different varieties of beers such as lagers, ales and hand craft beers. All Deschutes facilities are interested in joining the Better Plants program and we are currently talking with their sustainability group. Mr. Alexander and Mr. Faivre are interested in having a cross-cutting (all system) IAC energy assessment completed at their Bend, OR Plant. I believe this plant meets all the eligibility requirements for receiving an IAC assessment. Mr. Alexander and Mr. Faivre could contact you in the nearest future and provide you additional information if needed. I just wanted to introduce you to Mr. Alexander and Mr. Faivre and give you a heads-up. If possible, please give them a priority in terms of scheduling an IAC assessment as they may become a Better Plants Partner.\n\n \n\nPlease let me know if you have any comments or questions.\n\n \n\nThank you!\n\n \n\nBest Regards,\n\nSachin\n\n \n\nSachin Nimbalkar, PhD\n\nTechnical Account Manager\n\nUS DOE’s Better Plants Program\n\nOak Ridge National Laboratory\n\n865-576-3590\n\nnimbalkarsu@ornl.gov', NULL, NULL, 314),
(1891, 225, 1415, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-21', 'Hi Joseph,\n\n \n\nPlease let me know any additional information you require, and I will be happy to provide it.\n\n \n\nThanks,\nTim', NULL, NULL, 314),
(1892, 225, 1415, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-25', '\nJoseph F. Junker <joseph.f.junker@gmail.com>\nAttachments\n3:12 PM (26 minutes ago)\nto TAlexander, Joseph\n\nHello Tim,\n\nI\'ll be pleased to work with you to set up an assessment!. I expect we should be able schedule a visit for either this December, or some time in Winter term or over spring break (March 25-29).\n\nIf you like, I could give you a call to answer any questions you might have regarding our program. If so, let me know what number to call. In the mean time I\'ve attached our pre-audit letter. It describes our assessment process and information we work to collect to prepare our visit. Most important to us are the:\nIndustrial Pre-Assessment Form:  This requests some logistic details along with eligibility information U.S.DOE asks us to have on file for assessments we perform.\n\nUtility History:  You have several options including the Utility Release Permission form to help us collect a year or more profile of your of month-to-month energy use and utility expenses. Typically most pertinent are Electric and Gas utilities. \nThe letter also lists additional helpful preparatory information. While not required, these items can greatly improve the job we are able to do for you. \n\nPlease call me if you have any questions.  I look forward to working with you. Thanks!\n\n-Joe Junker.\n\n(We have been able to visit you in the past, but it has been over five years, so likely lots has changed, offering new opportunities. You may recall our visit, we have you in our records as Brew Master from then.)\n\n\n\nYou might want to visit our web-site to find out more about our program: http://eec.oregonstate.edu/)  \n', NULL, NULL, 314),
(1893, 378, 2295, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-09-14', 'Norman,\n\nGood afternoon, I just wanted to check in on the status of the questions I sent earlier. A couple of our recommendations rely heavily on the requested information in order to be accurate. If the information can\'t be acquired, let me know, part of our job is making due with what we have. Again, if there is anything I can do for you please let me know. \n\nThanks,\nPeter', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1894, 378, 2295, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-09-17', 'Called Norman and left a message regarding getting back to us with answers to the questions we sent several weeks ago. ', NULL, NULL, 940),
(1895, 373, 2018, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-10-03', 'Implementation call completed, sent an email before the call and then sent an email saying thanks for the call and to disregard the email.', NULL, NULL, 861),
(1896, 368, 2256, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-04', 'Called and emailed Kelly asking to clarify some of his implementation responses. ', NULL, -1, 921),
(1897, 368, 2324, 2, 0, 13958, '2018-10-04', 'Called and spoke again with Michael. He said he would reply to my email with his response.', NULL, 872, 364),
(1898, 225, 1415, 1, 0, NULL, '0000-00-00', 'Hi Joseph,\n\n \n\nThis is quite an involved form!  I will try to fill it out when I have time, but I don’t know how much we spend on utilities or who our providers are, as that is not really my department.  I’ll see if I can figure out the people who know.\n\n\nTim\n______\n\nThanks Tim,\n\nLets not let the form be a barrier to moving forward!  We can duck a lot of it as I already know your facility is eligible: we have been there in the past, and ORNL recommends you.  I would like to give you a call to sort it out. I tried a number for you we had in our records from our last visit: 408-761-1725, but it seems to be someone else\'s now. \n\n(FYI: When we were last there, your electrical and gas providers were Pacific Power and Cascade Natural Gas) \n\nAlso - would it work for you to target our visit for early December?.\n____\n\nActually found the right person!  I will forward you a lot of utilities info in a second.\n\nTim\n\n_______\n\nThe excel document has our usage way back to 2005 for most utilities, and the word document is our account information. Mathew at pacific power (see below) is the only personal contact we have, the rest are just general contact numbers.\n\nThanks,\n\nTim\n\n_________\n\nHave fun! Below is Mathew’s contact for pacific power, but I’m not sure you need much more that what we have here.\n\nMatthew J Chancellor  |  Regional Business Manager  |  Pacific Power\n63820 Clausen DR STE 100|  Bend, Oregon 97703|Office (541) 633-2483  | Cell (541) 419-0373\n______\n\nGot this, thanks! I will get my baseline team going on preparations: \nNot sure if you saw, I am thinking I would like to target our visit for early December - does that work for you? \nOur next step will be to schedule a pre-visit conversation to go over assessment logistics and get up to date with your process and current key interests.  \nWill it be ok with you for us to invite Utility and incentive folks to participate if they are interested? (Matthew - Pacific  Power, Energy Trust, Cascade Natural Gas) They can sometimes facilitate incentives on energy projects. \n- Joe\n\n__________\n\nHi Joseph,\n\nEarly December works with the caveat that my wife is pregnant with a due date of December 12, so if it happens a little early I will have to get someone else to host.  Still, we can cross that bridge when we get there.  After that I will be out until early next year.  But I would say if you can make the first week of December work, then this should be good.\n\nSounds good on the conversation…I am booked this week, but next week I have some time Wednesday morning from 9-11 and Thursday after 2PM.\n\nYou are welcome to invite utility folks.  With regard to CNG, it is important to note that we buy our natural gas wholesale, and only pay CNG for the delivery, so often the incentives they offer do not apply to us.\n\nThanks,\n\nTim\n\n___________________________\n\nGood to know. We might want to target a date in January then. (I was thinking the week of Dec. 10th - the week of the 3rd is finals week. ). Perhaps January would be better.  I do not know my team availability for winter term yet, so not sure of the exact date, but if we could make it before January 7, when term starts, getting there and back would be easier before students are in classes. If not we are able to get over during term, we just have to work around everyone\'s schedule.\n\nWe should set the date and our team before the call so we can have the right folks on our end, so likely more than a week or two out for that. \n\nWe will invite the utility follks.  Roger on CNG.  They likley will not want to join. \n\nBy the way - what is your phone number?\n\n_____\n\nHey Joseph,\n\nLet’s plan on the first week of January then.  I should be back on the first business day after January 1.\n\nMy number is 408-761-1725.', NULL, NULL, 314),
