e if they are interested? (Matthew - Pacific  Power, Energy Trust, Cascade Natural Gas) They can sometimes facilitate incentives on energy projects. \n- Joe\n\n_______________\n\nHi Joseph,\n\n \n\nEarly December works with the caveat that my wife is pregnant with a due date of December 12, so if it happens a little early I will have to get someone else to host.  Still, we can cross that bridge when we get there.  After that I will be out until early next year.  But I would say if you can make the first week of December work, then this should be good.\n\n \n\nSounds good on the conversation…I am booked this week, but next week I have some time Wednesday morning from 9-11 and Thursday after 2PM.\n\n \n\nYou are welcome to invite utility folks.  With regard to CNG, it is important to note that we buy our natural gas wholesale, and only pay CNG for the delivery, so often the incentives they offer do not apply to us.\n\n \n\nThanks,\n\nTim\n\n_____________\n\nGood to know. We might want to target a date in January then. (I was thinking the week of Dec. 10th - the week of the 3rd is finals week. ). Perhaps January would be better.  I do not know my team availability for winter term yet, so not sure of the exact date, but if we could make it before January 7, when term starts, getting there and back would be easier before students are in classes. If not we are able to get over during term, we just have to work around everyone\'s schedule.\n\nWe should set the date and our team before the call so we can have the right folks on our end, so likely more than a week or two out for that. \n\nWe will invite the utility follks.  Roger on CNG.  They likley will not want to join. \n\nBy the way - what is your phone number?\n\nThanks - Joe\n\n_____\n\nHey Joseph,\n\nLet’s plan on the first week of January then.  I should be back on the first business day after January 1.\n\nMy number is 408-761-1725.\n\nThanks,\nTim\n\n_________\n\nSounds like a plan. Thanks for the number. We will be following up to schedule a pre-visit call once we set a team. Thanks - Looking forward to it - Joe \n\n\n\n\n\n\n', NULL, NULL, 314),
(1900, 225, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-04', 'Phil Keith LVM : I am sorry we have not been able to connect to get a sense of the value of our assessment report. We will list you as \"unresponsive\" to U.S.DOE (that pays for these reports)', NULL, NULL, 859),
(1901, 365, 783, 1, 1, NULL, '2018-10-05', '\n\nHello,\n\nMy name is Davis Chamorro, I work for the OSU Energy Efficiency Center. We perform no-cost energy assessments for small to mid-size manufacturing facilities around the PNW. We visited Stahlbush in November 2013 -- roughly 5 years ago -- making Stahlbush eligible for another assessment. Our 2013 visit resulted in an estimated $50,000 recommended annual savings.\n\nIf this sounds like something of interest to you, please let me know. I can reply with more information about the EEC and we can start setting up a date for the visit.\n\nThank you and good day', 27, NULL, 31),
(1902, 362, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-05', 'Called Steve Nelson and did not get a response. I also sent him the standard template email summarizing our request for implementation feedback.', NULL, NULL, 890),
(1903, 362, 2195, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-05', 'I called Kyle Pyke and he did not respond. I also sent him the standard email response regarding implementation feedback.', NULL, NULL, 905);
INSERT INTO `ContactLog` (`ContactLogId`, `EmployeeId`, `PersonId`, `ContactTypeId`, `GrantId`, `TaskId`, `Date`, `Notes`, `LeadId`, `ProjectId`, `LocationId`) VALUES
(1904, 365, 2202, 1, 0, 14080, '2018-10-10', 'Hi Davis,\n\n \n\nBelow are my responses to your questions.  I hope these help.\n\n \n\nWe enjoyed having the Students visit our facility.  They demonstrated good insight and came to some good conclusions.  I’m not sure we learned anything significantly new as a result of the experience.  We were heading down these paths already.  That being said – we felt having the Students here was extremely valuable and encourage more such activities.   If I’m remembering correctly - one of the students was working on a Masters in Chemical Engineering.  I was shocked to learn that ours was the first Chemical Plant she had ever toured.  One of our companies core values is “Corporate Citizenship”.  By offering the opportunity for students to better understand what goes on in “the real world” I believe we make the community better.  We’re happy to provide “hands-on” experience to young minds.\n\n \n\nIf you have any further questions please feel free to contact me.\n\n \n\nMy best,\n\n \n\nTim\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nReduce Air Leaks\n \n\nImplemented – No expense other than simple maintenance items.  This is an ongoing opportunity.  When someone hears a compressed air system leak the Maintenance Department is notified.  The leaks are fixed.  The leaks are typically at connections or in a worn spot on a hose. \n\n \n\nThe cost savings have not been identified although the trend data for electricity usage over the past few years has remained pretty flat despite ever increasing production volumes.\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nIncrease Chilling Capacity\n \n\nNot implemented – working with outside vendors Chiller reliability and efficiency has dramatically improved.  It was determined that the existing chillers were sufficient for the operation at this time.  We will most likely implement this recommendation in the future as production volumes increase.\n\n \n\nTune Hot Oil Heaters\n \n\nImplemented – The Hot Oil Heaters were tuned during the annual maintenance cycle.  We track Natural Gas usage – the majority of which is used to heat the Hot Oil.  Here are the results over the years:\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\nAs you can see.  There was a significant drop in BTU/kg after the equipment was tuned in early 2018.  This re-established our downward trend line.\n\n \n\n \n\nUpgrade Lighting\n \n\nNot Implemented – Lighting has been upgraded from T-12 to T-8 bulbs.  We are not comfortable moving to LED lighting at this time.  We feel the technology is still a bit new and a greater benefit could be realized by waiting a few more years for further technology improvements and lower system costs', NULL, 862, 908),
(1905, 362, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-08', 'Kyle Pyke responded with an email answering our questions.', NULL, NULL, 905),
(1906, 362, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-08', 'Steve Nelson responded with an email with all the information.', NULL, NULL, 890),
(1907, 368, 2256, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-11', 'Follow up email with Kelly reminding him to get back to me. He has been responsive, but just hasn\'t supplied answers to my follow up questions.', NULL, -1, 921),
(1908, 368, 2324, 1, 0, 13958, '2018-10-11', 'Emailed Michael. He usually answers and says he will get back to me. Skipping the call this week, and sending a reminder email as another tactic to getting data.', NULL, 872, 364),
(1909, 368, 2244, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-10-11', 'Called and emailed Ted asking for implementation. ', NULL, -1, 926),
(1910, 374, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-11', 'Hello Cory,\n\n\nWe have finished our report of the assessment at your facility and I have attached it for your review.  In two weeks time we will be sending it to printing, so if there are any details you would like changed please let us know within that time.  \n\n\nAdditionally, please let us know what you think of the attached trip report. With your permission we would like to use this as an example of our services.\n\nRegards,\n\nRachel McAfee', NULL, NULL, 952),
(1911, 368, 0, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-15', 'TLDR; We have most of the data we need included below. Call back in April of 2019 to updates. Contact Ernie Turner.\n\n1. This is not something I would decide; it is Ernie Turner’s decision. As far as I know, no decision has been made to move forward on this.\n\n2.      This was something we thought would be very easy to accomplish. However, given the amount of difficulty we are having getting the electric buses on the road, this has not yet been implemented. The buses will need to be sent back to California over the next six months to have the battery packs updated. This program is in such flux it is anybody’s guess as to when the charging of more than one, or two buses will start.\n\n3.      This is something we have already started. A contractor has been selected, as has the HVAC equipment. When I received your report, I noticed the estimated cost for this was unreasonably low, around 14K, I think. Not sure where that figure came from, but it was less than the cost of one 3.5 ton unit (two will be used). Overall, the cost is projected to be around $57K. So, obviously, the payback period is artificially low.\n\n4.      This has not yet been implemented. We looked at this and want to do it, but we need to resolve the scheduling issues. Our want is to put it either on a schedule, or timer. The problem with a schedule is the maintenance team runs around the clock, and the water takes a long time to heat to temp. If it was predictable when things needed to be washed we could give either a timer, or schedule. So, if they knew at the beginning of shift one they would need it, we could turn on the timer to heat the water. Unfortunately, it isn’t always this predictable.\n#1. Automatic tire inflator-This is something that I can say with a high degree of confidence will NOT be done. The reasons as I understand them are: space requirement-such a piece of equipment would require a rather large footprint. Necessity-I was told the number of tires being done is not sufficient to warrant a device, such as this. Cost-the cost for such a piece of equipment cannot be justified, at this time. Mark as rejected.\n\n \n\n#2. Control Electrical Demand-This is dependent on the acceptance of the bus build. If BYD can deliver the bus, as promised, which meets all our criteria, we will move forward with this recommendation. If so, it will be implemented within 6-9 months. The new software for the chargers has the capability to sequence the charging of each vehicle, avoiding peak demand charges. I suggest you check back in six months to find out where we are. I am retiring at the end of April, so you will need to contact Ernie Turner directly, or Kelly Hoell.\n\n \n\n#4. Turn off Parts Washer-Currently we are maintaining the water temperature at 150* around the clock. The use demand for this piece of equipment has changed; we are utilizing more “kits” and doing less brake work on site. After having spoken with the mechanic who used this machine the most, I believe it is totally workable to install some controls that would allow the heater to be turned on prior to use, then shut off. Currently, the need for this machine is not unexpected; it can be turned on ahead of time. Check back in six months to see if the timer controls have been installed. Again, contact Ernie Turner.', NULL, NULL, 921),
(1912, 225, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-12', 'LVM: Reminder: $100K energy costs.  The Dalles Site over Spring Break? Portland site: Winter Break or later.', NULL, NULL, 931),
(1913, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-11', 'Hi Jim, \n\nWe have developed an opening for this fall term. Would it work for you to schedule our site visit for Thursday, November 15th?\n\n- Joe Junker\n', NULL, NULL, 431),
(1914, 225, 2306, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-17', 'Jim agreed to set the date for Thursday, November 15th', NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1915, 368, 2274, 2, 0, NULL, '2018-10-18', 'test with  characters that should not show up', NULL, -1, 926),
(1916, 368, 2324, 2, 1, 13958, '2018-10-18', 'The annual budget was frozen, so nothing was done. They are going to revisit the recommendations on next years budget. Call back in April to see if they are going to implement.', -1, 872, 364),
(1917, 225, 2116, 1, 1, -1, '2018-10-24', 'lvm: touching base4', -1, -1, 931),
(1919, 225, 2116, 1, 0, NULL, '2018-10-11', 'Joe, \n\nWe can talk in the morning, if that works for you.  We have three more facilities in Portland and one in The Dallas.\n\nSteve', NULL, NULL, 931),
