INSERT INTO `OMCs` (`OMCId`, `TaskId`, `OMCText`, `SubjectId`) VALUES
(1, 12153, 'The condenser fan attached to the South Stage Room One evaporative condenser is equipped with a VFD controller. The speed on this VFD is set by manual operator input rather than automatic sensors. According to the operator, the VFD is set to run at 30 Hz in the winter, 50 Hz in the summer, and 60 Hz during periods of high demand. Running the VFD at a lower frequency saves energy by reducing the load on the fan motor. In most modern refrigeration systems, a condenser fan VFD operates based on sensor feedback from the compressor head pressure and condensing temperature. Configuring the evaporative condenser fan to operate based on automatic sensors has the potential to save condenser fan energy. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because of a lack of data. Historical data for annual condensing temperature and fan speed is not available, and it is difficult to quantify cost savings associated with this measure.', 13),
(2, 12195, 'NULL', 0),
(3, 12197, 'The facility uses hot water for cleaning and other uses in various parts of the facility. The hot water is\nheated by the boiler, and facility personnel informed analysts that it is constantly needed during\nproduction hours. Analysts identified various heat source opportunities for the facility to dump heat\ninto a hot water loop that would charge a central tank and allow some of the hot water needs to be\nserviced by this added heat. Some of these heat sources include the boiler exhaust stacks that\ncurrently do not use an economizer, waste heat from the air compressors that are glycol cooled, and\nhot whey that is not pre-cooled with incoming milk during the last three hours of production. Facility\npersonnel informed analysts that no sort of economizer can be retrofitted to the boilers for heat\ncapture opportunities. Analysts collected data on both the air compressors and cooling glycol and the\nwhey process. While temperature of the glycol after the air compressors is known, flow\nmeasurements were unable to be taken. An air compressor analysis (shown in the CABAT of the Site\nData section of this report) shows that Compressor #1 and #5 use a combined 1,812,707 kWh\nannually. Assuming that 90% of this energy is rejected as waste heat, and that this can be dumped\ninto a hot water system, it would save the boiler 1,631,436 kWh annually; resulting in an approximate\n$68,500 savings. Analysts do not expect that this number is reflective of the actual heat exchange that\noccurs from the compressor to the glycol. For this reason, and unknowns about the feasibility of\ninstalling a hot water loop from the multiple heat sources to the hot water end uses, this was not\nwritten as a full recommendation.', 3),
(4, 12196, 'Upgrade all belt driven motors to notched V-belts as standard belts need to be replaced. The use of\nnotched V-belts will reduce motor energy use by 2% on average through improved transmission\nefficiency. This is achieved by reducing losses due to bending, friction, slip between the sheave, and\nstretching of the belt over time.\nThere is an estimated 100 HP connected load driven by belts. A rough estimate of 50% of those belts\nare notched V-belts.\nUpgrading standard V-belts to notched V-belts will save an estimate of 534 kWh annually, resulting\nin an annual cost savings of $23. An incremental electricity cost of $0.042/kWh was used. Since the\nfacility already has some notched V-belts stocked, the upgradation will yield an immediate payback\nwith no implementation cost.\nLike standard V-belts, notched V-belts require periodic re-tensioning to maintain their efficiency.\nCalculations were done with general assumptions based on conversations with facility personnel and\ngeneral observations. Notched V-belts are about 20% more expensive than the standard V-belts and\nhence might not ideally be used on motors that cause excessive wear on the current belt (or where\nthe belt is the intended failure point in case of system jam) as savings would be reduced by the\nincreased replacement costs.\nThis did not appear as a full recommendation because we were unable to obtain the necessary\ninventory information and the low cost savings potential.', 11),
(5, 12194, 'Installing a wind turbine can potentially save energy costs by locally producing energy instead of\npurchasing it through a provider. Facility personnel stated that the parking lot would be an\nappropriate location due to open space for wind access, and for aesthetic purposes. An issue with\ninstalling a wind turbine is finding one that can withstand gusts over 100 mph while still being\neffective. There is also a limited wind speed range where a wind turbine is effective. Average wind\nspeeds for Tillamook are lower than optimal for energy production. See Table 1 for annual energy\nproduction as a function of wind speeds.Vendor listings show that a viable option can cost approximately $5,000. These turbines can produce\nroughly 3,500 kWh annually, using an average wind speed of 18.5 mph. This results in an annual\nsavings of approximately $147 using an incremental electricity cost of $0.042. A simple payback\nperiod of 34 years was calculated for this option. This was not researched further due to low savings\npotential.\nThis option may become more viable as technology advances in the alternative energy field. Options\nutilizing wind speeds closer to that of Tillamook’s can potentially lead to more lucrative savings.', 17),
(6, 12098, 'The facility has a 20,000 sq. ft. cool preparation room that is used to prepare ingredients for the cooking step of the production process. There are 8 personnel doors that are of interest due to the lack of insulation. There are 2 large high speed doors that are used by forklifts. The high speed doors open automatically for approximately 10 seconds. Refer to Figure 1 for preparation room layout along with a temperature profile. Only doors of interest were included in the diagram. Temperatures were recorded on site. Refer to Figure 2 for a heat entry profile for a personnel door.\n\nHeat is gained through the uninsulated gaps in the personnel doors, and through the high speed door each time it opens. Replacing insulation would save an estimated 2 MMBtu per year, with an annual savings of $33. Reducing high speed door open time by 20% would save an estimated 12 MMBtu per year, with $96 in savings annually. This yields a combined simple payback period of approximately 0.3 years based on an estimated implementation cost of $41 for door insulation. This proposal was not investigated further due to low cost savings. \n', 18),
(7, 12092, 'The facility has two 72 sq. ft. truck delivery doors that accommodate, on average, 2 deliveries per\nday. One truck model used is too small for the dock doors and leaves a gap between the truck and the\nproduction room wall, allowing outside air into the production space. The smaller truck represents\nless than 10% of all deliveries. The gap is approximately 25% of the total area of the door. The\ntemperature immediately inside the facility near the doors in question was 75 °F compared to 60 °F\nfurther into the facility, demonstrating heat transfer into the facility. Using an atmospheric\ntemperature of 75 °F and a target temperature of 60 °F, heat gain was calculated to be 52.75 kWh per\nyear. A 25% efficiency is assumed for the heat removal process, resulting in 211 kWh per year of\nenergy required to remove this heat. The operation time was estimated using 6.5 work days per week,\n52 work weeks per year, and 1 hour per delivery. An incremental electricity cost of $0.054/ kWh was\nused to estimate an annual savings of $11.00. The easiest method of eliminating heat entry is opening\nthe door to fit the truck size rather than completely opening the door for every delivery. This was not\nexplored as a recommendation due to its low savings.', 9),
(8, 12154, 'During the facility visit analysts observed insulation on supply steam piping in the main boiler room, but some sections of piping and valves were uninsulated. Photos were taken using a Fluke thermal imager to quantify the heat loss in the valves. These images indicate an approximate temperature of the exposed pipes and valves, and a more accurate temperature was measured with a digital contact thermometer. The uninsulated pipe temperature was measured at 272 °F with a contact thermometer. There are seven uninsulated valves total. Two of them have uninsulated lengths of two feet with average diameters of 14 inches. Four valves have uninsulated lengths of 40 inches and average diameters of 10 inches. One valve had an uninsulated length of one foot and an average diameter of 9 inches. Significant heat energy is lost from un-insulated steam supply pipes. Analysts recommend insulating the sections of piping that are currently uncovered. Estimated energy savings is 274.5 MMBtu/yr with annual cost savings of $925. Different sized insulation blankets were priced using Grainger and many options are available for insulation. The blankets needed to cover these valves would add up to a price of approximately $3,000 resulting in a 3.2 year simple payback. This was not considered as a full recommendation due to the low cost savings.', 9),
(10, 12170, 'The evaporative coils in the refrigeration cold rooms are currently set on a manual defrost schedule. During the site assessment, refrigeration technicians informed analysts that these coils are manually defrosted approximately every eight to nine hours. Analysis of logged data indicates that defrost intervals usually range from six to nine hour intervals. The refrigeration technicians informed analysts that the automatic defrost controls do not work effectively, and manual controls have proven more effective.\n\nDefrosting evaporative coils in a cold room is necessary due to frost build up on the coils. Frost limits airflow over the coils, reducing cooling effectiveness. Defrosting too frequently introduces an unnecessary amount of heat into the cold room, wasting energy. Defrosting too infrequently results in frost build up on the coils, limiting cooling effectiveness. Automatic defrost controls set by the manufacturer potentially use the most effective defrost schedule, if correctly designed. However, some automatic defrost schedules are not designed optimally and result in frost build up. \n\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation due to the difficulty of identifying the optimum defrost schedule. The original refrigeration vendor may have more helpful information on why the current automatic controls do not work effectively. Automatic controls would have the benefits of optimizing the defrost schedule, saving energy, and reducing manpower required to operate the refrigeration system.\n\nAuthored by: Josh Mutch\nReviewed by: Tyler Gidding\n', 13),
(13, 12116, 'In the South Stage Refrigeration System there are two 100 hp compressors located in Compressor Room 2; both compressors have liquid injection cooling. These compressors only operate six months out of the year, from August to January. Facility personnel informed analysts that the first six weeks of operation the compressors operate at near full capacity, drawing up to 200 amps per compressor. The remainder of the operating time the compressors operate closer to half capacity.\nSouth Stage Room 2\'s compressors use liquid-injection oil cooling. Liquid refrigerant is expanded to a vapor that is injected into side ports in the compressor housing. The injected liquid cools both the oil and the refrigerant being compressed. Liquid-injection cooling usually requires 5-15% more power to recompress the additional refrigerant vapor.\nThermosyphon oil cooling involves the use of a heat exchanger (oil cooler) and a cooling medium, such as liquid ammonia. The lubricating oil of the compressor is cooled in the heat exchanger by boiling liquid ammonia into gas. Thermosyphon oil cooling is beneficial because there are no power or capacity penalties to compressor performance as with the existing liquid injection systems. It also allows compressors to operate more efficiently with lower discharge pressures because a higher system pressure is no longer required to inject cooling liquid.\nIn typical thermosyphon operation, high pressure liquid ammonia flows from the condenser to a receiver. The receiver must be elevated above the heat exchanger so that the weight of the ammonia column is sufficient to overcome the pressure drop through pipes, valves, and the heat exchanger tubes. Gravity causes liquid ammonia to flow to the heat exchanger where it begins to vaporize, thus removing the heat from the oil. Liquid from the receiver displaces the lighter vapor which rises from the heat exchanger to the receiver. The vapor within the receiver returns to the condenser to complete the loop.\nThe following diagram depicts a thermosyphon oil cooling system. With a two stage system this diagram would be consistent with thermosyphon oil cooling on the high stage compressors. A datalogger was set up to collect amperage readings for a week of operation off of Compressor 2. Using this data and data off the compressor’s motor nameplate analysts performed a motor analysis found in the Site Data section of this report. Average electrical input power was found to be 12.4 kW. Assuming that this data was measured at low production analysts calculated yearly energy consumption to be 111,974 kWh.\nThe power penalty associated with liquid injection oil-cooling systems cannot be precisely determined. It is estimated that 5-15% of compressor power is lost to the liquid injection system. This percentage is based on the compression ratio. Analysts estimated a percentage of 10%, which resulted in an annual energy savings of 11,197 kWh and annual cost savings of $656. The cost to retrofit existing refrigeration compressors with thermosyphon oil coolers ranges from $13,000 to $15,000 per unit [1]. This measure was not considered a recommendation because of low cost savings compared to implementation costs.', 13),
(14, 12112, 'The product leaving the main production area is near 200°F and needs to be reduced to 40°F as quickly as possible for food safety reasons. The current process employs a spiral chiller where the product is moved through a conveyor system as cooling water (near 32°F) is pumped up to the ceiling and rained down at a rate of 3200 gpm over the product. Facility personnel informed analysts that the current process takes 3 hours to cool the product to desired temperatures and is considered a production bottleneck. Personnel estimated the labor costs to be $300 per production line per hour. The facility runs 20 hours per day, 3 days per week in the summer (off-peak) and 24 hours per day, 5 days per week during the winter (peak). Based on these figures, reducing the cooling time by 1 hour could result in an estimated annual savings of $1,404,000 per production line. Scaling this estimate, decreasing the current 3 hour (180 minutes) cooling time by 10% (18 minutes) results in an annual estimated savings of $421,000.\n\nFacility personnel asked analysts to consider the possibility of adding a solute such as ethanol or salt to reduce the freezing temperature of the cooling water solution below 32°F in order to increase the temperature difference and expedite the cooling process. After performing extensive research on the topic, it was determined that the corrosion, flammability and safety issues associated with solute-induced freezing point depression were too numerous to make an effective assessment recommendation.\n\nFurthermore, analysts considered the possibility of increasing the contact time and contact area between the cooling fluid and the hot product. Currently, only a small percentage of cooling fluid being pumped into the chamber is in direct contact with the product at any given time. This water is recycled through the cooling water system and pumped back up to the ceiling. When the product enters the chilling room, the difference in temperature between it and the cooling fluid is at a maximum. Reengineering the system to include jets that spray cooling water at the product and have sections along the conveyor system that would immerse the product in a flowing stream of cooling fluid could potentially reduce cooling time. Cutting the cooling time by 5% (9 minutes) could save $210,500 annually based on the aforementioned figures provided by facility personnel.', 18),
(16, 12151, 'Combined heat and power (CHP) systems use a fuel source to generate electricity and use the waste heat in the process. CHP systems use some form of thermodynamic engine to convert chemical energy into physical work, which is then converted into electricity.\nCHP systems typically use the following engines:\n\n   Reciprocating engines - Typical internal combustion engines attached to a generator.\n   Turbines or Micro-Turbines - Gas turbine engines with a compressor and turbine attached to a generator.\n   Fuel Cells - Directly converts chemical energy to electricity.\n   Rankine Cycles - Burns fuel to generate steam in a closed cycle system which drives a turbine attached to a generator.\n\nSizes for reciprocating engines range between 1 kW and 10 MW. Most of these technologies are configured to use natural gas as its main fuel.\n\nProduction of biogas through anaerobic digestion is an alternative to the disposal of fruit scrap. In this process organic waste is fermented by anaerobic bacteria, producing gases with 55-65% methane and 35-45% carbon dioxide. These gases can then be collected and burned in a reciprocating engine. Close to 40% of the energy equivalent of these gases is used to produce electricity and nearly 50% is recovered and used as a heat source, achieving high values of efficiency.\n\nNormally the cost for a 1 MW plant, including shipping, external cooling radiator, project management, pre-engineering & deployment/startup support services, documentation, and warranty will exceed one million dollars. This does not include the required equipment for the production of biogas. \n\nThis was not considered as a full recommendation because the payback would be too long.', 19),
(17, 12148, 'The facility has six 15,000 gallon aeration tanks. Three of the tanks have aeration blowers to raise dissolved oxygen (DO) levels and reduce biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) levels. There are no DO or BOD sensors in place, so the aeration blowers are running 8,760 hours per year. Facility personnel did not provide a minimum DO level trying to be achieved, but they are charged based on BOD levels. The city charges based on the BOD levels of the water that is sent to the city\'s water reclamation facility.\n\nIf the facility installed DO and BOD sensors, they would be able to monitor the levels and minimize the operation hours of the aeration blowers based on their needs. This would lead to electrical consumption savings. The DO and BOD levels would be stabilized and changed based on the time of the year, which would lead to increased energy savings in the off season.\n\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable to analyze savings. Measurements of DO or BOD levels would be required to perform further analysis and calculate possible cost savings.', 16),
(18, 12149, 'The facility has an aeration blower system that spans across three 15,000 gallon tanks which holds wastewater before releasing it into the city’s sewer system. The wastewater needs to be oxygenated before disposal to remove as much biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) as possible. Currently a 5 hp motor is used to power the aeration blowers. According to facility personnel, each tank blower has a main head and then splits off into 12 different 2 inch diameter pipes to disperse air within the tank.\nThe aeration blowers are currently running 8,760 hours per year, but facility personnel do not currently measure the dissolved oxygen levels consistently. A single 5 hp motor is small for blowing air into three large tanks, which means the facility could potentially reduce operation hours and BOD levels by using a larger motor for the aeration blowers.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the dissolved oxygen and BOD levels are not observed on a consistent basis. The efficiency of the single motor to oxygenate the water was not fully analyzed for this facility. A 5 hp motor is undersized for that volume of water to aerate, based on analyst experience.', 16),
(19, 12143, 'The facility has five high speed doors installed throughout the cold rooms to store product. Facility personnel informed analysts that these doors have a history of frequent malfunction, and that they were fixed the day before the assessment. The refrigeration technician informed analysts that when the high speed doors malfunction, significant energy is lost to maintain temperature in the cold rooms. The technician is able to see the strain on the refrigeration system from these doors being propped open while employees are moving product through. The dimensions of a door were determined to be 16 ft. wide and 20 ft. tall with a tape measure. To determine the volume exchange when the door is open, an Alnor Velometer was used to collect air flow speeds at several points of the door. This is shown in the Figure 1 below. Air curtains are used in a wide variety of applications to create distinct environments through an opening, allowing traffic to flow unobstructed. High velocity air is directed across the opening creating an air shield by a fan and nozzle system. The high air velocity provides resistance to unwanted air or particles, the penetration of outside air due to wind, and also the invasion of insects.\nWhen used as thermal barriers, air curtains prevent the natural flow of air caused by temperature differences. The temperature difference between the outside and inside temperature creates a difference in density, therefore a pressure variation causes the infiltration of outside air. With an air curtain, the high velocity air deflects the natural convection air flow, and significantly reduces the exchange in conditioned and non-conditioned air [1]. The application would include installing a 16 foot long air curtain with high speed door compatibility on each of the high speed refrigeration doors. Configuring the air curtains to turn on and off with their respective high speed door. Implementation would save 10,422 kWh per year. Energy savings are calculated assuming air curtains are 30% effective at reducing volume exchange and assuming a refrigerated COP of 3. In some case studies, air curtains have been customized to be as high as 70% effective. However, most curtain configurations are found to be only 30% effective. The annual cost savings were estimated at $3,372 after an implementation cost of $35,000. This would result in a simple payback period of 10.4 years. Due to the length of the simple payback period this was not considered a fully developed recommendation.', 13),
(21, 12146, 'During the site visit facility personnel expressed interest in analysts looking into the facility’s compressed air leaks. The compressed air system is primarily served by two 75 hp Gardner Denver air compressors that alternate operation. There were several areas in the facility that have significant leaks, especially in one of the control rooms where leaks were clearly audible.\nAir leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them, also people often assume that compressed air is cheap. Many leaks occur due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. The term leak load refers to the minimum sustained air demand, which is assumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks throughout the facility to achieve a leak load equal to 30% of peak, non-leak plant demand can be a reasonable objective (i.e. the air demand for feeding leaks is only 30% of plant peak demand, excluding leaks). In addition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\nA datalogger was installed to collect amperage measurements on one of the compressors for a week. Using the data recorded a compressed air analysis was performed to assess compressor operation. Assessing air leaks requires the compressor be running while no pneumatic equipment is running in order to capture the airflow serving leaks in the system. After receiving the dataloggers analysts were unable to identify a period of time where the air compressor was serving just air leaks. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable to obtain enough information to accurately calculate the savings.\nBased on the leaks analysts observed during the facility visit, the current minimum sustained air flow is above 30% of the peak, non-leak plant demand. A leak load percentage of 60% could result in savings of up to $5,000. As a standard maintenance procedure, it is assumed that on average the cost to repair a leak will be equal to the associated annual savings.\nUtility incentives are generally not available for fixing air leaks as this is considered a maintenance project.\nReducing air leaks is an iterative process. A compressed air system with few leaks can become leak-ridden if efforts are not continuously made to keep leaks to a minimum.\nAn ultrasonic leak detector can assist in locating leaks in noisy environments. Utility providers may allow customers to borrow an ultrasonic leak detector. If a vendor provides leak detection services, be sure that they document all leak locations rather than merely tagging them.', 3),
(22, 12150, 'The facility currently operates two air compressors in a room shared with one of the boilers. They are both Gardner Denver 75 hp compressors and operate one at a time. The boiler is a 400 hp Keeler water tube boiler serving the facility’s steam needs including the steam turbine driven pump and steam treatment for fruit.\nDuring the assessment analysts recommended using the heat produced by the air compressors to heat the feed water for the boilers. It is assumed that the boilers and compressors operate simultaneously during the facility’s annual operation hours. It is estimated that the heat output of the compressor is 70% of the energy input and 50% of that heat is feasibly recoverable with a heat exchanger. Analysts estimate a savings of approximately 119,000 kWh or 406 MMBtu. Recovering this energy would reduce the natural gas energy demand and result in savings of about $1,900. A heat exchanger and plumbing would have to be installed to capture the heat released by the air compressor. Analysts estimate that the combined implementation cost for both compressors would be over $10,000. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because of the long simple payback period of over 5 years.', 7),
(24, 12145, 'The facility has a line dedicated to cooling and packaging jars of their product. The jars are sent through a high powered blower and packaged at the end of the line. During the facility assessment, Line 5 was running all day, but there was no product moving on the line. The operation hours are approximately 1920 hours per year, during which approximately 560 hours the Line 5 blower is running without product.\nElectrical consumption could be saved if the facility shut off the line blower during the down time. The analysis on this line resulted in $350 annual savings with a $0 implementation cost. Savings are based on turning the blower off. There may be further savings by reducing the conveyor operation as well, but they were not analyzed.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because of the low cost savings.', 18),
(25, 12378, 'The facility uses hot water for cleaning and other uses in various parts of the facility. The hot water is heated by heat exchangers found on the boiler stacks, and facility personnel informed analysts that all the hot water is used during the cleanup shift. Analysts identified various heat sources that could be used to help generate hot water. Two viable heat sources include the exhaust heat from the air compressors and the exhaust heat from the hydraulic systems. Analysts collected data on both the air compressors and hydraulic systems. Assuming 70% of the energy input for the air compressors is rejected as waste heat, and 40% of the energy input for the hydraulic systems is rejected as waste heat, analysts calculate 0.38 MMBtu per hour of heat rejection. Assuming only half of this heat energy can be captured, there would be approximately 0.2 MMBtu per hour of usable heat, or approximately $6,688 per year in energy savings. A vendor quote informed analysts that it would cost at least $30,000 to retrofit the air compressors with a water cooled system. This quote did not include additional costs of retrofitting the hydraulic systems with a heat exchanger. Due to the high implementation cost and low cost savings, this recommendation was not analyzed further.', 1),
(26, 12307, 'The facility uses a 450 hp blower fan to blow chips and sawdust to storage for use as hog fuel in the powerhouse facility. Facility personnel informed analysts that the fan is clean-side, has radial tip fan blades, and operates 24 hours, 5.5 days per week. Clean-side fans operate downstream of the baghouse where no particulates flow. They use negative pressure to pull particles from the plant to their storage destination. There are six types of centrifugal fan blades commonly used in fan systems. These blade types, efficiencies and characteristics are listed below. Increasing the static efficiency of a system decreases the motor input power by approximately the ratio of the current static efficiency over the proposed static efficiency. Analysts considered installing airfoil blades in the current blower fan system. This was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable obtain the fan vendor quotes for implementation costs and were unable to identify a proposed system to calculate savings.', 4),
(27, 12325, 'The plywood facilities have a combined total of 43 air conditioning (AC) units. Facility personnel informed analysts that the AC units operate continously during the year. The facility provided analysts with a map of the AC unit locations and a detailed list of AC units with sizes based on ounces of refrigerant required. Some AC units are needed in motor control rooms to protect the equipment, while others are located in conference rooms and employee offices.\nAnalysts considered recommending turning off AC units around the facility and using outside air during the colder months, if needed. This would reduce the total energy consumption of the AC units. Analysts did not develop this into a full recommendation because thet were unable to quantify the energy consumption of each unit and the difference in electrical demand of the units from warmer months to colder months.', 19),
(28, 12395, 'The facility uses a 5.1 MMBtu Cleaver Brooks boiler to produce steam for the smokehouses and the retort. Currently, the 5 hp forced draft fan uses a variable frequency drive (VFD) to control air flow. The VFD is controlled by the firing rate of the boiler. The boiler is on standby for approximately half of its operation time and sits at medium fire or high fire for the other half of its operation time. Combustion measurements were taken during the assessment and it was found that the boiler runs at approximately 83.2% efficiency.\nInstalling an O2 sensor to control the fan speed will save energy in two ways. First, it would control the inlet flow of air to allow the boiler to burn all of the fuel without sending unnecessary excess heat out of the stack. This increases combustion efficiency and will save money by reducing natural gas use. Second, the controls on the fan will slow the fan speed down reducing electrical consumption.\nThis measure did not appear as a full recommendation because of low potential cost savings. There is little room for improvement since the current controls on the boiler allow it to run at a high efficiency.', 1),
(29, 12388, 'The facility uses two separate hydraulic systems to power machinery on the process floor. The larger\nhydraulic system consists of five 40 hp motors, one of which is controlled with a variable frequency\ndrive (VFD). The unneeded motors shut down during periods of low demand, and the trim motor\n(VFD) helps meet the load. The smaller hydraulic system consists of two 50 hp motors. These motors\nrun simultaneously to meet demand without any additional control strategy. The measure being\nconsidered is to replicate the current control strategy of the larger hydraulic system on to the small\nhydraulic system.\nData loggers were placed on the large hydraulic system’s VFD controlled motor, and one of the small\nhydraulic system’s motors. When analyzing the resulting data logs there were few differences in the\namperage patterns between the two systems. Furthermore, no data was collected on the motors in the\nlarge hydraulics system that are not on VFDs.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts could not obtain data\non the non-VFD motors in the large hydraulic system.', 0),
(31, 12141, 'The facility currently uses three McCormick screw compressors with slide valve controls in Engine Room 1. The compressors are 75, 150, and 300 hp. The condenser system in Engine Room 1 is composed of two evaporative cooling towers: A small Baltimore Air Coil cooling tower with one 10 hp fan, and a large tower with three fans. The large tower has a 15 hp motor mounted on one fan, and a 25 hp motor double mounted on the other fans. The fan on the small tower previously had a VFD installed, but is currently non-functioning and operates at full speed. The refrigeration technician indicated that the condenser cooling towers likely have the capacity to maintain adequate cooling even with lowering the compressor head pressure.\nThe discharge pressure control panel indicated a discharge pressure of 136 psig. The approach temperature was calculated at 6.6 °F. During the assessment, analysts used a sling psychrometer to measure the wet bulb at 72 °F and dry bulb temperature at 100 °F.\nThe minimum discharge pressure setting was not determined by analysts. A setpoint of 125 psig was used for an analysis. If the facility reduces the minimum setpoint on the compressors from 125 psig to 105 psig, it will save 26,500 kWh annually, resulting in an annual cost savings of $1,500 with an immediate payback. This was not written as a full recommendation because analysts were unable to determine the actual minimum discharge pressure setpoint. A full analysis is shown in the following pages.', 13),
(32, 12102, 'The facility currently has two Atlas Copco air compressors that alternate in use. The compressors are 215 hp and are Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) controlled. The compressors are water cooled and a heat exchanger attached to the hot water heats a nearby rotary drum dryer. Compressed air discharge pressure is currently 115 psig.\n\nThe compressors are set to this high pressure in order to produce enough heat to operate the drum dryers. Higher pressures also result in higher electrical consumption. Reducing the air pressure from 115 to 91 psig would save approximately $2,500 annually. Other measures must occur to replace the lost heat from this pressure reduction. \n\nOne option is to install an external heating mechanism. Waste heat from nearby processes can be captured and used for the current dryer. Electrical energy can also be used to power a heating device. Two mechanisms would be required since each compressor has its own dryer. The author did not have adequate data to explore this option further.\n\nA second option would be to replace the drum dryers with a different dryer type. Consolidating both compressors onto one dryer would also reduce costs. Savings of $2,500 with an implementation cost of $15,000 results in a 6 year simple payback. Operation costs will decrease due to the reducing two dryers to one, therefore the estimated savings is a conservative estimate. \n\nIt is recommended that air pressure be decreased from 115 to 91 psig if possible. This was not developed into a full recommendation due to moderate cost savings and not enough data available to the author.\n', 3),
(33, 12101, 'The facility has two 215 hp oil-free dry air compressors labeled PL 3177 (Compressor #1) and PL 3188 (Compressor #2). Both compressors are Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) controlled and alternate operation. The system pressure is maintained at 115 psig.\n\nDataloggers were installed to collect amperage measurements on both compressors for one week at 16 second intervals. Based on the data from the dataloggers when no air is being used for operations on site, an air leaks assessment was completed. The current percent of compressed air lost to leaks, or leak load, was calculated by observing average air flow over the datalogged period and picking a low point, where the compressors are assumed to be serving only leaks. A current leak load of 20 acfm (equating to 4%) was calculated, meaning that the amount of air being lost to leaks is 4% of the air being used effectively. For this facility, a leak load of 20% would ordinarily be proposed. This system is already operates below the proposed leak load.\n\nAir leaks are often neglected due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. Also, people often assume that compressed air is cheap. Many air leaks occur due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. Reducing air leaks is an iterative process. A compressed air system with few leaks can become leak-ridden if efforts are not continuously made to keep leaks to a minimum. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic, but energy savings are available by reducing air leaks by any amount. In addition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation due to the low current leak load.\n', 3),
(35, 12544, 'The facility currently has several compressed air nozzles located throughout the plant used for blowing saw dust off equipment during operation and cleaning shifts. The pressure was measured at 82 and 85 psig at two end use locations, the saw mill stacker and the quad shack, respectively.\nAnalysts were unable to obtain the information needed to analyze this process, but utilizing general best practices may lead to energy savings due to reducing the total amount of produced compressed air.\nInstalling a compressed air receiver tank will reduce the compressor’s need to cycle or will allow the compressor to run at a lower load. This will allow the storage of compressed air until it is demanded, rather than producing the air as needed. A typical compressor control system will measure the system pressure, and stop the compressor from producing compressed air when it reaches a given set point. Any demand for compressed air is then provided by the receiver until the pressure is reduced, which turns the compressor back on. Reducing the amount of compressor cycling will reduce energy consumption by limiting the amount of inrush current, instantaneously every time the compressor needs to turn on and produce compressed air. A receiver tank can also reduce humidity content by draining condensation. The number and size of receivers will depend on the facility’s compressed air demand, but a general rule of thumb is 4 gallons of receiver volume per every cfm of compressed air required by the process.\nUtilizing engineered air nozzles can also reduce compressed air use given that they provide the same air velocity as an open hose, but using less volume of air. Another benefit is the ability to control the amount of air used with precision quick release grips.\nRefer to the compressed air appendix at the end of this report for a more detailed list of common general practices.', 3),
(36, 11833, 'The facility has a building known as the “Old Power House”. This building contains many lights that are on all day even though traffic through this area is minimal. Facility personnel explained that there are air compressors that are in this building, as well as a few other reasons why lighting needs to be in the building for facility personnel. However, there is no control scheme, and the lighting in this building has not been updated as much as the rest of the facility. It contains 41 sodium vapor lights, 8 metal halide lights, and 16 T12 fixtures. Because these are on all day, these lights consume about 139,000 kWh annually, which costs $5,100. The facility could save energy if these lights were turned off, or were able to decommission the majority of lights and only keep on what is needed for the personnel that use the building. This was not written as a full recommendation because of the lack of interest from the facility for a project in this area, low cost savings, and lack of knowledge for which lights would have to remain in operation.\nAuthored by: Miles Naughton\nReviewed by: Brad Moore', 11833),
(37, 11821, 'The facility currently uses two hydraulic power units to operate two pumps in the digester. The two hydraulic motors operate simultaneously. One was recently replaced and the second is older. Live amperage from both motors were obtained from the facility’s data collection system and showed that the motors operated at 40 and 30 A. The facility also provided amperage data for a year from the hydraulic power units, as shown in Tracie Tran’s Site Report.\nHydraulics are typically used to perform tasks that require high torque at low speeds. Hydraulic motors are driven by a pump that is driven by an electric motor. Hydraulic systems lose a significantportion of energy through heating of fluid and conversion losses. Systems that only use electric motors do not experience the same types of energy losses. Combined with a gearbox, electric motors can achieve the same torques and speeds as hydraulic motors. Hydraulic systems also typically maintain a constant base operating pressure, but electric motors can be turned off whenever they are not in use, allowing for additional savings. Hydraulics typically require higher maintenance costs compared to electric motors.\nAn average amperage for the last 4 months was used and it was determined that the motors operate at 30 and 40 A, which results in approximately 280,000 kWh/year. It is estimated that electric motors are 93% efficient while a hydraulic power unit is 36% efficient. Converting from hydraulic to electric would result in a $23,000 savings per year in electricity costs. Analysts were unable to obtain maintenance savings from converting but estimate it to be approximately $3,000 per year from prior experience. Analysts also recommend investigating if the facility can downsize to a smaller electric motor (35-40 hp) as it would achieve the same work but allow better part-load efficiency if sized correctly. Costs for installing and purchasing two 50 hp electric motors are estimated to be $15,000 resulting in a 0.65 year payback period.\nA full analysis was not done on this recommendation because of the relatively low savings and the unknown maintenance costs.\nAuthored by: Tracie Tran\nReviewed by: Scott Lindbloom', 8),
(38, 11830, 'Capture Dissolving Tank Waste Heat\nBlack liquor is burned in the Power Boiler to generate steam. The waste product is a molten salt solution that requires further treatment. Using direct-injected steam jets, this molten metal is broken into droplets where it forms “green liquor”. Because of the extreme temperature of the wash and steam injected into the wash, a large mass of vapor is evaporated through a stack. Analysts considered capturing this vapor and condensing it to capture energy. There are new energy generation products that can utilize waste heat to heat a hot water loop, which feeds an Organic Rankine Cycle (ORC). The ORC is depicted in the figure below. Hot water feeds into an evaporator (1), which evaporates the working fluid. The superheated vapor of the working fluid then feeds into a turbine (2) which powers a generator. The fluid is then cooled using a cold water loop and condenser (3), and the process starts over. A pump is used to push the fluid in the process (4). Figure 1: Organic Rankine Cycle. Shows hot water loop supplying energy to the working fluid, which then powers a turbine/generator. The working fluid must then condensed using a cooling water loop.\nThe current ORC technologies that exist need hot water of at least 170°F to operate. This would likely require the current waste vapor to be passed through a preliminary heat exchanger. This would be barely viable given that the measured temperature of the vapor leaving the dissolving tank exhaust is about 170°F. Power generated would range between 10 kW and 20 kW, resulting in energy production of between 86,000 kWh and 172,000 kWh annually. If this energy was used to support existing systems, this would represent between $3,000 and $6,000 in annual energy savings. This was no written as a full recommendation because analysts are not confident in the viability of the waste heat stream leaving the dissolving tank. Implementation would also be difficult, as the facility would have to install a water system with heat exchanger, controls, and the packaged ORC machine which can cost well over $100,000 in capital expenditures.\nAuthored by: Miles Naughton\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 11830);
INSERT INTO `OMCs` (`OMCId`, `TaskId`, `OMCText`, `SubjectId`) VALUES
(39, 11827, 'Optimize Air Dryer System\nThe facility has a desiccant air dryer attached to the Joy centrifugal air compressor. The Joy compressor is 600 hp and is used for base loading the air system. This compressor operates 99% of the time annually. The compressor’s specific power consumption is 0.22 kW/SCFM, which is derived from the facility’s data collection system. The dryer is heatless and has energy efficient purge controls, which may not be functioning properly according to the maintenance vendor. Also, the maintenance vendor told analysts that the dryers purge orifice was reduced from 3,600 SCFM to 1,100 SCFM. Facility personnel said they require a dew point of -40 °F in the dryer but have a faulty sensor and have no way of measuring the moisture level.\nRegenerative desiccant dryers blow dry compressed air across the desiccant bed to regenerate it, and then purge the air and moisture to the atmosphere. Some models heat the dried air first to increase its capacity to absorb moisture. Heated dryers have better efficiencies because the energy required to heat the air to 300 °F is less than the energy required to compress and dry the additional air that would otherwise be necessary to dry the bed. Heated air dryers require only about 1-7% of the total compressed air to purge the desiccant bed, while non-heated dryers require 15% or more. There are three typical methods of controlling dryer purging: time, temperature, and moisture. Time-based controls will terminate regenerative purging after a specified time elapses. Temperature controls will terminate purging when a specific temperature of the purged air is reached. The most efficient strategy is moisture control, which is also referred to as \"energy efficient controls\". This includes a sensor that detects moisture content in the purged air and terminates the purge cycle when an optimal moisture level is achieved.\nAnalysts considered installing a heated blower purge dryer (HBPD) in place of the current air dryer system. A HBPD is the most efficient form of air drying. These systems use a dedicated blower and external heater to produce purge air for desiccant regeneration. The blower allows for atmospheric air to flow through the heater and then through the desiccant bed. Altogether, this system eliminates use of compressed air for purging. Compressed air is 90% inefficient because most energy put into it escapes as heat, making it a valuable energy source once created.\nFigure 1: HBPD Desiccant Regeneration Cycle\nAnalysts assumed the energy efficient purge controls are not operational and the purge air flow is 15% of the rated air flow. The annual energy consumption for the current system is estimated at 314,808 kWh. The maintenance vendor quoted analysts $55,766 for a HBPD rated at 1,200 SCFM, 12 kW heater, and 7.5 hp blower not including shipping or installation. The proposed annual energy consumption would be 256,703 kWh, reducing energy consumption by 58,105 kWh and saving an estimated $2,187 based on the facility’s incremental electricity cost calculated in the Utility Analysis of this report. The simple payback period would be 25.5 years. This was not written as a full recommendation because of the small savings and long payback period associated with this system.\nAnalysts also considered increasing the dew point temperature to -40 °F. The maintenance vendor suggested that the dew point temperature is presumably much lower than needed and the facility was unaware of the current dew point temperature. When the dew point temperature is lower than necessary it causes the system to work more frequently and purge more compressed air. Fixing the moisture sensor and energy efficient purge controls will allow the dryer to work only as much as necessary. Analysts did not write this as a full recommendation because there is no reliable dew point temperature measurement to base an analysis on.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 11827),
(40, 11861, 'Install Ultrafiltration (UF) for Pre-Concentration of Black Liquor\nThe facility generates black liquor from the pulping process. This liquor contains organic compounds such as lignin and carbohydrates that when concentrated enough (>60%) can allow the liquor to burn similar to heavy oil. When the black liquor exits the digester (pulping process), it typically has a solids concentration of 15-17%. This solution is then sent to evaporators, where steam is used to evaporate moisture from the solution resulting in the black liquor having enough concentrated solids to combust, generating steam and power [1]. Data that facility personnel provided for one evaporator line shows that entering concentrations of black liquor average 22% solids. After exiting the evaporator, black liquor entering the Power Boiler averages 53% solids. Facility personnel estimated that the overall evaporative process uses 2.25 MW.\nUltrafiltration is a less energetic concentration method where porous membranes with specific filter areas and pore diameters are chosen to filter solutions containing molecules of certain molecular weights and diameters. Ultrafiltration relies on a constant pressure gradient to drive flux across this porous membrane, which is typically composed of polymer fibers. Due to the corrosive nature of black liquor, ceramic filter types are used.\nNew technologies in ultrafiltration allow for black liquor to be concentrated to higher intermediate solid contents. Installing an ultrafiltration system in conjunction with each evaporator system would allow black liquor concentration to reach levels of 35% before entering the evaporators [2]. This would allow less power to be used in the evaporator system, potentially saving $95,000 annually in energy costs across all evaporative systems. It would also allow the black liquor concentrations to reach higher concentrations which result in better combustion efficiencies, resulting in improved power generation.\nThis was not written as a full recommendation due to the prohibitively high capital costs of current technology and unavailability of this technology industrially today. While several new technologies exist, there seem to be no commercially implemented technologies. Capital expenditures for new ceramic ultrafiltration units are estimated at $180/ft2, which amounts to a total expense of approximately $130,000 for each evaporative system. There are also pumps, re-piping, controls, and general construction expenses that aren’t included in this estimate. There is currently research being accomplished to provide better low-cost alternatives for these membranes [3].\n[1] IEA Bioenergy. Black Liquor Gasification. <http://www.ieabioenergy.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/5608.pdf>\n[2] New Logic International, Inc. Black Liquor Treatment for Pulp Mills. <http://www.vsep.com/pdf/BlackLiquor.pdf>\n[3] U.S. DOE Advanced Manufacturing Office. Sacrificial Protective Coating Materials That Can Be Regenerated In-Situ to Enable High-Performance Membranes.\nAuthored by: Miles Naughton\nReviewed by: Tanida Chongvilaiwan', 11861),
(41, 11862, 'Install Extraction Port on Turbine\nThe facility burns both black liquor and natural gas to generate high-pressure steam that powers a turbine to generate electricity. This high pressure steam is at 850 psig. The facility also requires intermediate (600 psig) and low (50-150 psig) pressure steam. The intermediate steam is used primarily for soot blowers and other components in the facility. Currently, the intermediate steam is generated by taking a 30,000 lb/hr bypass from the 850 psig line before the turbine and passing it through a pressure reducing valve (PRV) to the lower pressure header. If an intermediate steam pressure withdrawal port were installed on the second stage of the turbine the facility would gain electrical generation from an additional 30,000 lb/hr of high pressure steam through the turbine. The facility would also still have functioning intermediate pressure steam. A steam generation calculator is shown below [1]. By assuming generator and isentropic efficiencies, analysts were able to estimate the amount of additional power generation if this were implemented.\nAdditional power generated amounts to approximately 360 kW. This amounts to an additional 3.1 mega-watthours of annual energy production. If the facility is receiving $0.025 for every kWh produced, this represents $77,000 in annual energy revenue.\nThere was not enough information about the system to complete a detailed implementation analysis. Multiple case studies of rebuilt generators were found to estimate the costs of a project this large. Analysts assumed similar project costs compared to a study where a turbine of a similar size was rebuilt [2]. Implementation costs could be as much as $3,400,000; which would results in over a 44 year payback; however actual costs are really unknown and it could be worth getting an estimate from your vendor. Incentives in many cases can equal up to 50% of project costs, which still leave the payback period of this project at 22 years. This was not written as a full recommendation due to the anticipated high payback period associated with this project.\n[1] U.S. Department of Energy. Steam Calculators. <https://www5.eere.energy.gov/manufacturing/tech_deployment/amo_steam_tool/equipTurbine>\n[2] U.S. Department of Energy. Office of Industrial Technologies. Rebuilding Steam Turbine Generator Reduces Costs At a Steel Mill. <https://www1.eere.energy.gov/manufacturing/tech_assistance/pdfs/betsteel.pdf>\nAuthored by: Miles Naughton\nReviewed by: Peter Kreider', 11862),
(42, 11839, 'Increase Pump Efficiency\nThe facility has two pumps in the east aeration sanitation basin (East ASB) that are used to pump water from the basin to the nearby river. The pumps are similar, operating with 50 horsepower motors having nameplate full load amps of 61 and 61.5 amps.\nDuring the site assessment a non-contact flow meter measurement showed these pumps to displace treated water out of the basin at rates of 960 gallons per minute and 2,000 gallons per minute. Other information such as an elevation change of 6.25 feet and pipe inlet and outlet diameters of 40 inches were collected using a tape measure on site. Soon after the visit, plant personnel provided an outlet pressure of 12 psig and a few weeks later provided pump motor amperages of 22.6 and 25.6 amps.\nPump efficiencies were calculated by assuming the pumps operated without variation over the time data was collected. However the results were not realistic (Calculated efficiency for one pump significantly exceeded 100%). This suggests that the pumps do not operate in a steady state as assumed, and that to effectively evaluate the pump efficiency, all measurements would need to be made over a short period in which operation does not change.\nCollected data does indicate a number of anomalies that could bear further investigation:\n• When amperage data was collected, the motors were operating at low load factors of 55.7% and 66.8%, at which motor efficiencies can be degraded. If the pumps spend significant time operating in these conditions, an alternative configuration could be called for to reduce annual operating cost.\n• Although the pumps are nearly identical, the flow rates they produced on the day of the assessment were very different. This suggests there is either some sort of line blockage disrupting water flow, a problem with one or both of the pumps, or a flow control system that we were unaware of. If the difference in flow is caused by a line blockage or pump issue, correction should improve operation and reduce operational energy costs. If the source of the difference is a control strategy, there may be an opportunity to revise it for more efficient operation (a control valve could be replaced with variable speed operation for instance).\nThis was not developed as a full recommendation due to inconsistencies in the data and results. To obtain clearer results flow rates, amperage and other data would need to be collected at the same time. If the operating conditions vary as much as the data suggests, information about the control mechanism and variation in operation over time would also be required.\nAuthored by: Megan O’Leary\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 11839),
(43, 11836, 'The amount of solar energy that strikes the earth every day is substantial. Photovoltaics use material that converts this energy into electricity at the atomic level. This is possible because of a material property known as the photoelectric effect, which allows the material to absorb photons of light and release electrons. These free electrons can then be captured, resulting in an electrical current that can be used as electricity. The resulting electrical current is Direct Current (DC), therefore an inverter must be used to convert the DC into Alternating Current (AC) before the electricity can be used.\nInstall a 223 kW photovoltaic array in the area identified in Figure 1. This will reduce associated annual energy consumption by 0.1%, saving 331,930 kWh annually and result in an annual cost savings of $12,490 for a net payback of 40.4 years after an implementation cost of $505,302.\nIncentives can be provided by both the State of Oregon and the Federal Government to cover up to 75% of the total cost of the project, leading to an approximate payback of 10 years after incentives.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation due to the long payback period.\nFigure 1: Image of facility obtained from Google Earth.\nAuthored by: Alberto Pezzani Silva\nReviewed by: Tanida Chongvilaiwan', 11836),
(44, 11841, 'Install VFD on Paper Machine Vacuum Pump\nThe facility uses a combination of gravity and vacuum to remove moisture from pulp in the Paper Machine. The vacuum is generated at different points in the Paper Machine using fans and a vacuum pump. Dampers are used to restrict airflow, controlling vacuum generation. Each fan and pump has its own main header, at which vacuum is controlled by a combination of a damper at the end of the line (secondary dampers) and a bypass damper (primary damper) to allow free airflow, reducing vacuum. Some headers have multiple lines off of them, each with different target vacuums that are maintained by secondary dampers. This analysis addresses only the vacuum control at the main headers, proposing to replace the primary dampers and bypasses with VFDs to maintain the same header vacuum. The secondary dampers will remain unchanged.\nA damper controls air flow by increasing flow resistance within the system. Partially closed dampers are inefficient because significant power is required to overcome the added resistance. The method of controlling pressure in this case is by pressure release valves that allow ambient airflow into the vacuum system to reduce vacuum. This is inefficient as it requires the fan or vacuum pump motor to move additional air mass.\nVariable Frequency Drives (VFDs) vary vacuum and fan motor speed instead of restricting airflow like a damper valve. When airflow can be reduced, the VFD reduces motor speed to produce the required airflow while using the least amount of energy. Likewise, it will increase motor speed as more airflow is demanded to increase vacuum. Savings are achieved by installing a VFD to reduce the electrical power required to operate the fan at specified settings. The power savings available by switching to a VFD controlled system are represented by general curves of percent loaded power versus percent loaded capacity.\nDuring the site assessment, facility personnel informed analysts that the fans and vacuum pump do not have VFDs. Vacuum pump and fan data were collected from facility personnel, including data logs and performance curves.\nBased on data collected, two vacuum systems were identified to have significant potential for energy savings: the PDM and PRI Bel Bond systems. The PDM system is powered by two 20 hp fans, with a primary bypass damper, and six secondary dampers at vacuum point of use. The bypass damper is meant to allow air flow to regulate (reduce) vacuum generation. This damper is constantly 100% open. The average header (bypass) vacuum was 23.8 inches water column. The average vacuum at the six secondary dampers increased incrementally at each from 4.5 to 17.7 inches water column. Performance curves for these fans were collected. Analysts were unable to confidently define the operation of these fans because the operating pressure appears to lead to an undefined point on the fan curves. Furthermore, it is difficult to estimate the amount of air flow through the bypass damper based on the data collected. This system only has a total of 40 connected horsepower, so energy savings may have been relatively small.\nThe PRI Bel Bond system is powered by a 400 hp vacuum pump. This system has one primary damper for bypassing air flow and three secondary dampers at the points of use. This primary damper is constantly 100% open. The average vacuum at the header (bypass) is 12.4 inches of mercury, while the vacuum at the points of use are seven inches of mercury. Analysts believe that the header vacuum could be maintained at eight inches of vacuum to maintain the same performance. This would be accomplished by eliminating the bypass damper and installing a VFD on the vacuum pump motor. Below (Figure 1) is a performance curve for this vacuum pump. This curve is based on one collected from the pump manufacturer, with vacuum units converted to inches of mercury.\nFigure 1. Performance Curve for Nash CL9001G Vacuum Pump\nAnalysts used the performance curve above (Figure 1) to develop an equation to translate vacuum data into power draw. Using the performance relationship, one year of vacuum readings, in one hour increments, was converted into instantaneous power values. These power values were multiplied by the time increment between readings to get energy values at each interval. The sum of all energy values equals the total energy consumption. This process was repeated assuming that vacuum at the header is maintained at eight inches of mercury, except when the current vacuum values are less than eight. Based on this analysis, annual cost savings are $500. The cost to install a VFD on the vacuum pump motor is expected to be $33,000, resulting in a 66 year payback period.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation due to uncertainty of cost savings on the PDM system and excessively long payback on the PRI system.\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez\n74', 11841),
(45, 11649, '1.	Reduce Compressed Air leaks\n\nThe facility uses two nearly identical 250 hp Ingersoll Rand variable frequency drive (VFD) air compressors. Their maximum rated pressure is 110 psig, and the current pressure set point is around 100 psig.\n\nDataloggers were installed to collect amperage measurements on both compressors for one week at 16 second intervals. Based on the data from the dataloggers, an air leaks assessment was completed. The current percent of compressed air lost to leaks, or leak load, was calculated by observing average air flow over the datalogged period and picking a low point, where the compressors are assumed to be serving only leaks. A current leak load of 150 acfm (equating to 13%) was calculated, meaning that the amount of air being lost to leaks is 13% of the air being used effectively. For this facility, a leak load of 30% would ordinarily be proposed. This system already operates below the proposed leak load.\n\nAir leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. Also people often assume that compressed air is cheap. Many leaks are due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. Reducing air leaks is an iterative process. A compressed air system with few leaks can become leak-ridden if efforts are not continuously made to keep leaks to a minimum. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic, but energy savings are available by reducing air leaks by any amount. In addition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors. \n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation due to the low current leak load.\n\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: Colleen Johnson\n', 11649),
(46, 11629, '2.	Insulate GTS End\n\nThe facility has two GTS hot oil process heaters which provide hot oil to heat the press and dryers. The heaters are rated at 42 MMBtu/hr, fueled by natural gas, and use direct fire burners. During the facility visit, only burner #2 was operating. Analysts used a thermal imager and digital contact thermometer to take temperature measurements on the outside of the process heater. The ambient temperature of the room was measured at 80.2 °F. The main body of the chamber already has some insulation, and the surface temperature was measured at 130 °F. According to facility personnel, the flat end of the chamber with the entrance for the burner has internal 6 3/8” refractory insulation. It was too hot to use a contact thermometer on, however thermal images were collected and analysts assumed the surface temperature to be 350 °F based on these images, which are shown below. \n2.	Insulate GTS End\n\nThe facility has two GTS hot oil process heaters which provide hot oil to heat the press and dryers. The heaters are rated at 42 MMBtu/hr, fueled by natural gas, and use direct fire burners. During the facility visit, only burner #2 was operating. Analysts used a thermal imager and digital contact thermometer to take temperature measurements on the outside of the process heater. The ambient temperature of the room was measured at 80.2 °F. The main body of the chamber already has some insulation, and the surface temperature was measured at 130 °F. According to facility personnel, the flat end of the chamber with the entrance for the burner has internal 6 3/8” refractory insulation. It was too hot to use a contact thermometer on, however thermal images were collected and analysts assumed the surface temperature to be 350 °F based on these images, which are shown below. \n2.	Insulate GTS End\n\nThe facility has two GTS hot oil process heaters which provide hot oil to heat the press and dryers. The heaters are rated at 42 MMBtu/hr, fueled by natural gas, and use direct fire burners. During the facility visit, only burner #2 was operating. Analysts used a thermal imager and digital contact thermometer to take temperature measurements on the outside of the process heater. The ambient temperature of the room was measured at 80.2 °F. The main body of the chamber already has some insulation, and the surface temperature was measured at 130 °F. According to facility personnel, the flat end of the chamber with the entrance for the burner has internal 6 3/8” refractory insulation. It was too hot to use a contact thermometer on, however thermal images were collected and analysts assumed the surface temperature to be 350 °F based on these images, which are shown below. \nDue to this high surface temperature, there is an opportunity to save energy by insulating the outside of the process heater’s flat end. Using a surface area of 89 ft2, analysts performed an incremental analysis for energy savings due to insulation. The heat transfer software 3E Plus was used to calculated heat losses at varying insulation thicknesses. This 3E Plus analysis, and all other calculations for each insulation thickness can be found in the Site Data Section of this report. According to these calculations, adding 0.5 inch insulation will save an estimated 676 MMBtu/yr, which is equivalent to $3,277/yr. one inch insulation will save 728 MMBtu/yr, yielding savings of $3,532/yr, and 1.5 inch insulation will save 751 MMBtu/yr with $3,641/yr cost savings. Implementation cost for this insulation is estimated around $700. \n\nThis opportunity was not written as a full recommendation because the facility informed analysts that in past attempts to insulate the GTS end, the heat trapped by the insulation caused the metal to warp. Analysts were unable to predict if the different thicknesses of this insulation would also cause warping, and therefore cannot confidently recommend the facility invest in it. \n\nAuthored by: Colleen Johnson\nReviewed by: Vincent Albright\n', 11629),
(47, 11606, '3.	Turn Off Idle Conveyors\n\nThe facility uses several different conveyors throughout the day. There is a 10 hp Ripsaw #2 Infeed, a 10 hp Reman System Infeed Belt, and a 15 hp Transfer Chain #2. Each process depends on the use of multiple material handling systems, mainly via conveyors. These conveyors operate continuously during operation hours even without conveying material. The types of conveyors vary from chain to standard belts.\n\nIdle motors or motors that operate continuously while not loaded can be a large part of a facility\'s energy consumption. Finding ways to control these systems more effectively can lead to dramatic decreases in annual energy use. There are many different load-sensing capabilities that can control the operation of conveyors. The two that are most practical for wood product facilities are optical sensing and pressure or force sensing. This was not made a full recommendation because analysts were unable to obtain the information necessary to estimate savings.\n\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Justin Conklin\n', 11606),
(48, 11607, '4.	Convert to Electric Forklifts\n\nThe facility currently operates propane forklifts which are used to transport veneer and finished products throughout the facility. Analysts noticed the majority of the forklifts are operated indoor under facility cover. Personnel perform maintenance on the facility’s own forklifts.\n\nAnalysts recommend incrementally upgrading to electric forklifts as propane forklifts become inoperable and require replacement. Electric forklifts use electric motors and batteries to increase energy efficiency and decrease maintenance costs compared to propane or diesel forklifts. This is more cost effective because propane can cost up to three times more than electricity for equal energy content. Charging systems are a typical way to measure forklift electrical energy use. Monthly demand and total electrical energy consumption comprise the energy costs for electric forklifts. Electric forklifts are beneficial for indoor use, especially cold rooms, since there are no harmful emissions. They also have average operational lives of 8 years compared to 6 years for propane forklifts.\n\nImplementation options include fork lifts with the newest generation of fast-charging stations. These stations require little floor space and can be placed where forklifts can easily access them. Fast-charging stations deliver 400 to 600 amps to rapidly charge a battery from below 20% to 80%. Installing fast charging stations may have a greater impact on electrical demand cost then slow charging stations.\n\nA full analysis on this recommendation was not performed because analysts were unable to obtain necessary information.\n\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Vincent Albright\n', 11607),
(49, 11671, '5.	Convert to LED Lighting\n\nThe facility currently has approximately 118-1000 W metal halide lamps in the inventory yard. An approximate lighting inventory was completed and included in the Site Data Section of this report. The facility expressed interest in reducing light pollution from excessive, misdirected light from the metal halide lamps and upgrading to more efficient lighting. \n\nLED lamps provide brighter and more efficient lighting, have a longer life expectancy, and lower maintenance costs compared to metal halide lamps. Additionally, LED light is directional, as opposed to standard lamps that spread light spherically. This allows lower power draw to provide the same perceived light. This is an advantage for security lighting and any other uses that require light to be focused on a specific area. Directional lighting also cuts down on light pollution. Light pollution occurs when excessive, misdirected light makes it more difficult to focus on the intended target area.\n\nAnalysts investigated savings associated with upgrading from metal halide lamps to LED lighting in the inventory yard. An upgrade to LED lighting may require lowering the fixtures and additional study. Analysts assumed a 75% output factor for the 1,000 W metal halide lamps and a 100% output factor for 300 W LED high bay lamps. Analysts predict that the facility will be able to replace 118, 1000 W metal halide lamps with 152, 300 W LED high bay lamps. This results in annual cost savings of $15,908 after a $165,680 implementation cost resulting in an 11 year payback period. \n\nAnalysis was also considered for replacing 400 W metal halide wall packs with 60 W LED Exterior wall packs. This would result in an annual cost savings of $4,576, after an implementation cost of $38,640, resulting in an 8.4 year payback period.\n\nThese measures were not developed into full recommendations because of the long payback periods.\n\nAuthored by: Tracie Tran\nReviewed by: Shelby Foley\n', 11671),
(50, 12161, 'The facility uses a V-Still evaporator to separate the solids in the product solution from the water. Paratherm NF oil is heated within the V-Still\'s oil jacket in order to heat the solution in the evaporator. The oil is electrically heated causing the solution to evaporate. Approximately 400 to 500 gallons of solution go through the evaporator per day. The operators of the V-Still have the solution temperature set to 310° F, while the solution has a boiling point of about 220° F. According to facility personnel, the operators have the oil heat set to 310° F because of established protocol. Analysts collected data about the energy consumption of the evaporator heater using HOBO dataloggers and contacted facility personnel for more information regarding the V-Still evaporator operation hours and set points.\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the collected data was not enough to do a full analysis. However, the datalogger information did show that the V-Still evaporator powers off and on throughout the day to keep a consistent heat. Analysts recommend doing a further study on the energy consumed by the evaporator.', 7),
(51, 12160, 'The facility currently operates two boilers with sustained steam pressures of 150 psig. The boilers are alternated to provide steam to the facility 8,760 hours per year. Facility personnel told analysts that the boilers produce steam at a rate of 13,000 lb/hr. Both boilers burn natural gas at a rate of approximately 650,000 scfm per day between the two of them. This recommendation is based around heat recovery from the boilers.\nAnalysts wrote two recommendations regarding the boiler systems. This is an alternative for assessment recommendation No. 3, Connect CB RTO Gases to Boiler #2. The full recommendation can be found on the following pages.', 1),
(52, 12541, '4. Replace Propane Forklifts with Electric Forklifts\nThe facility uses five propane forklifts. Forklifts operate approximately 2 hours per day, 260 days per year. The load capacity requirements range from 8,000 to 12,000 pounds. The following analysis investigates replacing a 12,000 pound capacity propane forklift that is currently ready to be retired.\nReplacing the propane forklift with an electric forklift will save $2,505 annually in energy costs and $390 in operational costs, for a total of $2,895 in savings. This was calculated using a conservative cost estimate of $4.00 per gallon of propane, and $0.05660 per kWh for electricity. It is recommended that the forklifts are replaced incrementally, which reduces the implementation cost to the difference between a new electric forklift and a comparable propane forklift. Analysts received a vendor quote of $69,444 for a 12,000 pound electric forklift, including one battery and a charger. Analysts estimated an implementation cost of $45,444, using an estimated propane forklift cost of $24,000. This results in a 15.7 year payback period.\nAnalysts did not develop this into a full recommendation due to its high payback period. The current forklifts do not operate long enough to generate energy savings to pay for the implementation cost.\nElectric forklifts are able to perform the same tasks a comparable propane forklift will accomplish, but at a lower operational and energy cost. These reduced costs will make up for the more expensive electric forklift, assuming that the forklifts are operated long enough to produce enough savings.', 6),
(53, 12545, 'The facility has three hydraulic power units. Two of them are located at the debarker, and the third one is located at the stackers and scramblers. One of power units provides power to the rotary kicker and the linear kicker. The other power unit at the debarked is for the step feeder. The last power unit is used for the stacker and the scrambler. Analyst believe that the rotary kicker, stacker, and scrambler could be powered directly using electric motors with gear reduction. All the hydraulic power units are powered by 50 hp electrical motors. Live amperage from the three electric motors were obtained on site. Live readings indicated 10 to 30 amps of current draw. Further detail is located in Isshu & David’s site report.\nHydraulics are typically used to perform tasks that require high torque at low speeds. Hydraulic motors are driven by a pump that is driven by an electric motor. Hydraulic systems lose a significant portion of energy through heating of fluid and conversion losses. Systems that only use electric motors do not experience the same types of energy losses. Combined with a gearbox and clutch, electric motors can achieve the same torques and speeds as hydraulic motors. Hydraulic systems also typically maintain a constant base operating pressure, but electric motors can be turned off whenever they are not in use, allowing for additional savings. Hydraulics typically require higher maintenance costs compared to electric motors.\nConverting the all three hydraulic motor with an electric motor and gear box can result in an annual savings of 285,261 kWh/yr. Estimating that the hydraulic unit has a total efficiency of 37% [1] and a premium electric motor with gearbox has an efficiency of 84% [2]. The energy savings is estimated to be $16,000/yr. with a savings from maintenance cost of $3,000 per year per unit according to facility personnel. Analysts also recommend investigating if the facility can downsize to a smaller electric motor (35-40 hp) as it would achieve the same work but allow better part-load efficiency if sized correctly. The costs for installing and purchasing three 50 hp electric motors are estimated to be $15,000. The cost of installing and purchasing three gearboxes and clutches is estimated to be $30,000. This results in a 1.8 years simple payback\nA full analysis was not done on this recommendation because of inadequate data. The amperage readings from the dataloggers and live readings is below the expected readings for 50 hp motors. A 50 hp motor can draw 68 A at full load, but the live amperage readings were inconsistent but average 20 A.', 8),
(54, 12311, 'NULL', 0),
(55, 12528, 'The hardboard facility contains two 50 hp Quincy Northwest rotary screw compressors, which\nsupply compressed air to the facility. Pneumatic motors are inherently less efficient than electric\nmotors as electrical energy must be converted into compressed air power using the compressor, and\nthen converted to mechanical energy wherever the motor is located. Conversely, an electric motor\nonly has to convert electrical energy into mechanical energy, bypassing the extra energy conversions.\nSome of the motors used in the production process are air driven by two air compressors. Analysts\nnoted that many of these air driven motors could be replaced with electrically driven motors, to\nreduce energy consumption. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation due to an\nincomplete list of pneumatic motors used in the facility.', 3),
(56, 12597, 'The facility processes lumber products by grade, cross-sectional size and, length. The lumber boards\nare sorted based on grade, cross-sectional sizes, and length to match different target sizes to be\neligible for sale. The facility uses two machines, the Edger and Green Chain, to process their lumber\ninto the required sizes. Analysts were informed by facility personnel that the Edger and Green Chain\nmachines do not operate simultaneously. The Green Chain is operated by nine workers who are\nclassified as operator, grader, trimmer, and pullers. Similarly, the Edger machine requires nine\npersonnel which include one operator, two graders, and six pullers.\nThe machines have individual bin sorting stations. The pullers sort the lumber into bins from the\nEdger and Green Chain by grade, cross-sectional size, and length. Facility personnel informed\nanalysts that the machines run for 8 hours per day for 5 days per week. Production information for the\ntwo machines was provided by facility personnel.\nAn analysis was done for redesigning the current machine layout by, unifying and automating the\nlumber sorting station for the machines with 1.5 times the number of sorting bins in the Green Chain.\nDoing so will allow the facility to relocate six workers into high rotation positions, reducing\noperational costs by six full-time-equivalent (FTE) at temporary work labor costs (an estimated\n$300,000 at $25 per hour), after an implementation cost of $2,000,000.\nInstalling an automated sorting station with automatic bin discharge, sensors, programmable logic\ncontrollers (PLC), and Edger optimization systems would help increase productivity and eliminate\nwaste. Relocation of personnel from the Edger and Green Chain machines into other available\npositions would be favorable for the facility and help save productive training time for new\nemployees.\nThis measure was not fully developed into a recommendation because analysts were unable to\nestimate the costs of moving the machinery nor were able to calculate the impact in productivity that\nthis measure may have. This consideration’s high cost savings make this a lucrative opportunity,\nespecially in the event that the North side of the facility need to be relocated.', 18),
(57, 12599, 'Palletized sorted wood is either sent into a storage area or directly into the sticker-stacker machine. At\nthe sticker stacker wood is sorted into stacks, separating the lumber with one-inch-high Brazilian\nhardwood. The spacing between the lumber allows the steam of the kilns to uniformly transfer heat to\nthe wood. At the sticker stacker, units of wood are loaded into a platform that lifts the unit one layer\nof wood at the time, allowing the upper layer to slide onto the stacker machine. The sliding layer is\nrepositioned onto a different platform, downstream from the first one, where Brazilian hardwood laths\nare used as separators from the upcoming layer.\nThe facility currently operates two sticker-stacker machines, E and L. Sticker stacker E requires one\noperator to supervise the alignment of the laths on the sliding surface, and two operators to place the\nstickers in-between layers of wood. Sticker stacker L is mostly automated and requires one operator.\nSticker stackers E and L can handle lengths up to 26 ft and 20 ft, respectively.\nFacility personnel expressed their concern for attrition of personnel in eight positions that have had\nseveral different employees during the last year. They are also interested in upgrading the sticker\nstacker E to a similar technology of that implemented at sticker stacker L. After the upgrade, they\nwould be able to release two full-time-equivalent (FTE) employees from sticker stacker E and\ndesignate them into different tasks.\nUpgrading the sticker stacker will allow the facility to save $93,750 annually, equivalent to two FTE\nof the day shift salary, by relocating two of the experienced personnel from the sticker-stacker into\nthe high rotation positions. An additional $20,000 savings will occur corresponding to the one-on-one\n5-hours-training that new personnel have to go through during a two week training period.\nAnalysts were unable to determine the cost of overhauling and upgrading the current sticker stacker;\nhowever, commercial alternatives for replacing the current system are found with values that range\nbetween $200,000 and $400,000.\nThis measure was not fully developed into a recommendation because analysts were unable to\nestimate the costs of moving the machinery nor were able to calculate the impact in productivity that\nthis measure may have.', 18),
(58, 12591, 'The facility has a total of 15 air compressors on site. These compressors range between 3 and 50 hp.\nDataloggers were placed on three of the larger compressors during the assessment. These\ncompressors are located near the Planer A, the Resaw, and the Drychain, and are 50, 15, and 25 hp,\nrespectively.\nAir leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. People\noften mistakenly assume that compressed air is cheap. Many leaks are due to faulty fittings, lines,\nvalves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. The term leak load refers to the minimum sustained\nair demand, which is assumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system. Eliminating all leaks in a\ncomplex air distribution system is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks throughout the facility to\nachieve a leak load equal to 30% of peak, non-leak plant demand can be a reasonable objective. In\naddition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks\nand deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because the data collected did not include\ninformation on when the compressors were running but not being used. Thus, the leak load could not\nbe identified.', 3),
(59, 12538, 'Insulate the boiler condensate return pipe with a 1.5\'\' fiberglass insulation jacket. This will reduce heat loss and energy consumption by 211 MMBtu per year. Annual cost savings will be $348 with a 0.3 year payback period after an implementation cost of $105.', 1),
(61, 12435, 'The facility has two dust collection systems (DCS) to remove particulate from the air to enhance the air quality of the workplace. Both systems included clean side fans that are open to the atmosphere. Facility personnel informed analysts that the fans operate approximately from Sunday at 3:30 pm to Friday at 5:00 pm. The 150 hp DCS is located next to the Planer Shavings (PS) process and the 40 hp DCS is located next to the Quad Band Mill (QBM).\nDust collection systems ducted into the facility on the inlet and open to the outside on the outlet can pull heated air out of the facility and create a negative pressure in the facility; this can result in building energy losses and cause a fire hazard as well. Pulling air out of the facility can increase the load on the fan and pulling heated air out increases the operation of the heaters inside the facility. Ducting the outlet of the filtered air back to the facility will recapture heated facility air and promote a positive pressure in the facility. This was not developed into a recommendation because the facility uses heaters to directly provide heat to employees and does not heat the entire facility space. Also, analysts were unable to quantify the savings available in reducing the fan load.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Isshu Lee', 4),
(62, 12429, 'The facility uses compressed air to power air knives and air blasters. Facility personnel explained that the air knife air flow duration has recently been reduced. This is beneficial because at the facility’s 100 psig air pressure, a ¼ inch diameter air flow orifice produces approximately 90 acfm of air. This is costly if bursts have long durations or occur frequently.\nCost savings can be realized if compressed air uses such as air knives and air blasters are replaced with blowers to achieve the same effect. This opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts placed it as low priority on the facility visit and did not collect sufficient data to conduct an analysis.\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: Tyler Giddings', 3),
(63, 12433, 'The facility uses multiple meters to measure and pay for electrical energy consumption. Consolidating electrical service to one meter can reduce service fees you pay each month and the rate schedule you fall under. There may be a reduction in total and reactive power demand because of the combined savings. These savings are possible because the peak demand period for the separate meters probably will not occur at the same time. Consolidated demand may be less than the sum of the individual meters. There are no energy savings associated with this recommendation, only cost savings. The largest savings occur when the combined usage qualifies you for a different rate schedule available to larger power and energy users. This was not pursued as a recommendation due to the high cost of installing new power lines, generators, and production interruption.\nAuthored by: Pavel Grechanuk\nReviewed by: Miles Naughton', 15),
(65, 12431, 'The facility has three rooms with AC units that maintain a specific temperature. The PDM room has one Fujitsu AC unit that has an approximate set point of 72 °F. The Cylinder room has one Fujitsu AC unit that has an approximate set point of 60 °F. The Server room has three Fujitsu AC units that have approximate set points of 60 °F. The AC units in the Server room are used to keep the servers from overheating. Facility personnel informed analysts that the AC units operate continuously throughout the year.\nAnalysts considered increasing the set points of the AC units in the PDM, Cylinder, and Server Rooms. Analysts assumed that the set points of each the rooms can be increased by 5 °F. The increase in set point will allow the AC unit to operate less and save electricity. Analysts estimate an annual savings of approximately $200. This was not pursued as a full recommendation because of low savings.\nAuthored by: David Chan-Vasquez\nReviewed by: Pavel Grechanuk', 19),
(66, 12426, 'Facility personnel informed analysts that the hydraulics on the Sorter production process may be operating excessively. Facility personnel stated that a behavioral change is all that would be necessary to reduce operation hours, and thus energy consumption, of the Sorter hydraulics. The Sorter hydraulic motors add up to about 100 connected hp. Facility personnel estimated that operation could be reduced by one hour per day, five days per week. Assuming that the loading cycle of these motors is ¼ of operation time at full load and ¾ of operation time at ¼ load, annual cost savings are estimated at just under $600 per year. There would be no implementation cost for this energy savings opportunity. This opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation because of the relatively small annual cost savings available.\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: Scott Lindbloom', 8),
(68, 12436, 'The facility uses heaters to provide heat for employees. Heat rises as it is produced and collects at the facility ceiling, providing no added benefit. Also, heat can escape from holes or openings in the ceiling and be conducted through non-insulated materials. This commonly causes heaters to operate longer than necessary to provide added heat. Using fans at the top of the workspace to circulate air will evenly distribute the heated air and prevent heat from rising and escaping. This was not developed into a recommendation because the facility use heaters to directly provide heat to employees and do not heat the entire facility space. Further, the heaters do not have external temperature set point controls that influence the operation of the units.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Isshu Lee', 4),
(70, 12472, 'The facility uses compressed air to remove wood chips from machinery during processing. The facility air pressure is 110 psig. Of particular interest is the replacement of two air blasters on the Distribution Line, which blow wood shavings after drilling holes in wood timbers. Analysts estimated the air flow orifice diameter to be ¼ inch, resulting in an air flow of about 90 acfm. Analysts observed and timed the operation of these air blasters. On average there was 1.5 seconds of air flow, and seven seconds between bursts. Given the production schedule of this line, the cost to power these air blasters is less than $200 per year.\n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation because of the low cost savings, which would likely yield a long payback period.\n\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: Miles Naughton\n', 3);
INSERT INTO `OMCs` (`OMCId`, `TaskId`, `OMCText`, `SubjectId`) VALUES
(71, 12463, 'The facility has three kiln rooms to dry wood products. The facility uses a dehumidification system to help maintain a specific humidity during the drying process. This system has fans that drive the dehumidification process, which must be on when this system is running. These fans also contribute to the overall airflow during the kiln process. Two kiln rooms have two 15 hp fans and the other kiln room has two 25 hp fans. The drying process lasts approximately 324 hours. All fans are maintained at full speed for each kiln’s drying cycle while the dehumidification process is in operation.\n\nIn typical kiln drying processes, lumber drying occurs in two stages: convection limited and diffusion limited. In the first stage, drying is limited by how fast the air can carry surface moisture away from the lumber. The convection limited stage generally requires the fans to operate at full speed. In the second stage, diffusion limited, the drying is limited by how fast the moisture within the wood can move to the surface. In this stage, the fan speed can be reduced without slowing the drying process. Generally, the fan speed can be slowed to 80% of the original speed [4.1].\n\nSavings are achieved by installing a VFD to reduce the electrical demand required to operate the fan at a specified setting. A VFD will allow a fan speed to be reduced. The relationship between the power a fan consumes and its speed is shown in the equation below:\n							\n				\n\n\nIt can be seen, as the fan speed (ni) decreases, the power that the fan uses (Pi) decreases in a cubed relationship. \n\nAssuming that all three kilns are capable of 20 kiln cycles annually, running the fans at 100% costs the facility approximately $34,500 annually. Thus, reducing the fan speeds by 10% during the last third of each cycle would save the facility approximately $3,100 annually. This was not developed into a full recommendation due to the complexity of modeling the kiln dehumidification processes. Fan speed corrections could result in negative effects to the quality of product produced.\n\nReferences:\n\n[4.1] Carroll, Hatch and Associates, \"A Guidebook to Electrical Energy Savings at Lumber Dry Kilns through Fan Speed Reduction,\" Bonneville Power Administration, 1987, Dept. of Energy Technical Report DOE/BP-23462-2.\n\nAuthored by: W. Keith Striby, Jr.\nReviewed by: Miles Naughton\n', 4),
(72, 12473, 'The facility currently has two compressors (5 hp and 30 hp) supplying air to the Distribution and Sawing Lines. Facility personnel informed analysts that the 30 hp compressor operates based on demand and is equipped with throttle controls. The 5 hp compressor has on/off controls. The compressed air system operates at 110 psig. Analysts observed that the 5 hp compressor turned on after the pressure on the receiver tank dropped below 110 psig. \n\nAfter observing the system in operation, analysts suspect there may potentially be significant leaks present. Air leaks often represent a high hidden cost with rapid payback for inspection and repair activities. The facility’s utility provider may be able to provide an ultrasonic leak detector to identify repairable air leaks. \n\nAnalysts attempted to record compressor operation data for a week of production. Unfortunately, the data which was recorded by a datalogger was not usable. As such, this was not pursued as a full recommendation due to the lack of data to analyze compressor energy use profiles. \n\nAuthored by: David Chan-Vasquez\nReviewed by: Pavel Grechanuk\n\n', 3),
(73, 12467, 'The facility uses multiple meters to measure electricity consumption. Consolidating meters will reduce total monthly service fees. Further cost savings are available if consolidating meters causes the facility to fall into a different rate schedule. There may be a reduction in demand costs because the peak demand periods for the various meters likely do not occur simultaneously. Therefore, the peak demand costs of consolidated meters may be less than the sum of the individual meters. There are no energy savings associated with this recommendation. The largest cost savings would occur when the combined usage qualifies the facility for a different rate schedule for larger power and energy users. This was not pursued as a recommendation due to the high cost of installing new power lines and generators as well as production interruption.\n	\nAuthored by: Pavel Grechanuk\nReviewed by: Miles Naughton\n\n', 15),
(74, 12461, 'The primary air compressor at the facility is a 30 hp throttle-controlled rotary screw compressor. There is also a 5 hp reciprocating back-up compressor. Facility personnel informed analysts production delays occur approximately once per month when air demand exceeds the compressor capacity. Facility personnel estimated that these delays last for anywhere between five to 45 minutes. They estimated that the delays last 30 minutes on average. The cost of 30 minutes of downtime was estimated by personnel at $200 in labor costs. The annual cost of downtime due to monthly delays is $2,400.\n\nThe production downtimes due to air shortages could be avoided by installing a larger compressor and additional receiver capacity. A 75 hp variable frequency drive (VFD) air compressor is believed to be a suitable solution. Based on past quotes obtained by EEC analysts, the total installed cost of such a compressor is estimated at $50,000. An additional 500 gallons of receiver capacity could be purchased for $10,000 from an online industrial supplies catalog. \n\nEnergy savings would be achieved by operating a VFD compressor rather than a throttle-controlled compressor, even though the proposed compressor would be larger than the existing compressor. The current compressor was observed to operate with a 24 kW power draw, which is likely at full capacity. The following estimates were calculated assuming a similar demand will be placed on the new compressor.\n\nAssuming that the compressor operates 2,500 hours per year (10 hours per day, five days per week, 50 weeks per year), energy savings are 7,500 kWh per year. Electrical demand savings are 36 kW-months per year. Total electrical cost savings are $700 per year.\n\nTotal net cost savings are $3,100 with estimated implementation costs of $60,000, resulting in a 19 year payback. This opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation due to the long payback period. Incentives may be available to help pay for implementation.\n\nInstallation of a VFD air compressor of sufficient size will maintain energy efficient operation while reducing its capacity. This will be beneficial at this facility because of its various operating schedules for the many production processes. For example, the main planer’s air demand is served by the main facility compressor, because the dedicated planer compressor is undersized. With a larger primary facility compressor, the air demand of the planer can be met in addition to the other air demands, but the compressor will operate energy efficiently at low loads.\n\nAuthored by: Brad Moore\nReviewed by: Miles Naughton\n', 3),
(75, 12468, 'The facility has a Burnham three-pass steam generator, which is primarily used to dry lumber in the three kilns. The common steam header that supplies each of these kilns has damaged steam traps according to the kiln operations manager. Analysts were informed that the steam entering the kiln needs to have most of the moisture content removed to effectively dry the product. Steam used to dry product is used to heat coils or is directly injected into the kiln. The steam has a large moisture content and all condensate is lost when steam is directly injected into the kiln. The kiln operator informed analyst that both methods of heating the kiln are used as necessary based on the moisture content in the kiln. \n\nSteam traps are automatic valves that filter out condensate and non-condensable gases without allowing steam to escape. Steam-based heating processes use latent heat and transfer it to a given product. When steam is formed, water molecules are still bonded or mixed with steam, and the heating efficiency will be reduced if condensate is not removed rapidly. \n\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable to quantify the energy savings resulting from repairing the steam traps.\n\nAuthored by: W. Keith Striby, Jr.\nReviewed by: Anthony Mizener\n', 12468),
(76, 12303, 'Reduce Trim Waste\nThe main product of the facility is particleboard, which is a composite material made using sawdust,\ngreen shaves, and a suitable binder. The raw materials are initially dried to reduce the humidity\ncontent from approximately 43% to 3% and then stored in silos, separated by particle size and\nmaterial. These particles are then mixed with different resins and deposited into sheets. A pressing\n(extruder) process is carried out at both cold and hot temperatures, to achieve desired density and\nthickness of the particleboard sheet as well as to cure the binding agent. Finally, the particleboard\nsheets are cooled, trimmed, and sanded. The sheets can be sold as raw material or move on to a\nfinishing process.\nStudies have shown that chip form, size distribution and the type of added glue, influence the result of\nthe cutting process in particle boards, being related to and including fracture energy, friction and cut\nmaterial deformation [1]. This suggests the possibility of dynamically adapting the trimming width of\nfinal boards depending on their manufacturing parameters, reducing the width of the trimmed board,\nhence, waste. Kollmann et al. [2] also mention that other factors that influence the cut quality in a\nparticleboard are the type of teeth in circular saw blades, cutting speed, clearance angles, and resharpening\nschedules of cutting tools.\nFacility personnel indicated that they currently trim two inches per side to the boards before they\nmove into dimensioning, and that every inch has a cost impact of $500,000 per year. Implementing a\ndynamic system that adjusts its trimming parameters depending on material properties and process\nparameters will allow the facility to reduce waste and its associated cost. This measure was not\npresented as a fully developed recommendation since analysts were not able to obtain information\nnecessary to estimate waste reduction accurately.\n[1] Beer, P., Sinn, G., Gindl, M., & Tschegg, S. (2005). Work of fracture and of chips formation\nduring linear cutting of particle-board. Journal of Materials Processing Technology, 159(2), 224-228.\ndoi:10.1016/j.jmatprotec.2004.05.009\n[2] Kollmann, F., Kuenzi, E., & Stamm, A. (2012). Section: 5.4 Properties of Particleboard. In\nPrinciples of Wood Science and Technology: II Wood Based Materials (p. 517-522). Springer\nScience & Business Media.\nAuthored by: Alberto Pezzani Silva\nReviewed by: Vincent Albright', 12303),
(77, 12299, 'NULL', 0),
(79, 12301, 'NULL', 0),
(80, 12302, 'Automate Quality Control\nThe facility operates a painting line to add value to the particle board sheets. This process conveys\nparticle board sheets through machines that apply paint, and sand and cure the product. After one side\nhas been coated the sheet is flipped and the process is repeated for the unpainted side. The line is\ncontrolled by two operators who perform all quality control processes. Salaries for operators range\nbetween $25 and $30 per hour, resulting in an average of $27.50 per hour.\nFacility personnel told analysts that if a defect is detected, then an average of 15 sheets will be\nconsidered defective in the process. If defective sheets are not detected till the end of the cycle, then a\nmajority of the value added process is applied to defective product. The facility has used a previous\nautomated quality control system that did not adequately detect defects due to the lighter wood used\nin the particle board sheets. However, this system operated satisfactorily for a sister facility that used\ndarker wood in their process.\nAnalysts considered that the facility seek an automated systems vendor to implement an automated\nquality control system for their particle board sheets and reallocate one operator for more productive\nuse, resulting in a savings of $171,500 (assuming 6,240 for operation hours) of labor productivity that\ncan be allocated to more productive use. This was not developed into a full recommendation because\nanalysts unable to obtain the cost of defective products and the paint line operation hours.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Adam Shareghi', 12302),
(81, 12304, 'Install Efficient Fan Blades on Dust Collection Systems\nThe facility uses multiple Dust Collection Systems (DCS) to remove particulates from the air to\nenhance work place air quality. There were four clean side fans that analysts considered the biggest\nopportunities for savings. These fans are clean side operated and are open to atmosphere on the outlet.\nFacility personnel told analysts the fans operate from approximately Sunday at 11:00 pm to Friday at\n9:30 pm each week.\nDust collection systems can be arranged in either “material-handling” or “clean-side” configurations.\nMaterial-handling fans operate with particulates flowing through the fans and use positive pressure to\npush the material downstream. Clean-side fans operate downstream of the baghouse where no\nparticulates flow. They use negative pressure to pull particles from the facility into a baghouse.\nThere are six types of centrifugal fan blades commonly used in dust collection systems. These blade\ntypes, efficiencies and characteristics are listed below. Increasing the static efficiency of a system\ndecreases the motor input power approximately by the ratio of the current static efficiency over the\nproposed static efficiency.\nAnalysts considered installing airfoil blades in the current DCS system as opposed to radial blades.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because the facility’s fan vendor informed\nanalysts that the current systems either use airfoil or backward curved blades. This is a best practice\nand no recommendation is necessary.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Isshu Lee', 4),
(84, 12637, 'NULL', 0),
(85, 12393, 'Facility personnel expressed concerns about the peak electrical demand caused by the forklift charging stations. Utility companies charge for the peak demand (highest power draw). During breaks, forklifts are parked at the charging stations to charge all the batteries at once. There may be savings available if the charging station is currently the cause of the peak demand. If so, it is recommended to install a demand controller that de-energizes the forklift charging station if power draw approaches peak electrical demand. There will only be savings if the charging stations are causing the highest peak demand. However, if the batteries are charged on a meter that is not charged for demand, cost savings likely do not exist.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were not able to confirm that the charging stations are the cause of the peak demand or the savings that would occur if the demand control was implemented.', 12393),
(86, 12162, 'The facility recovers condensate from flash steam using two Pivotrol pressure powered pumps. Operating on a positive pressure displacement principle, condensate enters the pump body through an inlet valve which causes a float to rise. As the chamber fills, the valve changeover linkage is engaged opening the steam inlet valve and closing the exhaust valve. As pressure inside the pump increases above the total back pressure, condensate is forced out through the outlet valve using compressed air. The condensate is sent into a reservoir tank which is recirculated into the boiler feed water. The cycle is repeated each time the chamber fills up.\nA pressure pumps meter started counting cycles from the beginning of June through June 7th.. During this one week period, the pressure pump went through 36,805 cycles. Plant personnel informed analysts that each pumping cycle last about 10 seconds, the chamber size is between 10-15 gallons, and the pressure of the water exiting the chamber is approximately 30 psig.\nAnalysis recommended replacing the pressure pumps with electric pumps to save on electricity consumption since compressed air is expensive. An initial analysis done on one pressure pump resulted in an operational cost of approximately $250 per year. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the operational cost alone will not compensate for the purchase and operational cost of replacing the pressure pumps with electric pumps.', 12),
(87, 12163, 'The facility reclaims silver from the soap solution. After the soap mixture is made, the facility uses as much of the solution as possible for the process and puts the rest through a series of tanks to separate the silver chloride from the solution. The facility then sends the water to the city sewer system, and sends the silver chloride to a third party to separate the silver out.\nDistilled water is used by the facility for the production process, and the facility does the distilling process on site. The facility brings city water in and then distill it before use. A possible recommendation would be to create a closed loop for the water to be used repeatedly through the production process. The solution then moves through the current reclamation process with an added step to remove sodium nitrates and fatty acids. The water would then be fed back to the distiller and through the process again. Water from the city would need to be added periodically due to evaporation, but the water use would be reduced significantly. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the process of removing sodium nitrate from water would be very energy-intensive. Sodium nitrate is soluble in water, so the process to remove it from water would require high temperatures and high pressures. The cost of additional tanks and energy use do not justify due to the low cost of water at this time.', 16),
(88, 12648, 'The facility produces hot rolled steel products. Billets are preheated in the preheat furnace to 2200 °F before the products are rolled. The furnace uses natural gas, and the air for combustion is provided by a 400 hp motor driven fan. The combustion air flow is currently being controlled by a VFD and an inlet damper. Analysts proposed to control the air flow by using a VFD only. A damper is a mechanical barrier that reduces the cross section of a duct, reducing the total air flow while operating the fan at constant rotational speed. Energy savings may be generated by reducing air flow using only the installed VFD instead of using dampers. Analysts performed an analysis on the 400 hp motor by data logging a week of amperage readings and extrapolating to determine a full year’s energy consumption using the Motor Analysis Tool (MAT), which is located in the site data section of this report. It was determined that the motor consumes 964,444 kWh annually which results in $40,825 annually, assuming $0.04233 per kWh. Analysts attempted to determine when the fan operates with the VFD only, Inlet Damper only, or both by using variations in motor amperage. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because information of how the fans currently modulate by using the motor amperage readings was inconclusive. Analysts assume that if the inlet vain is eliminated and the fan be controlled by a VFD only, 5% of annual energy consumption can be saved which results in $2,041 annually.  ', 2),
(89, 11920, 'The facility currently uses a chlorine-based disinfection process to clean waste water. Sodium\nhypochlorite is initially introduced to the water in the secondary clarifier, where it then needs 30 minutes\nof contact time to disinfect the water to the correct level. The water is moved through a serpentine\nchannel for half an hour. Sodium bisulfate is added to remove any excess chlorine before the water leaves\nthe facility.\nDuring the site assessment, facility personnel expressed interest in changing the chlorine-based\ndisinfection process to a peracetic acid disinfection process. Peracetic acid is widely used in Europe for\nwaste water, but it has not gained popularity in the United States. Demand in the US causes the cost to be\nincreased compared to chlorine-based disinfection.\nPeracetic acid has multiple benefits over the chlorine-based disinfection process. One major benefit is\nthat the peracetic acid does not create chlorination compounds or harmful by-products. Peracetic acid can\nbe safely introduced to the water and not cause potential harm once the water leaves the facility. Another\nbenefit is that only one chemical would be needed to complete the disinfection process. Chlorine-based\ndisinfection requires both the chlorine chemical, and a chemical to remove any excess chlorine before\nleaving the facility. In addition, peracetic acid requires a shorter contact time to achieve the same results\nas the chlorine-based process. Water would move more quickly through the facility, which could lead to a\nsmaller chance of flooding during the winter.\nThe facility treats approximately 4203.47 MG of water per year. Chlorine-based disinfection chemicals\ncost the facility $114,008 per year, and the estimate for the peracetic acid disinfection would be about\n$270,000 per year.\nThis measure is not developed into a full recommendation because the cost of changing to the new\nprocess would be double the current costs. Peracetic acid would lead to better results if implemented, so it\nis strongly recommended to continually look into the pricing. As the demand for peracetic acid increases\nin the US, the price should become more comparable to that of chlorine-based disinfection.', 0),
(90, 11921, 'Influent pumps move wastewater from the city’s sewage and storm drains to the main side of the facility\nand initially passes through a primary filter. There are four 20 hp grit pumps used at the facility.\nTypically, one grit pump operates at a time. The lack of pump data made it very difficult to accurately\ncalculate pump efficiency of the return activated sludge (RAS) and trickling filter pumps.\nIt is recommended to continuously monitor pump efficiency to identify when maintenance is necessary.\nThe facility would be able to quickly know when pumps are not operating efficiently and could make\nnecessary changes to improve the efficiency. During the visit, analysts were not able to obtain useful\npump data for the influent pumps and grit pumps due to a lack of pressure gauges.\nEfficiency evaluation could be conducted if pressure ports with reliable gauges were located on the inlet\nand outlet of each pump. Operating pumps at optimal efficiency can reduce energy consumption\nsignificantly compared to operating pumps at a low efficiency. This recommendation was not developed\ninto a full recommendation because annual cost savings could not be accurately calculated.', 11921),
(91, 11924, 'Standard v-belts are inefficient due to friction losses when belts bend around a sheave. The inside of the\nbelt must be compressed in order to bend, which takes energy away from the power transmitted. Friction\nbetween the belts and sheave also reduces power transmission efficiency. Using high torque drives or\nnotched v-belts improves efficiency.\nReplacing the standard v-belt with a notched v-belt on the motor would reduce energy losses due to\nbending, friction, slip between the belt and the sheave, and stretching of the belt over time. The use of\nnotched v-belts on the four main process grit pump motors will reduce associated motor energy use by\n8,964 kWh with an associated cost saving of $519 annually. Switching to a notched v-belt will result in a\n2.1% decrease in motor annual energy consumption. There will not be an implementation cost to the\nfacility because it is based on incremental replacement as needed. Notched v-belts will also have a longer\nlife time compared to standard v-belts.\nThis measure did not appear as a full recommendation due to the low cost savings. See the Replace\nStandard V-belt to Notched V-belt OMC Analysis on the following pages of this report for details on this\nmeasure.', 11924),
(92, 11925, 'Sludge goes through three steps in the treatment process. The first step is thickening, the process of\nforming larger and more rapidly settling aggregates in the sludge. This is done by stirring the sludge in\neither the primary or secondary clarifiers. The next step is dewatering, where the water content in the\nsludge is reduced to improve the suitability for composting. The last step is digestion in which the\nmicroorganisms digest the remaining organic matter in the sludge and create biogas as a byproduct of\ntheir metabolism. [1]\nCurrently, the facility uses two 20 hp pumps to move sludge from the clarifiers to an anaerobic digester.\nSludge in the digester breaks down and releases both CO2 and methane gas. The facility uses some of the\nmethane gas as fuel for the boiler, which is located at the digester, and the rest of the gas is flared. The\npumps operate during the operation hours of the treatment plant, 8,760 hours per year. Every four years,\nthe pumps are rebuilt because of the wear resulting from transporting the sludge.\nAnalysts proposed dewatering the sludge enough to be handled as a solid. This would improve the\nsuitability for the composting process in the anaerobic digester. The proposal would involve either\ndewatering the sludge prior to being transported to the digester and using a conveyor to move the sludge\nto the digester or using another dewatering process after the sludge has been pumped to the digester.\nAltering the process of transporting the sludge will create an additional implementation cost. A complete\nrecommendation was not developed because of the inability to obtain exact cost benefits of dewatering\nthe sludge and speculation that the payback after implementing the project would be too long.\n[1] Fair, Gordon Maskew; Geyer, John Charles; Okun, Daniel Alexander (1968). Water and Wastewater\nEngineering 2. New York: John Wiley & Sons', 0),
(93, 11919, 'The facility operates an anaerobic digester to create biogas from thickened sludge pumped from the\nprimary clarifier. Biogas flows from the digester at a rate of 45 scfm, into a biogas fueled boiler. Excess\nbiogas is burned off (flared). Water is heated in the boiler to a target temperature of 180 °F and pumped to\nthe main office. There, a water-to-air, air handling unit provides the building’s heating needs. Facility\npersonnel informed analysts that the boiler operates at a low capacity, about 10 to 40 percent. The boiler\ncan operate on either biogas or natural gas when the digester cannot supply the boiler with biogas. Natural\ngas is typically only used about once per year if there is a problem with the digester. The boiler is tuned to\nuse biogas with maximum efficiency. Typically, this means the boiler is less efficient when heating with\nnatural gas, but analysts found that the boiler is operating efficiently with 3% excess oxygen.\nFacility personnel provided analysts with the yearly production and consumption of biogas and natural\ngas. The digester produces approximately 23,600,000 ft3 (14,632 MMBtu) of biogas per year.\nApproximately 6,100,000 ft3 (3782 MMBtu) is burned in the boiler to heat the main office building, and\nthe remaining 17,500,000 ft3 (10,580 MMBtu) is flared. There are several auxiliary buildings on site for\nstorage of equipment and chemicals that are heated by natural gas pack heaters. These buildings use\napproximately 769,500 ft3 (731 MMBtu) of natural gas. Other municipal buildings, located approximately\n330 yards from the digester, use approximately 1,020,000 ft3 (969 MMBtu) of natural gas in a natural gas\nfired boiler to meet their heating needs. Further details, including equipment specifications, are located in\nthe Analyst Site Report by Justin Conklin in the Site Data section.\nFacility personnel are interested in a combined heat and power (CHP) cogeneration system using biogas\nthat would allow them to sell power generated back to the utility company and use waste heat for their\nbuilding heating needs. Analysts worked with a company that specializes in cogeneration systems. The\nvendor informed analysts that the 45 scfm flowrate of biogas would require a generator with a 150 kW\noutput. There are currently no biogas generators with that small of capacity. If there were a generator\nsmall enough, the value of electricity generated would be approximately $72,000 annually.\nAnalysts considered the option of sending a portion of the flared biogas to the other municipal boiler\nnearby to burn biogas instead of purchasing natural gas to fuel the boiler. The boiler is a steam boiler\nwhich requires a scrubber and pre-filter system to clean the biogas before it is burned in the boiler. The\nboiler would also need a new burner that is able to burn biogas. Using biogas in a steam boiler increases\nthe risk of corrosion, requiring extra insurance and inspections to ensure safe operating conditions.\nAssociated annual energy savings would be 873 MMBtu, a cost savings of $10,616. The cost to install the\npipeline, scrubber, pre-filter, new burner, and the extra maintenance costs are estimated to be in excess of\n$110,000 for implementation, giving this project a simple payback greater than 10 years.\nIf the facility decides to replace the steam boiler in the other municipal building with a hot water boiler in\nthe future, there may be an opportunity for incremental implementation that would drastically reduce the\nimplementation cost. Analysts recommend the facility identify an alternate use of the 10,580 MMBtu of\nflared gas in the future if the technology becomes available.', 11919),
(94, 12533, 'NULL', 0),
(95, 12642, 'The facility uses a reheat furnace to heat steel billets to 2,250 ºF before rolling them in the rolling mill. The furnace uses oxy-fuel burners which use an air-fuel mixture for combustion. The combustion air is preheated to 800 ºF using a recuperator. Replacing air with pure oxygen as the source of oxidizing agent during combustion can increase the heating capacity of the furnace. With the oxy-fuel combustion there is substantial increase in available heat as compared to air-fuel combustion. The increase in available heat is directly related to reductions in energy consumption and increases in furnace throughput as when available heat is increased the amount of total energy input required to do a constant amount of work is reduced.\n\n\nA PHAST analysis where air was replaced with O2 for combustion in the reheat furnace, showed a 33% reduction in furnace fuel usage which will result in annual cost savings of $666,490. \n\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because the cost of pure oxygen is high and the cost savings cannot be realized without considering productivity benefits. The increase in throughput in the furnace with pure O2-fuel combustion cannot be supported by the rolling mill due to limited processing capability. \n\n', 2),
(96, 12644, 'One of the products produced by the facility is steel rebar. The first stage in the production of rebar is scrap steel melting, which takes place at the melt shop facility, operated for 6,000 hours per year. At the melt shop, the steel is melted using an arc furnace, tested with a ladle refining furnace, and cast into steel billets in a continuous caster. An intermediate vessel called a tundish is used to transfer the refined molten steel from the ladle furnace to the casting molds. The tundish is an open container with refractory linings which acts as a reservoir for the molten steel to ensure continuous feed to the caster during change of ladles. The tundish is equipped with a fuel fired heater-dryer system that reduces heat loss in the molten steel, prevents formation of bubbles in the first billet at the beginning of the casting sequence, and also prevent degradation of the refractory lining of the tundish due to thermal shocks. A typical tundish maintains the molten steel at a temperature of 2,300 ºF. The combustion in a typical tundish heater-dryer system results in flue gases at temperatures close to 1,600 ºF. Recovering the usable thermal energy from the flue gases, and using it to preheat the combustion air in the heater-dryer system will result in fuel savings.\n\n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation because information about the tundish heater-dryer system could not be obtained by analysts. \n', 12644),
(97, 12641, 'The facility produces steel rebar by melting scrap steel. The steel scrap is initially melted using an electric arc furnace (EAF) which runs for 6000 hours per year. The EAF processes 600,000 tons of steel per year. Steel slag is created during the melting of the steel scrap and is an unavoidable by-product of the EAF. The furnace produces 0.10-0.15 ton of steel slag per ton of steel produced. The slag is removed from the furnace at a temperature range of 3000-4300 F, and possesses a substantial amount of thermal energy which can be recovered. The facility does not currently recover heat from high temperature slag.\n\nSeveral physical methods, chemical methods, and direct electricity generation methods have been exploited for recovering thermal energy from high temperature slag. These methods can save up to 55% of the usable energy that is lost to the atmosphere from the hot slag. \n\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because of the challenges which restrict heat recovery from slag such as low thermal conductivity of slag, easy crystallization trend, discontinuous availability of heat from slag, and human safety constraints during chemical reactions. \n\n', 12641),
(98, 12640, 'The facility currently uses photocell sensors in order to control outside lighting. Analysts noticed the outside lights were on in the middle of the day during the site assessment. If the sensors are working correctly, the outdoor lighting should only be on when dark. There was a possibility that the sensors were covered or dirty at the time of the facility visit. The plant engineer expressed an interest in using astronomical timers in place of sensors to control the outdoor lighting. Astronomical timers differ from photocell sensors because they control lighting based on time of day rather than the light the photocell receives. Installing astronomical timers would replace the need to maintain photocell sensors.\n\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because analysts did not know how long the sensors have been faulty or what the issues with the sensors was due to.\n\nA general best practice would be to clean or repair the current sensors, or install astronomical timers that will control the outdoor lighting based on the time of day.\n', 12640),
(99, 12530, 'The facility utilizes compressed air to operate large and small equipment in the manufacturing process. Analysts noted that compressed air was still being supplied when the equipment was turned off. Analysts also noted that there were numerous small leaks that could be heard coming from the equipment. If the equipment had a shut off valve installed upstream, then air could be disconnected from the machine completely. This would reduce the leak load, and save energy associated with the air compressor. This was not pursued as a full recommendation since that analysts were unable to quantify the leak load on the equipment.', 12530),
(100, 12534, 'The facility uses hog fuel (a mix of wood scraps and sawdust) from the saw mill as fuel for the three Cleaver Brooks boilers. This hog fuel is made during the production process and is an unavoidable byproduct of the saw mill. The boilers run most efficiently when the moisture content of the hog fuel is not too high. The facility uses a heated tumbler barrel to help dry the hog fuel before it is burned in the boiler. \n\nDuring the rainy season, the facility is unable to sufficiently dry the hog fuel to efficiently run the boilers. This results in dry hog fuel being purchased from an outside source in order to meet the steam demands of the boilers. Analysts recommend constructing a large dry storage warehouse to cover the hog fuel piles. This would shield the hog fuel from rain and help reduce the moisture content of the fuel. This measure was not written as a full recommendation because analysts could not determine the annual cost of purchasing hog fuel from an outside source.\n', 12534),
(101, 12524, 'The facility uses a paint oven to expedite drying product after paint has been applied. Analysts noted that the paint oven and associated ducting are not insulated. Un-insulated or poorly insulated surfaces are a significant source of wasted energy. Temperature differentials between the surfaces and surroundings act as driving forces for the heat transfer between these bodies. Insulating these surfaces will decrease this rate of heat transfer, saving energy and improving system efficiency.\n\nAnalysts measured surface temperatures using a thermal imager which also provided an ambient temperature in the room of 99 °F. The paint booth duct surface temperature was 178 °F, the paint booth was 170 °F, and small paint booth duct was 340 °F. \n\nA full analysis was not done for this recommendation, since savings are likely minimal due to the paint room operations occurring once per week\n', 12524),
(102, 12525, 'Install an automatic stacker for the trimmer machine with a staging area.  This will reduce the associated labor of the trimmer by 30%. The trimmer at this facility is used to sand boards and prepare them to be packaged. This is operated by two people: the grader, who loads the machine and inspects the boards for quality, and the packager, who bands the stacks of wood and takes them to storage on a forklift. Currently, the trimmer operates 80 hours per week, for 50 weeks per year, for a total of 4,000 annual operating hours. After a stack is ready for banding, the forklift driver moves it to a banding table, bands it, and then moves it to storage. It takes about 90 seconds to band a stack and 90 seconds to move it to storage. When there is high demand for smaller stacks of boards, personnel can’t move fast enough, and without a staging area, the machine must be slowed down. Sixty percent of wood goes into small 25 piece stacks, while forty percent goes into stacks of 50 and above. When everything is operating smoothly the machine can process up to 5,000 boards over a 10 hour shift. But, when demand for smaller stacks is high, the speed must be reduced to 2,500 boards per shift. ', 12525),
(103, 12527, 'The facility has many belt-driven motors with standard V-belts that could use notched V-belts to increase power transmission efficiency, reducing energy consumption. Some motors identified for belt replacement are large conveyors, shaker, and dust collection fans/blowers.\n\nA notched V-belt reduces slip and allows the belt to bend around sheaves with less energy loss compared to standard V-belts. Reduction in output speed and efficiency occurs when a standard V-belt slips within the groove of the sheave. Efficiency improvements have been found to range from 1% to 3%. An average efficiency improvement of 2% over standard V-belts is used in this analysis. Friction between the standard V-belt and sheave generates heat within the belt, resulting in an energy loss and shortened belt life. Notched V-belts can last twice as long as standard V-belts. Notched V-belts may be used with existing V-belt pulleys but typically cost 20-30% more. The additional cost of the notched belts will typically be offset by a longer lifetime.\n\nAnalysts requested motor and transmission belt inventories from facility personnel, but did not receive these documents. Because of this, analysts could not accurately estimate annual cost savings and implementation costs, which prevented development of a full recommendation. A recent analysis for another facility estimated cost savings of $7,600 per year, accounting for motors with a total of 3,450 connected horsepower, operating 2,080 hours per year.\n', 12527),
(104, 12519, 'High pressure steam is used in multiple locations at the facility. It is used to soften wood chips and sawdust at the beginning of the process, and it is used in a steam press to remove moisture and form boards. Facility personnel explained that the steam press experiences difficulties that slow production.\n\nThe steam press is served by metal steam lines. As the press height changes, the metal lines must move with it. The movement is made possible by Barco valves, or elbow joints. Platon adapters connect steam lines to the press.\n\nThe Barco valves and Platon adapters are the source of most difficulties on the steam press. Personnel estimated that Barco valves break on average ten times per year, causing four hours of downtime with each occurrence. Platon adapters fail two or three times per year, causing ten hours of downtime for each occurrence. Each Barco valve and Platon adapter repair costs $830 and $100 for materials, respectively. Barco valve repair requires two maintenance personnel and three operators for 2.5 hours of labor; the latter are needed for fire safety. Repairing Platon adapters requires two maintenance personnel and three operators for nine hours. If these repairs are reduced or eliminated, maintenance personnel and operators can spend their time doing tasks that better utilize their skills. Wages for maintenance personnel and operators are assumed to be $48.75 per hour and $25 per hour, respectively. Production time is valued at $4,000 per hour.\n\nFacility personnel have considered using flexible steam hoses instead of Barco valves, but this is not recommended by the manufacturer for this application. Personnel estimated that repair of a steam hose would only require 45 minutes and one hour to resume production. It is assumed that steam hoses would fail as often as Barco valves since the manufacturer does not recommend this change. Personnel also estimate that making this switch would reduce the occurrence of Platon adapter failures.\n\nCost savings may be available by switching from Barco valves to flexible steam hoses. For Barco valve replacement, reducing downtime from 2.5 hours to 1.75 hours per occurrence will save $1,500 in labor and $30,000 in downtime, for a total of $31,500 saved per year. This assumes the same frequency of repairs for steam hoses as for Barco valves and equal material costs. Analysts believe that steam hoses may be less costly. Each Platon adapter repair costs $40,000 in production downtime and $2,000 in materials and labor, assuming that material costs are only $100 per repair. Thus, reducing Platon adapter failures by one occurrence per year will save $42,000.\n\nThere are potentially large cost savings associated with switching from Barco valves to steam hoses, approximately $73,500 per year. However, this estimate is based on several assumptions (discussed above). This opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation because the action is not recommended by the steam press manufacturer, and because analysts lacked information to estimate implementation costs.\n\nAn additional cost of failing Barco valves and Platon adapters is steam lost to leaks. Analysts are not able to estimate steam losses or the energy required to produce and transport lost steam. Although they could not be estimated, additional cost savings likely exist to reduce lost steam.\n', 12519),
(105, 11974, 'The facility uses a natural gas-fired oven to dry stainless steel parts and to cure paint. The oven operates at 450° F, nine hours per day for four days per week. The oven is approximately 52 x 19 x 10 feet, and has a firing rate of 2 MMBtu per hour. Facility personnel expressed concerns about oven heat loss of the oven due to hot spots on the walls. The average temperature of the hot spots is estimated to be 105 °F, taken from thermal imaging camera. There are approximately 1,035 square feet of hot spots. Analysts performed an energy balance of the furnace using PHAST modeling software and found that the maximum heat loss is 0.109 MMBtu/hr. This equates to $1731.96 a year. \n\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the cost of repairing hot spots is unknown. The facility personnel told the analysts that the oven installation contractor did not insulate the cracks properly. Facility personnel wanted an estimate of the heat loss caused by the poor insulation.\n', 11974),
(106, 11980, 'The facility has seven welding stations. Six of the stations have Miller Syncrowave 250 welders, while one station has a Miller Dynasty 350 welder. The Dynasty welder is considered to be more efficient than the Syncrowave welder because it is better technology. The Dynasty welder is more efficient with energy, and could produce better quality work depending on the welder operating it. The one welding station equipped with the Miller Dynasty 350 welder is currently being used less than many other stations with older welders.\n\nDuring the site assessment, facility personnel expressed interest in upgrading the six Syncrowave welders to Dynasty welders to increase efficiency of welding at the facility. The idea is based on an assumption that the Dynasty welders would be more capable of creating better welds with less energy consumption than the Syncrowave welders.\n\nWhile at the facility, analysts put two data loggers on two of the six Syncrowave welders to collect amperage consumption every 16 seconds for a week. The data collected showed that one of the welders was used for about 29 hours, while the other welder was only used for about 2 hours.  This data showed that the current welders are plenty capable of keeping up with the work load that the facility needs. These numbers are only based on two of the welders, but analysts assumed that the other four Syncrowave welders would run between 2 and 29 hours per week.\n\nAnalysts recommended using the Dynasty welder more than its current use. The Dynasty welder has more updated technology and may be able to perform better for specific jobs. This would require the supervisor to be willing to work on any of the seven welding stations, rather than constantly using the Dynasty for welding purposes.\n\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the cost of upgrading to the new welders is $49,896 and the savings from the change is not large enough to justify the purchase. The price of a brand new Dynasty welder is $8,316, and does not provide the energy savings per station to have a reasonable payback period.\n', 11980),
(107, 11977, 'The facility uses water for two main purposes during production. One big source of using water is the multi-step wash and rinse process before the products are painted. The other big consumer of water is when the facility needs to leak test particular products before leaving the welding station. The leak testing is not very common, according to the facility it is only done on 20-40 units per month compared to the hundreds that go through the facility in a month. Each unit holds approximately 19 gallons of water. Once the unit is checked for leaks, the water is poured down the drain. The paint line wash and rinse process uses much more water regularly. According to the facility, 1200 gallons of water is used in the wash and rise process per work week (Monday through Thursday). The water is used so quickly for this process because the water does need to be changed in order to keep the process of washing and rinsing effective.\n\nDuring the site assessment, facility personnel expressed interest in looking into ways to repurpose the water used around the facility. The leak test water could be reused because there are no contaminants being added. The wash and rinse water would be tougher to reuse because the chemical used for the wash process has hydrogen fluoride in it.\n\nThe presence of hydrogen fluoride makes the water from the wash and rinse process hard to repurpose. Hydrogen fluoride requires a method that includes adding large amounts of calcium to the water causing the calcium fluoride precipitate to settle so it can be removed from the water. This process is expensive to build, and the water savings would not give a reasonable payback period.\n\nThere are opportunities to reduce the water use within the facility. The easiest reuse opportunity would be to take the leak testing water and use it for the wash and rinse water. The wash and rinse process water is changed frequently enough that the leak testing water would be easy to work into the process. Another possible opportunity would be to use the rinse water as wash water. The rinse water would get the chemical in it over the time the items are rinsed, but all that would only require adding more chemicals to the rinse water to turn it into wash water. This is working under the assumption that there are no other contaminants in the rinse process water.\n\nThis measure was not developed into a full recommendation because the cost of installing a whole water reclamation process to remove the hydrogen fluoride would be very expensive. The system would require building a new process and purchasing chemicals. This would easily exceed $50,000. The savings at most would be $2,400 per year, and that would not cover the cost of the chemicals for removing the hydrogen fluoride from the waste water.\n', 11977);
INSERT INTO `OMCs` (`OMCId`, `TaskId`, `OMCText`, `SubjectId`) VALUES
(108, 11983, 'The facility uses two air compressors. There is the primary and the secondary compressor, but the specifications are similar for both. The compressors are 25 hp with a rated air pressure of 125 psig. The primary compressor has a VFD installed, while the secondary compressor is start-stop. When the air demand exceeds the amount that the primary compressor can handle, the secondary compressor is kicked on to provide the additional air needed.\n\nDataloggers were installed on both compressors to collect amperage measurements every 16 seconds for one week. An air leak assessment was completed based on the data from the dataloggers. The current percent of compressed air lost to leaks was calculated by observing average air flow over the datalogged period and picking a low point. This point was assumed to be purely made up of leaking compressed air.  The low point found for this system was 19 acfm, which is the equivalent of 25% of the system. This means that 25% of the compressed air use was being lost to leaks.  For this facility, a leak load of 30% would ordinarily be purposed. This system already operates below the purposed leak load.\n\nAir leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to find and fix them. Also people often assume that compressed air is a cheap resource. The process of making compressed air is very energy-intensive and adds up quickly for a facility. Many leaks are due to facility fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. Reducing air leaks is an iterative process. A compressed air system with few leaks can become leak-ridden if efforts are not continuously made to keep leaks to a minimum. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic, but energy savings are available by reducing air leaks by any amount. In addition to decreasing costs, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility’s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation due to the low current leak load.\n', 11983),
(109, 12732, 'The process at the facility requires the application of paint to its products to seal, protect and for aesthetical purposes of their parts. Paint is applied by one painter using spray guns to parts exiting the drying oven. These parts are hanging on a conveyor chain moving at 6 feet per minute, and after painting these are passed through the curing oven. \n\nThe facility provides 10 minutes breaks to its employees, but for longer breaks, such as lunch, the entire production line is stopped due to lack of personnel at the paint booth station. During the ramp-up process after breaks, painter increases the operation pressure at its station to speed up the painting process, increasing paint loses. Potential throughput increase at the facility are limited to an increase in availability at the paint station.\n\nIn the AR “Reduce Paint Overspray” in section 3 of this report, it was estimated that an efficiency improvement in the painting process could generate up to $20,000 per year in savings due to a reduction in paint loses. One of the alternatives suggested to achieve this improvement is to hire a second painter and alternate them during breaks to create a continuous productive process. Salaries for painters in Oregon ranges between $31,000 and $59,000 annually.  \n\nThis measure was not considered as a full recommendation given that it does not generate savings at the current rate of production.  \n', 12732),
(110, 12375, 'The facility uses multiple Dust Collection Systems (DCS) to remove particulate from the air to enhance the air quality of the work place. The DCS for the facility is a single fan powered by a 50 hp motor. Analysts took live electrical readings and flow measurements using pitot tubes and magnahelic gauges on site. \n\nDust Collection Systems can be arranged in either “material-handling” or “clean-side” configurations. Material-handling fans operate with particulates flowing through the fans and use positive pressure to push the material downstream. Clean-side fans operate downstream of the baghouse where no particulates flow. They use negative pressure to pull particles from the facility into a baghouse.\n\nThere are six types of centrifugal fan blades commonly used in dust collection systems. These blade types, efficiencies, and characteristics are listed below. Increasing the static efficiency of a system decreases the motor input power approximately by the ratio of the current static efficiency over the proposed static efficiency.\n\nAnalysts calculated a fan efficiency of 77.6% using the collected data. This was not developed into a full recommendation because the current efficiency is already at a respectable value. This is a best practice and there are no recommended actions for the DCS at this time.\n\n\n', 12375),
(111, 12383, 'Analysts noticed excess heat coming from the steam trap in the boiler room. It is recommended that the steam trap be replaced for savings and safety purposes. A steam trap stops steam from passing through until it condenses into water. A faulty steam trap does not allow this separation between condensate and steam, lowering the efficiency of the boiler system. Steam traps should be replaced about every six years. A temperature reading of approximately 216 °F was measured for the steam trap. The high temperature on the trap suggests there could be significant blow by at the trap.  \n\nIf the temperature of the pipe downstream from the trap is consistent with the trap, this suggests that steam has passed through the trap and there is a leak. This is the case according to a thermal image taken during the facility visit (see Anthony Mizener’s site report for thermal image), which means that replacing a steam trap can be a possible savings opportunity. This was not written as a full recommendation since not enough information was acquired.\n', 12383),
(112, 12377, 'The facility has three 50 hp rotary screw Quincy air compressors. Compressors 1 and 3 operate on load/unload. Compressor 2 is variable frequency drive (VFD) controlled. The system pressure is 115 psig.\n\nData loggers were placed on Compressor 1 & 2 to collect amperage measurements for one week at one minute intervals. The current percent of compressed air lost to leaks (leak load) was calculated as the minimum air flow, where the compressors are assumed to be serving only leaks. A current estimated leak load of 1 acfm (equating to 0.5%) was calculated, meaning that the amount of air being lost to leaks is 0.5% of the air used effectively. For this facility, a leak load of 20% would ordinarily be proposed. This system appears to be already operating at an ideal leak level.\n\nAir leaks are often neglected due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. Also, people often assume that compressed air is cheap. Many air leaks occur due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. Reducing air leaks is an iterative process. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic, but energy savings are available by reducing air leaks. In addition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\n\nThis opportunity was not developed into a full recommendation due to the low current leak load.\n', 12377),
(113, 12376, 'The facility uses a radio frequency (RF) oven to defrost product before sending it to be cut and prepped for packaging. The RF oven requires cooling water at less than 65? F to work properly. The water for the oven is cooled using a heat exchanger that rejects heat into the refrigeration loop. \n\nAnalysts collected data regarding the RF oven, such as temperature and flow measurements. The cooling water temperature was measured leaving the RF oven at 76? F and returning to the oven at 67? F. The flow of the cooling water was measured to be 24.3 gpm using an ultrasonic flow meter.\n\nAnalysts considered installing a heat exchanger or cooling tower dedicated for cooling the RF water. This was not developed into a full recommendation because the estimated savings of $3,000 from the heat rejection was not large enough to justify buying a cooling tower or heat exchanger.\n', 12376),
(114, 12699, 'The facility expressed concern with equipment being routinely left on in R&D Suite 5. Analysts collected an inventory of equipment in which operation time can be reduced. A total of 256W was accounted for. A list of equipment used in this analysis can be seen in Anthony Mizener’s site report in the Site Data section of this report. The time reduction varies from six hours per day with the fume hood light, to 23 hours per day for the stirrer plates. This results in an annual electrical savings of $418. There will be no payback period due to a lack of implementation costs.\n\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation due to its low savings potential. The equipment is not large enough to produce adequate savings even with large operation time reductions. This best practice will become more feasible as the company expands due to an increase of equipment and employees. It will be important for new employees to develop energy saving habits by turning off equipment when not in use. \n', 12699),
(115, 12698, 'The facility currently utilizes a server room to meet its data storage requirements. It is expected that an increased server capacity will be required as the company continues to grow. \n\nTemperature control in a server room is important to keep equipment functional. Heat is generated by information technology (IT) equipment, and must be removed. Overcooling a server room will also result in excess energy costs. An optimized temperature control system is therefore important to keep equipment functioning while not overspending on cooling costs. \n\nOne method to increase efficiency is to create a temperature differential containment strategy. This is done by establishing a hot aisle located where the cooling air exits the room, and a cold aisle located where the cooling air enters the room. This will maximize the temperature difference between the air entering the room and leaving the room, thus reducing the amount of required energy to cool the system.\nIndirect evaporative cooling is another option for air temperature control. Evaporative cooling uses the vaporization of water to cool air. The enthalpy of vaporization for water is large enough to provide an efficient heat sink for the air. Water is used to wet the surface of a heat exchanger, which will take thermal energy from the air stream to vaporize the water. ', 12698),
(116, 12700, 'The facility uses solvents, primarily acetone, in the manufacture of pharmaceutical products. Solvents are mixed with polymers and other active pharmaceutical ingredients (API) to ensure an even mix of compounds. The mix is then sent to spray dryers to separate the solvents from the mixture.\n\nThe facility currently disposes approximately 12,600 pounds of solvent waste per year at a cost of $2.00 per gallon, totaling approximately $3,800 in solvent waste management per year. \n\nAn alternative for waste management is to incorporate into the facility a combined heat and power (CHP) plant, which is an on-site electricity generation system that captures heat that would otherwise be wasted to provide useful thermal energy, and it also captures different type of wastes using them as fuel for an engine or turbine. \n\nThis recommendation was not developed into a full AR given that the payback of the project would exceed 20 years. This recommendation should be reevaluated if the facility considers incorporating a boiler to their equipment. Most conventional boilers use commercially available fuels such as natural gas, light oil and heavy oil. However, some boilers can be adapted to burn alternative fuels, or a combination of alternative and commercial fuels.\n', 12700),
(117, 12764, 'NULL', 0),
(118, 12405, 'The facility is implementing a new wastewater system that will reduce water use significantly. While the new wastewater system is a better process for reducing water consumption, it will affect biological oxygen demand (BOD) levels. The current excess water dilutes sewage from the facility reducing BOD levels. Most facilities are charged for excess BOD sent to the city wastewater plants, so a large increase in BOD levels could lead to an increase in sewage costs.\n\nIn the event that the new wastewater system raises BOD above allowable levels, analysts recommend installing tanks and aeration blowers. The aeration blowers would reduce BOD levels and make up for the reduction in sewage dilution. This measure was not developed into a full recommendation because it is unclear how the new wastewater system will effect BOD levels until it is in use.\n', 12405),
(119, 12400, 'There are two evaporator coils in Refrigeration Room 500. Each coil has a set of four ¾ hp fans, for a total of 3 hp per coil. The first coil is located near the forklift door and has severe frost build up. The second coil is located farther from the door and has no frost build up. Figure 1 below shows these two sets of coils. The refrigeration technician indicated that the fans always operate, even when the door is open. Turning off the fans when the door is open could help reduce this frost build up increasing evaporator performance.\n\nThe considered measure was not developed into a full recommendation because it is difficult to quantify potential cost savings. Furthermore, if the “Install High Speed Doors” recommendation is implemented, the cost savings from turning off the evaporator fans would be reduced and the frosting on the coils would be less of a problem.', 12400),
(120, 12404, 'Facility personnel expressed interest in determining how much energy is lost due to air leaks in the compressed air system. Data loggers were left with the facility to track live air compressor amperage over seven days. \n\n \n\n\nThe above figure represents a rolling average air flow over the seven days of logged data. The lowest sustained value of 35 acfm was used as the leak load (QC) for this analysis while a value of 327 acfm was used for the peak air demand (Qmax).\n\n\n \n\n\nEquation 1 was used to determine a leak load percentage of 11.9%. This was not developed into a full recommendation since the current leak load percentage is very low as compared to similar facilities.\n', 12404),
(121, 12759, 'NULL', 0),
(122, 12840, 'NULL', 0),
(125, 12721, 'The facility is separated into four departments. There is the Manufacturing, Manufacturing-3, Operations, and Corporate buildings. Each building is equipped with one air compressor. The table below summarizes the compressor size at each building. \n\nID	Location	HP\n		\nCentury	Manufacturing 3	5\nAtlas Copco	Operations	15\nAtlas Copco	Corporate	43\nAtlas Copco	Manufacturing 	79\n\nDataloggers were placed on the 15 and 43 hp compressors during the assessment. Air leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. People also often mistakenly assume that compressed air is cheap. Many leaks are due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. The term leak load refers to the minimum sustained air demand, which is assumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks throughout the facility to achieve a leak load equal to 30% of peak, non-leak plant demand can be a reasonable objective. Reducing leaks can also ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system which may extend the life of air compressors. The following tables summarize the current and proposed conditions of the 15 and 43 hp compressors. \n\nCurrent Conditions\nCompressor Name	Op. Hours	Avg. Air Flow (acfm)	Avg. Power (kW)	Energy (kWh/yr)	Energy Cost	Electrical Demand (kW-mo/yr)	Demand Cost	Total Cost\n								\n15 hp Compressor	2,656	31	7	17,359	$1,083	78	$292	$1,375\n43 hp Compressor	6,511	55	10	63,701	$3,975	117	$437	$4,412\nTotal	9,167	-	16.3	81,060	$5,058	195.8	$729	$5,787\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nProposed Conditions\nCompressor Name	Op. Hours	Avg. Air Flow (acfm)	Avg. Power (kW)	Energy (kWh/yr)	Energy Cost	Peak Power	Electrical Demand (kW-mo/yr)	Demand Cost	Total Cost\n									\n15 hp Compressor	2,656	28.7	6.0	16,012	$999	9.4	112.8	$420	$1,419\n43 hp Compressor	6,386	52.2	9.4	59,862	$3,735	21.4	257.2	$957	$4,692\nTotal	9,042	-	15.4	75,874	$4,735	30.8	370.0	$1,376	$6,111\n\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because the electrical savings would not be sufficient to overcome the increased cost of compressor electrical demand.\n', 12721),
(126, 12685, 'The facility currently uses two 2.45 MMBtu Laas natural draft boilers to heat water for the HVAC system. These boilers are set to reach a specific temperature set point across the facility. If individual rooms require a larger heating load, there are 0.275 MMBtu water heaters that are used as boosters to meet higher demands.\n\nFacility personnel indicated that approximately 30% of the annual natural gas use is dedicated to the HVAC system. Analysts performed a combustion analysis for one of the Laas HVAC boilers using a Bacharach Combustion Analyzer. The results of the combustion reading indicated high O2 levels at 15.9%, which is indicative of a low efficiency boiler. This is expected with a natural draft boiler since there is air flow control, allowing excess oxygen to enter the combustion process. This high amount of oxygen removes thermal energy from the exhaust stack, reducing the thermal efficiency of the boiler.\n\nAnalysts recommend a condensing boiler as a replacement for he currently used Laas boilers. A condensing boiler can recover the latent heat of vaporization from the water vapor that exits as exhaust gases. Please note that for a condensing boiler to be running at high efficiency (96-99%), there must be a large enough hot water demand or the exhaust gases will not condense and the heat will still be lost.\n\nAnalysts estimate that the increased efficiency of a condensing boiler would result in $4,000 annually in natural gas savings. This was not developed into a full recommendation because the high cost of implementation would result in too large of a payback to be financially attractive. \n', 12685),
(127, 12711, 'The facility currently utilizes a server room to meet its data storage requirements. It is expected that an increased server capacity will be required as the company continues to grow. \n\nTemperature control in a server room is important to keep equipment functional. Heat is generated by information technology (IT) equipment, and must be removed. Overcooling a server room will also result in excess energy costs. An optimized temperature control system is therefore important to keep equipment functioning while not overspending on cooling costs. \n\nOne method to increase efficiency is to create a temperature differential containment strategy. This is done by establishing a hot aisle located where the cooling air exits the room, and a cold aisle located where the cooling air enters the room. This will maximize the temperature difference between the air entering the room and leaving the room, thus reducing the amount of required energy to cool the system.\n\n \nFigure 1: Schematic of top view control scheme. Includes location of aisles as well as air conditioner locations.\n\nIndirect evaporative cooling is another option for air temperature control. Evaporative cooling uses the vaporization of water to cool air. The enthalpy of vaporization for water is large enough to provide an efficient heat sink for the air. Water is used to wet the surface of a heat exchanger, which will take thermal energy from the air stream to vaporize the water. \n', 12711),
