(138, 12928, 'NULL', 0),
(139, 12739, 'The facility currently has several compressed air nozzles located throughout the facility. They are\nused for various unregulated end uses during production.\nUsing engineered air nozzles will reduce the compressed air required. Compressed air is as much as\n90% inefficient due to compressor power dissipated as waste heat. Unregulated air discharges can be\nexpensive. Engineered air nozzles decrease air flow through blowdown wands while providing force\nto move objects. Another benefit is the ability to control the volume of air used with precision quick\nrelease grips.\nThe Coanda effect states that fluid flowing from a nozzle along a curved surface will follow the curve of\na smooth surface. Engineered air nozzles utilize the Coanda effect by forcing air out of small outlets in\nthe nozzle that then follows the curvature of the nozzle and directs the air to a single point. The air\nsurrounding the nozzle is entrained by flowing air ejecting from nozzle outlets, amplifying fluid flow.\nThe amplification of fluid flow by an engineered air nozzle reduces air consumption, while maintaining\noutput equivalent to an open end orifice.\nFigure 1. Air Nozzle Coanda Effect. [1]\nAnalysts were unable to obtain information needed to analyze this process, but utilizing general best\npractices will lead to energy savings by reducing total compressed air produced.\nReferences\n[1] Exair Corporation. www.exair.com\nAuthored by: Keith Striby\nReviewed by: Hari P. N. Nagarajan', 12739),
(140, 12740, 'Reduce Compressed Air Leaks\nThe facility has a total of four air compressors on site. These compressors range between 50 and 125\nhp. Dataloggers were placed on four of the compressors during the assessment.Compressed air leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix\nthem. Also, there is a common misconception that compressed air is cheap. Most air leaks are due to\nfaulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. Leak load is estimated as the\nminimum sustained air demand, which is assumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system.\nEliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution system is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks\nthroughout the facility to achieve a leak load equal to 10% of peak, non-leak plant demand can be a\nreasonable objective. In addition to decreasing costs resulting from lost compressed air, reducing\nleaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air distribution system and may extend the\nlife of air compressors.\nThis opportunity was not developed as a full recommendation because of its low savings potential.\nEvaluation of datalogger information of the compressors showed that a 10% reduction in leak load\nwould reduce energy consumption by 3,000 kWh annually, with an energy cost savings of $150 per\nyear. Leak reduction is still a worthy goal.\nAuthored by: Megan O’Leary\nReviewed by: Hari P. N. Nagarajan', 12740),
(141, 12859, 'Increase Granulator Efficiency\nThe facility has seven granulators in use to reprocess scrap from their manufacturing process. One\ngranulator services all SBM machines, while each EBM machine has a dedicated granulator. During\nproduction, these granulators remain in operation while their respective EBM or SBM machine are in\nuse. Live power readings of 7 kW loaded and 1.63 kW unloaded were recorded on a 20 hp granulator\nmotor. Facility personnel provided total operational hours for each EBM and SBM machine from\nJanuary 1, 2016 through June 21, 2016. Assuming that all EBM and SBM machines will continue this\noperational schedule for the remainder of the year, EBM machines will operate an average of 2,700\nhours, and SBM machines will operate an average of 4,200 hours.\nAnalysts were informed that the facility had previously implemented a Watt Wattcher and sensors to\nincrease granulator efficiency. An energy consulting company performed an analysis on the modified\ngranulator, and informed analysts that the granulator is off 66%, unloaded 30%, and loaded 4% of the\ntime.\nImplementing the facility’s current Watt Wattcher setup can result in an annual energy savings of\n27,258 kWh. The energy cost savings is estimated to be $1,100 per year for all machines, but the\nfacility reported that the implementation cost per Watt Wattcher setup is $1,100 for each machine.\nThis would result in a total implementation cost of $7,700 and a simple payback of seven years.\nA full analysis was not performed on this consideration because of the low savings and long payback.\nOperational requirements may increase in the future, which would provide additional savings and a\nreduced payback.\nAuthored by: Keith Striby\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 12859),
(142, 12838, 'Convert to Electric Forklifts\nThe facility uses propane forklifts to transport products throughout the facility. Analysts noticed the\nmajority of the forklifts operate indoors under facility cover. Facility personnel perform maintenance\non the facility’s forklifts.\nAnalysts considered incrementally upgrading to electric forklifts as propane forklifts become\ninoperable and require replacement. Electric forklifts use electric motors to increase energy efficiency\nand decrease maintenance costs compared to propane or diesel forklifts. This is cost effective because\npropane can cost up to three times more than electricity. Charging systems are a typical way to\nmeasure forklift electrical energy use. Monthly demand and total electrical energy consumption\ncomprise the energy costs for electric forklifts. Electric forklifts are beneficial for indoor use,\nespecially cold rooms, since there are no harmful emissions. They also have a life expectancy of 8\nyears compared to 6 years for propane forklifts.\nImplementation options include forklifts with the newest generation of fast-charging stations. These\nstations require little floor space and can be placed where forklifts can be easily accessed. Fastcharging\nstations deliver 400 to 600 amps to rapidly charge a battery from below 20% to 80%.Installing fast charging stations may increase electrical demand cost compared to slow charging\nstations.\nA full analysis on this consideration was not performed because the facility currently owns all of the\nforklifts and recently purchased the majority of them. To replace them would be a large expense.\nAdditionally, the propane cost for the facility is very low at $0.82 per gallon.\nAuthored by: Megan O’Leary\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 12838),
(143, 12839, 'Increase Pump Efficiency\nThe facility has three pumps to transport chilled water throughout the facility. Only two of the three\npumps operate regularly. The sizes of the two pumps are 5 and 25 hp.\nDuring the site assessment, a non-contact flow meter measurement showed these two pumps jointly\nmove water at 200 gpm. An elevation change of 3 feet and pipe diameter of 4.78 inches were\ncollected using a tape measure. The suction pressure was 14.7 psi and the discharge pressure was 28\npsi. Live electrical readings were measured, and the 5 hp pump was found to be operating at\napproximately 3.55 kW, while the 25 hp pump was operating at approximately 11.6 kW.\nPump efficiencies were calculated using the Pump System Analysis Tool. A pump efficiency of\n22.6% and 17.7% was calculated for the 5 hp and 25 hp pump, respectively.\nThis was not developed as a full recommendation due to the low energy savings. The annual cost\nsavings were estimated to be $300.\nAuthored by: Megan O’Leary\nReviewed by: David Chan-Vasquez', 12839),
(144, 12875, 'Reduce Mold Cooling Time\nThe facility uses a variety of resins for their stretch blow molding (SBM) process. Specific products\nthat use high density polyethylene (HDPE) require significant cooling time before releasing from the\nmold. Analysts considered chilling compressed air prior to blowing the hot resin, in order to reduce\nprocessing time. Chilling compressed air results in less time required to cool and solidify the part.\nThis would result in a reduction in processing time, and more throughput.\nVortex tubes are one way to chill compressed air. They are usually manufactured out of food-grade\nstainless steel or aluminum. Vortex tubes are interchangeable, stationary parts that regulate the\nvolume of compressed air. This mechanical device separates a compressed gas into hot and cold\nstreams. Compressed air is tangentially injected into the tube causing it to swirl in the chamber. Due\nto the conical nozzle at one end of the tube, only the outer, warmer air of the vortex is allowed to\nescape. The cooler air is forced in return and released at the opposite end of the vortex tube.\nFigure 4.3 – Air flow and temperatures in a vortex tube. [1]\nTemperatures and air flow can be adjusted using a control valve or knob that is attached to the vortex\ntube. Vortex tube air consumption at 100 psi ranges from 8 cfm to 35 cfm depending on the model\nand size. Utilizing this technology leads to a pressure drop and may potentially exceed the dew point\nof the compressed air. More detailed information can be found in reference [1].\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because implementation would result in a higher\nenergy use from the compressors as well as potential condensation, which may alter product quality.\nReferences:\n[1] \"What Is a Vortex Tube?\" How Does a Vortex Tube Work? N.p., n.d. Web. 11 Aug. 2016.\n<http://www.airtx.com/how-does-a-vortex-tube-work.htm>.\nAuthored by: Monica Heng\nReviewed by: Evan Talan', 12875),
(145, 13070, 'Currently the facility takes 48 hours to fully cool and crash a batch of IPA beer. It takes approximately 24 hours to cool the beer from room temperature to 32°F, and another 24 hours for the beer to crash. Crashing a batch of beer increases its clarity and reduces work required by filters in the next stage of the brewing process. According to facility personnel, filtering is currently the bottleneck of the beer finishing process. This crash time is important so that filtering can occur as quickly as possible.\nThe total brewing time for a batch of beer is between 14 and 21 days. If the facility could reduce crash time from 48 hours to 24 hours, they could increase annual production by approximately five to seven percent.\nInstalling a separate tank to pump the beer to when it is ready for cooling and crashing could allow the facility to begin brewing a new batch before the last one is crashed. Personnel informed analysts that there is a lot of time associated with cleaning and preparing a tank to hold beer. This would be greater than savings associated with reduced crash time. Analysts also considered installing a centrifuge to replace the crash and filter steps in the brewing process. Facility personnel expressed interest in a centrifuge but noted that it would not result in an increase in production, so much as reduction in lost beer during the filtering process.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation due to analysts’ inability to determine an effective method of reducing crash time.\n', 0),
(146, 13079, 'NULL', 0),
(147, 13078, 'NULL', 0),
(148, 13132, 'NULL', 0),
(149, 12766, 'Fan cycling is one of the easiest capacity control methods for evaporators and condenser fans, particularly\nin large systems where evaporative condensers may have several fan motors. Evaporative condenser fans\nat the facility operate on variable frequency drives (VFD); however, analysts noticed that the control\noperation strategy of fans was on-off, cycling its operation depending on the load on the refrigeration\nsystem. For fan systems, energy requirements are reduced with continuous partial speed operating instead\nof on/off operation. For instance, it takes ¼ the energy to run a fan continuously at half speed instead of\nhalf of the time at full speed.\nIn general, the operation of VFDs achieves a lower energy consumption by matching the motor load with\nthe system requirements instead of running at its peak. VFDs offer precise capacity control in a more\nefficient and long lasting way to reduce fan speed compared to fan cycling. In addition, VFDs are also\nable to provide soft starts and smooth accelerations to the motors, reducing wear and hence, prolonging its\nlife.\nA continuous but smoother operation of motors allows the system to reduce its inrush current, which is an\ninstantaneous input current drawn generated when electrical devices are first turned on. The magnitude of\nthis inrush current can be several times more than the normal full load current. Optimizing VFD controls\nto prevent this phenomenon can provide some energy savings, along with reduction in the temperature of\nthe motor’s winding, prolonging its operation life.\nThis measure was not fully developed into a recommendation due to a lack of information for a full\nanalysis.', 12766),
(150, 12763, 'The facility has a total of four air compressors on site in their compressor room. The total horsepower of\nthe compressors is 350 hp.\nAir leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular attention required to fix them. It is also\nassumed that compressed air is cheap. Most air leaks are due to faulty fittings, lines, valves, hoses, and\npneumatic rams or cylinders. The term leak load refers to the minimum sustained air demand, which is\nassumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system. Eliminating all leaks in a complex air distribution\nsystem is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks throughout the facility to achieve a leak load equal to 10%\nof peak, non-leak plant demand can be a reasonable objective. In addition to decreasing costs resulting\nfrom lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the facility\'s air\ndistribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\nThis opportunity was not turned into a full recommendation because data loggers were not able to be\nplaced on the compressors to evaluate air leaks. The facility runs 24/7 which means it does not have a\ntime when leaks could be found using data loggers.', 12763),
(151, 12762, 'The facility makes their own plastic jugs for packaging using molds. The molds contain excess plastic\nthat requires the jugs to be trimmed for the final product. The facility collects the trimmings in bins so\nthey can reuse any plastic that does not hit the floor. The plastic trimmings that touch the ground are sent\nto be recycled elsewhere because they are no longer sterile, otherwise they could be melted down again\nand made into new jugs.\nAnalysts recommend expanding the size of the bins so that they will be able to catch more of the jug\ntrimmings. This recommendation was not developed as a full recommendation because the value of the\ndiscarded plastic was unable to be determined.', 12762),
(152, 12756, 'The facility relies heavily on refrigeration to store product. There are different temperature zones, some\nthat require much lower temperatures. The work spent to maintain the lower suction temperatures and\npressures is lost when serving higher temperature and pressure zones, typically by the use of back\npressure regulators. The areas of differing temperatures are located near each other.\nA segregated system could offer improved efficiency but a common way to segregate the system would\nbe to lay a whole new set of pipes parallel to the old set throughout the facility to segregate the varying\ntemperature zones. A vendor told analysts that the process of setting new pipes would take at least two\nweeks to complete, and during this time the whole process would need to be shut off due to its reliance on\nrefrigeration. More information on the refrigeration system can be found in Pavel Grechanuk’s site report\nin the Site Data section.\nThis recommendation was not developed into a full analysis because the implementation of a segregated\nsystem would be a disruption to production in the facility.', 12756),
(153, 12755, 'The facility has a deaeration (DA) tank that prepares boiler feedwater. The tank operate 8,760 hours per\nyear and surface temperatures were recorded at 150 °F using a contact thermometer. The dimensions for\nthe DA tank are 4 feet in diameter and 11 feet long. The tank has a horizontal cylindrical geometry and is\nnot insulated. By reducing DA tank heat loss, less energy will be required to convert the feed water into\nsteam, reducing associated boiler fuel consumption. Thermal images of the DA tank can be found in\nPavel Grechanuk’s site report in the Site Data section.\nThis was not pursued as a full recommendation because the calculated yearly savings were $314 with an\nimplementation cost of $1,193, leading to a payback of 3.8 years.', 12755),
(154, 12983, 'The facility has a 100 hp compressor dedicated to their incisor. The compressed air is used to run\ndifferent parts of the machine and blow off accumulated dust. According to facility personnel, the incisor\ndoes not need to use compressed air to blow off dust. While the facility already keeps the air compressor\nturned off when not needed, when it is on, air used to blow off dust could be supplied more cost\neffectively with a blower.\nCompressed air typically takes five times or more electrical energy than a blower to produce the same\nvolume of air. Air blowers covert electrical energy into air flow with an efficiency on the order of 70-\n80%, with about 20-30% of the energy lost to heat. Air compressors lose over 80% of their input\nelectrical energy as heat.\nThis opportunity was not developed as a full analysis because the incisor was not running while analysts\nwere on site and analysts were unable to obtain information required for an analysis.', 12983),
(155, 12981, 'Replace standard V-belts with notched belts as they wear out. Notched V-belts have been shown to\nreduce motor energy consumption by increasing efficiency. Efficiency loss can occur in belts from\nbending, friction, and slippage between the belt and sheave. According to several government and\nindustry sources, notched V-belts can improve efficiency by approximately 2% to 4% when compared to\nstandard V-belts.\nPayback will be immediate since there will be no implementation cost. Although notched V-belts\ntypically cost 20% more than standard V-belts, the increased life expectancy will offset any initial cost\ninvestments. Friction between the standard V-belt and sheave generates heat within the belt, resulting in a\nshortened belt life.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable to obtain a\ncomprehensive facility motor list to fully assess energy savings.', 12981),
(156, 12902, 'The facility has a total of two air compressors on site. These compressors are 100 and 125 hp.\nDataloggers were placed onto one of the compressors during the assessment because only one of the\ncompressors run consistently.\nCompressed air leaks often go ignored due to the investment and regular maintenance required. There is a\ncommon misconception that compressed air is cheap. Most air leaks occur due to faulty fittings, lines,\nvalves, hoses, and pneumatic rams or cylinders. The term leak load refers to the minimum sustained air\ndemand, which is assumed to be feeding only air leaks in the system. Eliminating all leaks in a complex\nair distribution system is unrealistic. However, repairing leaks throughout the facility to achieve a leak\nload equal to 10% of peak, non-leak plant demand can be a reasonable objective. In addition to decreasing\ncosts resulting from lost compressed air, reducing leaks can ease bottlenecks and deficiencies in the\nfacility\'s air distribution system and may extend the life of air compressors.\nThis opportunity was not developed as a full recommendation because the savings came out to be roughly\n$300 per year. Evaluation of the datatlogged information of the compressors showed that the leak load is\nalready controlled.', 12902),
(157, 12982, 'The facility has storm and groundwater reclamation on site. Currently, the pumps are controlled using\nhigh/low controls. The pumps operate based on the water level in the basins.\nThe high/low controls modulate the pumps off and on based on the water levels. If a Variable Frequency\nDrive (VFD) were installed to control the pumps, the pumps would be able to stay on with a more\nconsistently lower energy use. This would reduce wear on the pumps associated with turning off and on,\nand would also reduce energy consumption. Pumping energy in a given system increases and reduces\ncubically with pump speed (e.g. at half speed a pump will only require 1/8 of the power at full speed.)\nThis opportunity was not fully considered because analysts were unable to obtain data on the pumps to\nfully assess the energy savings.', 12982),
(158, 12980, 'The facility has many large pumps to transport liquids throughout the facility. The efficiency of pumps\ncan be compromised if they are sized incorrectly or if the pumps are worn or have experienced a\ncavitation event. Since the facility’s processes involve heavy use of pumps, evaluating and maintaining\npump efficiency can significantly reduce associated energy consumption.\nContinuously monitoring pump efficiency can help identify when maintenance is necessary. The facility\nwould be able to identify pumps that are not operating efficiently and could make the necessary changes\nto improve efficiency. Efficiency evaluations are based primarily on flow and the pressure differential\ndeveloped by a pump in comparison to its power draw. Continuous or periodic efficiency evaluations\ncould be based on pressure ports and gauges at the inlet and outlet of pumps, flow, and pump power.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation due to the quantity of pumps and limited time.\nAnalysts were not able to collect substantial data to evaluate the facility’s pump efficiency.', 12980),
(159, 12907, 'The facility has 5 retorts for lumber treatment purposes. Treatment starts by decreasing retort pressure to\ncreate a vacuum, which removes native liquids from within the lumber. The pressure is then increased\nwhile introducing treatment oil, which transports the treatment chemical into the lumber. These oils\ninclude creosote, bunker oil, ammoniacal copper zinc arsenate (ACZA), and alkaline copper quaternary\n(ACQ). Time associated with pumping treatment chemicals can be reduced with a local rueping tank.\nFacility personnel mentioned that retort #81 would be most beneficial to alter. The oil is kept in a holding\ntank approximately 60 to 150 meters away and is pumped to the retort using a 60 hp motor. The retort\ncycles 213 times a year. Each cycle takes approximately 1.5 hours to pump. Valuable production time lost\nduring the oil transfer is valued at $110 per hour according to facility personnel.\nInstalling a rueping tank on retort #81 would drastically reduce the required pumping time per cycle. This\nwould result in pump energy and production time savings. Analysts assumed that pumping time could be\nreduced to 0.25 hours per pump with a rueping tank. Electrical savings were calculated to be $925, and\nproduction downtime savings were calculated to be $29,300, giving a total savings of $30,225.\nThis was not developed as a recommendation as analysts were unable to find an exact implementation\ncost for the rueping tank. Facility personnel had doubts that this would be a viable option when notified of\npotential savings due to the high cost of a rueping tank.', 12907),
(160, 12949, 'The facility collects and evaporates its processed water to separate distilled water from higher boiling\ncontaminants, eliminating the need to discharge wastewater into the sewer. The facility also treats the\nrainfall that hits their tank farms as contaminated water, due to environmental regulations. This rainfall\nwater is treated the same way as process water.\nAn evaporator turns a liquid mixture into its gaseous form and removes water or other liquids from\nmixtures. By removing moisture through vaporization, industry can greatly reduce the volume of waste\nthat must be treated. An evaporator can be used to obtain distilled water. There are two main energy\nsources for commercial evaporators: natural gas and electricity. Electric power has a 100% conversion\nrate from electricity to heat. Natural gas has an approximated 80% conversion rate, however natural gas\ncost is relatively less than electricity.\nThe facility uses a 6,100 gallon capacity evaporator to treat rainfall water and processed water. Steam\nfrom the boilers is used to heat and evaporate the water. Facility personnel stated that the current\nevaporator efficiency is 73%. This evaporator is currently consuming 11,808 MMBtu per year, costing\nthe facility $136,114 per year to operate. Upgrading to an evaporator with an 80% efficiency, could\nreduce energy consumption by 8.8%, and saving $11,913 per year. The cost of a new evaporator is\nestimated to be $180,212, with a simple payback of 15.1 years.\nDue to the high implementation cost and long payback period, this opportunity was not developed into a\nfull recommendation.', 12949),
(161, 12984, 'The facility currently uses a boiler to heat water for a variety of uses including, operating the evaporator.\nAnalysts observed that the condensate from system processes was not being recovered. This condensate\ncould be used to replace make up water to the boiler.\nReturning hot water condensate from steam operations to the boiler is a fairly common practice in\nindustry. Less make up water is required as more condensate is returned. The returned condensate is\nusually at elevated temperatures which reduces the heat load on the boiler. Additionally, the hot water\nreturned to the boiler is not lost, which would otherwise incur the facility waste water disposal charges.\nCondensate return saves on make-up water cost, reduces natural gas use in a gas fired boiler, and reduces\nthe charges associated with disposal of waste water.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation due to low potential cost savings on the order of a\nabout $300.', 12984),
(162, 12978, 'The facility has a 34,000 sq. ft. collection box surrounding their tank farm. The tank farm contains\nchemicals used in their process. Rainwater falling into this area has to be treated as process water due to\nenvironmental regulations. The facility collects this water by pumping it into the process water tank. The\nprocess water is evaporated in a 6,100 gallon capacity evaporator.\nThe current evaporator is fueled by natural gas, which has an approximated 80% conversion rate from\nnatural gas to heat. Analysts considered constructing a roof to redirect rainwater falling on the tank farms\nto an exterior location. This would significantly reduce the need to evaporate rainfall. The natural gas\nsavings were estimated to be 11,076 MMBtu, resulting in an annual savings of $52,610 and an\nimplementation cost of $475,000. The simple payback would be 9.0 years.\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation due to the long payback period.', 12978),
(163, 13097, 'The facility uses a total of three odor management (scrubber) systems. Four ½ hp fans operate\nconstantly to serve asphalt tanks. One system in particular uses two fans to service the filtration\nprocess. Sulfur dioxide emits off of the asphalt in the tanks, and if uncontrolled, can cause an\nunpleasant odor in the atmosphere. To control this, air flow from the asphalt tanks first enters into a\ntank filled partially with liquid sodium hydroxide to neutralize the pH of the sulfur dioxide. This\nsodium hydroxide tank has two pumps for fluid circulation. Treated sulfur dioxide then enters into a\nliquid hypochlorite tank to disinfect and further neutralize the gas. The exhaust then travels into a\ncarbon filter before it is released into the atmosphere. Facility personnel expressed interest in\nreplacing the fan scrubber system with activated carbon filter tanks alone for odor control.\nActivated carbon canisters are used for absorption of organic compounds from contaminated water\nand air streams. A variety of high activity activated carbons are available to suit multiple applications.\nCarbon filters require little maintenance and last for long periods of time. Analysts considered using\ncarbon filters to replace the double-chemical tank process.\nAnalysts estimated an approximately $5,500 implementation cost and $733 in annual savings. This\nwas not developed into a full recommendation due to the long payback period of 7.5 years. This\nmeasure may be justified if it is more effective in reducing gaseous odor than the current scrubber\nsystem. Analysts were unable to confirm this.\nAuthored by: Monica Heng\nReviewed by: Vinson Guov', 13097);
INSERT INTO `OMCs` (`OMCId`, `TaskId`, `OMCText`, `SubjectId`) VALUES
(165, 13104, 'The facility has a variety of heated processes and actively insulated the majority of heated surfaces.\nAnalysts noticed two braided hoses with approximately a 2” diameter and 1.5’ long. The hoses were\nmeasured to be 325 °F using a Fluke Thermal Imager.\nUninsulated or poorly insulated surfaces can be a significant source of lost energy. Temperature\ndifferentials between the surfaces and surroundings act as a driving force for the heat transfer between\nthese bodies. The rate of this heat transfer is directly proportional to the magnitude of the temperature\ndifferential. Insulating these surfaces will decrease this rate of heat transfer, saving energy and\nimproving system efficiency.\nAnalysts considered insulating the braided hoses. This was not developed into a full recommendation\ndue to the low total savings, and facility personnel’s explanation that this is only a temporary situation\nas a result of a recent repair.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Vinson Guov', 13104),
(167, 13105, 'The facility is planning to add a building to house offices and storage of chemicals on site. The\nbuilding will need to be heated to a temperature above 70 °F to keep the chemicals stable. The\nbuilding will be heated year round but the office will normally only be occupied during the\nproduction season (April-October). Following are some recommendations to consider when designing\nthe new building.\nAnalysts suggest consideration of a condensing water heater for hydronic heating of the facility.\nCondensing water heaters are over 90% efficient and often a normal recommendation over other units\n[1]. A gas fired heater might be the best option as the cost for natural gas compared to electricity in\nthe region is significantly lower. As a region the facility experiences average temperatures of 37 °F\nand average lows of 11 °F. Analysts suggest improved insulation for the exterior walls and ceilings.\nSeek higher R-values (ability to resist heat moving through it) when selecting insulation [2]. Consider\ninstalling energy efficient windows that help retain the heat [3]. Lastly, consider installing LED\n45\n4 – O ther Measures Considered\nlights. LEDs cost more but last longer reducing maintenance costs and draw less power using less\nenergy over time [4].\nThese items were not developed into full recommendations as the new building is not yet well enough\ndefined. This is just a reminder to consider energy efficient options when designing the new facility.\nReferences:\n[1] \"Save Money and More with ENERGY STAR Qualified Gas Condensing Water Heaters.\" Gas\nCondensing Water Heater Savings and Benefits. N.p., n.d. Web. 01 Nov. 2016.\n<https://www.energystar.gov/index.cfm?c=gas_cond.pr_savings_benefits>.\n[2] \"Recommended Home Insulation R– Values.\" ENERGY STAR. N.p., n.d. Web. 01 Nov. 2016.\n<https://www.energystar.gov/index.cfm?c=home_sealing.hm_improvement_insulation_table>.\n[3] \"Energy-Efficient Windows.\" Department of Energy. N.p., n.d. Web. 01 Nov. 2016.\n<http://energy.gov/energysaver/energy-efficient-windows>.\n[4] \"Learn About LED Bulbs.\" ENERGY STAR. N.p., n.d. Web. 01 Nov. 2016.\n<https://www.energystar.gov/products/lighting_fans/light_bulbs/learn_about_led_bulbs#why_choose\n>.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Vinson Guov', 13105),
(168, 13103, 'Implement Hot Oil Replacement Program\nThe facility has two hot oil heaters that are used in the heating of asphalt tanks. The heating process is\na closed loop system that heats tanks through heat exchangers. The facility has had the hot oil heatersfor a long time and has not replaced the oil in the hot oil heaters since installation. Closed loop hot oil\nsystems have a long lifetime in order of years but the hot oil can degrade through thermal damages\nover time and cause “coke” to build up in the system. Duratherm states that, once it has developed\n“coke will start to foul the heat transfer surfaces very quickly and the system could soon cease to\noperate” [1]. The thermal damage may not be as significant but can reduce the heat transfer properties\nand the effectiveness of the system.\nAnalysts recommend that the facility contact a hot oil vendor and develop an oil replacement\nprogram. This was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts could not quantify the\nsavings.\nReferences:\n[1] Industry, By. \"Proper Fluid Maintenance and Change-Out Procedures.\"Proper Fluid Maintenance\nand Change-Out Procedures. N.p., n.d. Web. 01 Nov. 2016.\n<https://durathermfluids.com/news/story/proper-fluid-maintenance-and-change-out-procedures/>.\nAuthored by: Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Vinson Guov', 13103),
(170, 13102, 'Improve Water-Oil Separation Process\nThe facility uses used cooking oil in the finished asphalt product. The oil helps increase cold weather\nstability and reduces cracking from thermal stresses due to extreme weather conditions. In order to\nready used cooking oil for production the facility needs to remove water that is mixed in with the oil.\nThe facility separates water and oil using a demulsifying agent and draining the water until they reach\nthe rag-layer (partially water and oil). Finally, the tank is heated to 180 °F for one day to evaporate\nremaining water from the tank.\nAnalysts considered reducing the evaporation time in this process by increasing the temperature the\nmixture is heated to. This would reduce the processing time by approximately half a day. The facility\nwould have to perform tests to determine the optimal temperature. This process should not be\nimplemented without significant testing and caution. Reaching a temperature above the optimal\ntemperature could allow the water in the oil to boil producing steam that in the worst case could result\nin an explosion. The optimal temperature would boil the water but not the oil. However, an optimal\ntemperature can be found safely. This process has been improved and documented in other places.\nOne article states that heated temperatures range from 160 °F – 210 °F [1].\nThis was not developed into a full recommendation because analysts were unable to calculate\nassociated savings.\nReferences:\n[1] Success Story No. 41 New Oil Demulsifier, ECO 22-4, Many Applications (n.d.): n. pag. Web.\n<http://emulsionsonline.com/Success 41.pdf>.\nAuthored by: Travis Hemingway and Tyler Giddings\nReviewed by: Ben Bellman', 13102),
